L["Scarlet Ledger Korgal"] = "Meven Korgal carried word of Deathknell's dead. No one will carry word of his."
L["Scarlet Ledger Perrine"] = "Captain Perrine drilled his men within sight of Brill. The drills have stopped."
L["Scarlet Ledger Vachon"] = "Captain Vachon's camp still stands. Nothing in it does."
L["Scarlet Ledger Melrache"] = "Captain Melrache promised his men the Light would shield them. It watched instead."
L["Scarlet Ledger Balnazzar"] = "The Grand Crusader was a vestment. You have seen what wore it. The sermons echo anyway."
L["Scarlet Ledger Radley"] = "Huntsman Radley tracked everything that moved in these fields. He never heard you."
L["Scarlet Ledger Durgen"] = "Cavalier Durgen rode out under Hearthglen's banner. His horse returned without him."
L["Scarlet Ledger Abbendis"] = "High General Abbendis commanded Tyr's Hand from behind high walls. Walls end."
L["Scarlet Ledger BenedictusVoss"] = "High Priest Benedictus Voss gave his daughter to the creed. That debt is not yours to collect."
L["Scarlet Ledger Thalnos"] = "Bloodmage Thalnos kept his order's oldest secrets. The grave kept none of his."
L["Scarlet Ledger Vishas"] = "Interrogator Vishas asks no more questions. You still hear Vorrel's voice."
L["Scarlet Ledger Loksey"] = "Houndmaster Loksey died among his hounds. They grieved him better than the Crusade will."
L["Scarlet Ledger Doan"] = "Arcanist Doan guarded books no one was allowed to read. The library is quieter now."
L["Scarlet Ledger Herod"] = "Herod, the Scarlet Champion. The recruits cheered his name. No one cheers now."
L["Scarlet Ledger Mograine"] = "Renault Mograine fell before his own altar. Whatever he was atoning for, it was not enough."
L["Scarlet Ledger Fairbanks"] = "High Inquisitor Fairbanks was walled up behind false stone. The Crusade buries its truths twice."
L["Scarlet Ledger Whitemane"] = "She called on the Light, and the Light answered her. You have stopped asking why."
L["Scarlet Ledger Courier"] = "The Crimson Courier's letters arrive nowhere now."
L["Scarlet Ledger Demetria"] = "The Scarlet Oracle foresaw many things. Not the road. Not you."
L["Scarlet Section 100"] = "A Private War"
L["Scarlet Section 250"] = "The Rhythm"
L["Scarlet Section 500"] = "The Mirror"
L["Scarlet Section 1000"] = "The Priest's Daughter"
L["Scarlet Tab Body 250"] = "The war has a rhythm now. You know the sound a zealot's prayer makes when it stops halfway. You know which roads they patrol and which farmhouses they have already emptied. Somewhere in Tirisfal, the old woman's lantern has gone out. You passed the sill where it used to burn, and you did not stop walking.\n\nYou tell yourself the arithmetic still means something. You no longer check the sums."
L["Scarlet Tab Body 500"] = "You have stopped remembering faces. Red tabards, silver flame, a prayer cut short: the details arrive already filed. What unsettles you is not the number. It is how clean the killing has become: no anger anymore, no grief. Just the ledger, asking to be balanced.\n\nWhitemane's zealots believe certainty is the same as righteousness. On the worst nights, you fall asleep before you can name the difference."
L["Scarlet Tab Body 1000"] = "The Crusade does not only recruit. It raises. In the camps you empty and the letters you do not keep, one name has begun to surface: a priest's daughter, brought up behind the Monastery's walls to burn the dead without flinching. Lilian. Her father, High Priest Benedictus Voss, gave his child to the creed the way other men tithe coin. They say she can kill a man with her hands folded, as if in prayer. They say she has never once asked why.\n\nOne day the Crusade will look at her and see what it sees in everyone it cannot use: infection. Whatever happens on that day will not be your doing, but you will understand it better than anyone alive.\n\nRemember the name Voss. The ledger is not finished with it. Neither are you."
L["Scarlet Tab Body"] = "You did not set out to be their enemy.\n\nIt began quietly. A burned farmhouse in Tirisfal that you happened past. An old woman crying over a lantern she had lit for her son. He wore a red tabard, she said, and stopped answering her letters. The Crusade had taught him a new prayer, and there was no room in it for her name.\n\nThen it was a boy hiding in the reeds outside Southshore, chapel ash in his hair. Then a merchant on the road, bleeding from a righteous sword. Then a farmer at his own gate. You told yourself it was chance. But each time a red banner came into view, your hand moved before your mind did. Each Scarlet you struck down felt less like war and more like arithmetic: a debt being counted against a lie dressed in silver light.\n\nYou have not written this in any letter. No commander has assigned it. There is no order, no charter, no oath. Just a tally, kept in silence, growing.\n\nYou are a Scarlet Crusader, in the older sense of the word. Not one of theirs. One against them. And every red tabard you cut down is another name added to a ledger no one else can read."

