L["Deathknell is a graveyard with a town attached, and the Scarlet Crusade had camped right next door. Executor Arren had me thin them out first and wanted their armbands as proof. Executors like a tally.\n\nThe real prize was their messenger, Meven Korgal, sitting in the Crusader camp with papers meant for somebody important. They reached somebody important, all right. Ours.\n\nArren read the documents and didn't care for a word of them. Next thing I'm carrying them up the road to Executor Zygand in Brill at a brisk walk. When an executor wants something delivered, you don't stop to admire the pumpkins."] = "Deathknell is a graveyard with a town attached, and the Scarlet Crusade had camped right next door. Executor Arren had me thin them out first and wanted their armbands as proof. Executors like a tally.\n\nThe real prize was their messenger, Meven Korgal, sitting in the Crusader camp with papers meant for somebody important. They reached somebody important, all right. Ours.\n\nArren read the documents and didn't care for a word of them. Next thing I'm carrying them up the road to Executor Zygand in Brill at a brisk walk. When an executor wants something delivered, you don't stop to admire the pumpkins."
L["Brill's war on the Crusade started with pumpkins. I stole ten off a farm up by Deathknell and hauled them to Apothecary Johaan, who did something to one of them that I didn't watch too closely. A laced pumpkin, for the Scarlet Zealot they kept in the tavern cellar. I carried it down myself. The zealot got a pumpkin. That's as far as I'm taking that story.\n\nExecutor Zygand preferred the direct approach, and kept sending me out to the Scarlet camps west of town until there weren't many captains left in them.\n\nOne of the deathguards wanted their rings as proof. My word, apparently, wasn't enough. Ten rings later, it was."] = "Brill's war on the Crusade started with pumpkins. I stole ten off a farm up by Deathknell and hauled them to Apothecary Johaan, who did something to one of them that I didn't watch too closely. A laced pumpkin, for the Scarlet Zealot they kept in the tavern cellar. I carried it down myself. The zealot got a pumpkin. That's as far as I'm taking that story.\n\nExecutor Zygand preferred the direct approach, and kept sending me out to the Scarlet camps west of town until there weren't many captains left in them.\n\nOne of the deathguards wanted their rings as proof. My word, apparently, wasn't enough. Ten rings later, it was."
L["Brother Crowley in the Stormwind Cathedral wanted me to go and see a Brother Anton. In Desolace. You don't just wander over to Desolace, but I went, and at Nijel's Point Anton had a job ready and waiting.\n\nThirty undead ravagers. Not twenty, not a handful. Thirty. I counted, because I knew Anton would.\n\nThat earned me a letter of commendation to Raleigh the Devout in Southshore. That's how it goes with the devout. You do them one favor, they write you a reference, and the reference gets you a bigger favor. Raleigh read it and started talking about a monastery up in Tirisfal Glades, and how badly it needed a visit."] = "Brother Crowley in the Stormwind Cathedral wanted me to go and see a Brother Anton. In Desolace. You don't just wander over to Desolace, but I went, and at Nijel's Point Anton had a job ready and waiting.\n\nThirty undead ravagers. Not twenty, not a handful. Thirty. I counted, because I knew Anton would.\n\nThat earned me a letter of commendation to Raleigh the Devout in Southshore. That's how it goes with the devout. You do them one favor, they write you a reference, and the reference gets you a bigger favor. Raleigh read it and started talking about a monastery up in Tirisfal Glades, and how badly it needed a visit."
L["Librarian Mae Paledust works in the Hall of Explorers in Ironforge and wanted one book in particular, the Mythology of the Titans. The copy Mae had in mind was sitting in the library of the Scarlet Monastery, where, as far as I could tell, nobody was reading it.\n\nSo a few of us went in to borrow a book. That's how I put it to myself, anyway. The Library wing is long halls, tall shelves and a great many serious people in red who don't care for visitors. Borrowing took some doing.\n\nWe came out with the book. Mae took it the way most people take a newborn, very carefully and without looking at anyone else in the room. I've had warmer thanks. I've rarely seen anybody happier, though, and I've watched people find gold."] = "Librarian Mae Paledust works in the Hall of Explorers in Ironforge and wanted one book in particular, the Mythology of the Titans. The copy Mae had in mind was sitting in the library of the Scarlet Monastery, where, as far as I could tell, nobody was reading it.\n\nSo a few of us went in to borrow a book. That's how I put it to myself, anyway. The Library wing is long halls, tall shelves and a great many serious people in red who don't care for visitors. Borrowing took some doing.\n\nWe came out with the book. Mae took it the way most people take a newborn, very carefully and without looking at anyone else in the room. I've had warmer thanks. I've rarely seen anybody happier, though, and I've watched people find gold."
L["There's a prisoner in the Monastery graveyard, Vorrel Sengutz, and Interrogator Vishas had been working on Vorrel for a long while. Vorrel didn't have much left to ask for. Two things: Vishas dead, and Vorrel's wedding ring, which Vishas had kept, taken to Monika Sengutz in Tarren Mill. Same last name. You can work out the rest.\n\nVishas didn't enjoy being interrupted. I took the ring back and walked it all the way to Tarren Mill. Light thing, a ring. Long walk, though.\n\nThe Library had its own customers. Half the scholars in the Horde seem to want a book off those shelves, and the Crusade guards its reading like it's gold. Can't think why. Most of it's about how to kill the rest of us."] = "There's a prisoner in the Monastery graveyard, Vorrel Sengutz, and Interrogator Vishas had been working on Vorrel for a long while. Vorrel didn't have much left to ask for. Two things: Vishas dead, and Vorrel's wedding ring, which Vishas had kept, taken to Monika Sengutz in Tarren Mill. Same last name. You can work out the rest.\n\nVishas didn't enjoy being interrupted. I took the ring back and walked it all the way to Tarren Mill. Light thing, a ring. Long walk, though.\n\nThe Library had its own customers. Half the scholars in the Horde seem to want a book off those shelves, and the Crusade guards its reading like it's gold. Can't think why. Most of it's about how to kill the rest of us."
L["Raleigh the Devout wanted the Scarlet Monastery's leaders dead, in the name of the Light. Funny thing to ask, since they'd tell you they did everything in the name of the Light too. I didn't argue theology. I packed.\n\nThe Armory belonged to Herod, a big man with a bigger axe, who spun like a top and announced he'd been waiting for a real challenge. Happy to oblige. In the Cathedral we put down the Scarlet Commander, and High Inquisitor Whitemane swept in and raised him right back up, which hardly seems fair. So we did it again, both of them this time, and nobody got up.\n\nRaleigh thanked me in the name of the Light as well. The Light, I'm told, was very grateful."] = "Raleigh the Devout wanted the Scarlet Monastery's leaders dead, in the name of the Light. Funny thing to ask, since they'd tell you they did everything in the name of the Light too. I didn't argue theology. I packed.\n\nThe Armory belonged to Herod, a big man with a bigger axe, who spun like a top and announced he'd been waiting for a real challenge. Happy to oblige. In the Cathedral we put down the Scarlet Commander, and High Inquisitor Whitemane swept in and raised him right back up, which hardly seems fair. So we did it again, both of them this time, and nobody got up.\n\nRaleigh thanked me in the name of the Light as well. The Light, I'm told, was very grateful."
L["When Varimathras asks you for something, you say yes, and you say it quickly. What the dreadlord wanted was the Scarlet Monastery's leadership dead, all four of them, kennel to altar.\n\nThe one I remember is the Cathedral. We put down the Scarlet Commander, and High Inquisitor Whitemane came in behind him and stood him right back up, which I thought was poor sportsmanship. So we knocked them both down together, and this time nobody got up. Herod, back in the Armory, had told us he'd been waiting for a real challenge. I hope we lived up to it.\n\nBack in the Royal Quarter I made my report. Varimathras looked pleased, which is the most worrying thing a dreadlord can do."] = "When Varimathras asks you for something, you say yes, and you say it quickly. What the dreadlord wanted was the Scarlet Monastery's leadership dead, all four of them, kennel to altar.\n\nThe one I remember is the Cathedral. We put down the Scarlet Commander, and High Inquisitor Whitemane came in behind him and stood him right back up, which I thought was poor sportsmanship. So we knocked them both down together, and this time nobody got up. Herod, back in the Armory, had told us he'd been waiting for a real challenge. I hope we lived up to it.\n\nBack in the Royal Quarter I made my report. Varimathras looked pleased, which is the most worrying thing a dreadlord can do."
L["Duke Nicholas Zverenhoff of the Argent Dawn wanted the Scarlet Archive in Stratholme burned and its archivist dead. Fine. Archives burn nicely.\n\nThe part worth telling came after. The Scarlet Bastion answers to the Grand Crusader, holiest man in the city. We hit him hard enough and he came apart into Balnazzar, a dreadlord, who'd been wearing the Grand Crusader the whole time like a borrowed tabard. Explains a few things about the Crusade, that. I brought the duke the head.\n\nThe duke would take Baron Rivendare's head too, from the dead side of town, if anyone was offering. Some folk collect spoons."] = "Duke Nicholas Zverenhoff of the Argent Dawn wanted the Scarlet Archive in Stratholme burned and its archivist dead. Fine. Archives burn nicely.\n\nThe part worth telling came after. The Scarlet Bastion answers to the Grand Crusader, holiest man in the city. We hit him hard enough and he came apart into Balnazzar, a dreadlord, who'd been wearing the Grand Crusader the whole time like a borrowed tabard. Explains a few things about the Crusade, that. I brought the duke the head.\n\nThe duke would take Baron Rivendare's head too, from the dead side of town, if anyone was offering. Some folk collect spoons."
L["Kirsta Deepshadow keeps to the north end of the Western Plaguelands and has a professional interest in the Scarlet Crusade. Unfinished business, Kirsta calls it, which is the kind of phrase that means somebody owes somebody blood. The first few jobs were a sampler of their outer lines, two of everything, like ordering off a menu.\n\nThe one worth telling is Hearthglen. Kirsta wanted the town itself measured, so I walked into a fortress full of Crusaders and went looking for somewhere high to stand and have a good long look. You'd think somebody would wonder what a stranger was doing up there. Nobody did.\n\nI came back with a count of everything they had. It wasn't a garrison. It was an army."] = "Kirsta Deepshadow keeps to the north end of the Western Plaguelands and has a professional interest in the Scarlet Crusade. Unfinished business, Kirsta calls it, which is the kind of phrase that means somebody owes somebody blood. The first few jobs were a sampler of their outer lines, two of everything, like ordering off a menu.\n\nThe one worth telling is Hearthglen. Kirsta wanted the town itself measured, so I walked into a fortress full of Crusaders and went looking for somewhere high to stand and have a good long look. You'd think somebody would wonder what a stranger was doing up there. Nobody did.\n\nI came back with a count of everything they had. It wasn't a garrison. It was an army."
L["Nathanos Blightcaller doesn't do small talk. I'd taken the Grand Crusader's orders off a dead Scarlet courier, and Nathanos had a better idea than reading them.\n\nHe handed me a rotten apple. One bite and I looked every inch a Scarlet Crusader, and off I went to Tyr's Hand to deliver those orders to the Crusader Lord in person. A whole town of zealots, and me strolling through it with the taste of bad fruit in my mouth. Nobody looked twice. The Crusader Lord took the orders and gave me plenty to carry back.\n\nWhat I brought Nathanos put the Scarlet Oracle, Demetria, on the road to Stratholme with an escort. She never got there."] = "Nathanos Blightcaller doesn't do small talk. I'd taken the Grand Crusader's orders off a dead Scarlet courier, and Nathanos had a better idea than reading them.\n\nHe handed me a rotten apple. One bite and I looked every inch a Scarlet Crusader, and off I went to Tyr's Hand to deliver those orders to the Crusader Lord in person. A whole town of zealots, and me strolling through it with the taste of bad fruit in my mouth. Nobody looked twice. The Crusader Lord took the orders and gave me plenty to carry back.\n\nWhat I brought Nathanos put the Scarlet Oracle, Demetria, on the road to Stratholme with an escort. She never got there."
L["Tirion Fordring lives alone in a cottage in the Eastern Plaguelands, and once he starts talking, you sit down. He lost his title and his home for standing up for the wrong orc, and his son Taelan grew up to be a Highlord of the Scarlet Crusade. Tirion wanted him back.\n\nSo I went collecting memories, the best of them a family painting hidden in Stratholme behind a picture of the twin moons. A hag at Uther's Tomb gave me a Scarlet face, and I walked into Hearthglen and handed Taelan his father's gift.\n\nIt worked. Taelan remembered, and we fought our way out together. On the road, Grand Inquisitor Isillien caught us. Taelan died there. Tirion arrived a moment too late, and Isillien didn't last long after."] = "Tirion Fordring lives alone in a cottage in the Eastern Plaguelands, and once he starts talking, you sit down. He lost his title and his home for standing up for the wrong orc, and his son Taelan grew up to be a Highlord of the Scarlet Crusade. Tirion wanted him back.\n\nSo I went collecting memories, the best of them a family painting hidden in Stratholme behind a picture of the twin moons. A hag at Uther's Tomb gave me a Scarlet face, and I walked into Hearthglen and handed Taelan his father's gift.\n\nIt worked. Taelan remembered, and we fought our way out together. On the road, Grand Inquisitor Isillien caught us. Taelan died there. Tirion arrived a moment too late, and Isillien didn't last long after."
