L["Where in K'aresh is Alleria Windrunner?"] = "Where in K'aresh is Alleria Windrunner?"
L["Your trainer sends you to Moonglade for lessons that cannot be taught in a city. The Cenarion Circle has work for young druids who are ready to listen to spirits, heal sick beasts, and follow the call of distant waters."] = "Your trainer sends you to Moonglade for lessons that cannot be taught in a city. The Cenarion Circle has work for young druids who are ready to listen to spirits, heal sick beasts, and follow the call of distant waters."
L["Speak with Duthorian Rall and take up the Tome of Valor. A paladin's lessons are not only prayers; they ask for aid given, judgment shown, and materials gathered with care.\n\nFollow the order's tests from early duty to the rites reserved for those who have carried the Light long enough to be trusted with more."] = "Speak with Duthorian Rall and take up the Tome of Valor. A paladin's lessons are not only prayers; they ask for aid given, judgment shown, and materials gathered with care.\n\nFollow the order's tests from early duty to the rites reserved for those who have carried the Light long enough to be trusted with more."
L["Master Mathias Shaw and Shenthul do not teach by lecture. They send rogues into guarded places, locked boxes, and situations where an antidote matters as much as a blade.\n\nBegin with your faction's poison work, then follow the hints that lead beyond city trainers into a wider trade of signals and reputation."] = "Master Mathias Shaw and Shenthul do not teach by lecture. They send rogues into guarded places, locked boxes, and situations where an antidote matters as much as a blade.\n\nBegin with your faction's poison work, then follow the hints that lead beyond city trainers into a wider trade of signals and reputation."
L["Your first totem begins with a call, not a command. The elements answer through trials, offerings, and long walks between trainers, spirits, and hidden shrines.\n\nFollow earth, fire, water, and air as each power is earned in turn. Later, the same lessons point toward a deeper test of balance."] = "Your first totem begins with a call, not a command. The elements answer through trials, offerings, and long walks between trainers, spirits, and hidden shrines.\n\nFollow earth, fire, water, and air as each power is earned in turn. Later, the same lessons point toward a deeper test of balance."
L["Warlock trainers do not offer power freely. They give names, errands, and circles, then ask whether you can bind what answers.\n\nBegin with the first summons and follow the darker lessons through demons, tomes, mounts, and bargains that always ask for more than coin."] = "Warlock trainers do not offer power freely. They give names, errands, and circles, then ask whether you can bind what answers.\n\nBegin with the first summons and follow the darker lessons through demons, tomes, mounts, and bargains that always ask for more than coin."
L["Your warrior trainer has a practical lesson first: learn to stand, endure, and fight with discipline. After that, older tests wait beyond the starting grounds.\n\nSeek the instructors who send warriors to brawls, islands, charms, and a windwatcher's summons. The reward is a weapon, but the road is the real trial."] = "Your warrior trainer has a practical lesson first: learn to stand, endure, and fight with discipline. After that, older tests wait beyond the starting grounds.\n\nSeek the instructors who send warriors to brawls, islands, charms, and a windwatcher's summons. The reward is a weapon, but the road is the real trial."
L["Apothecary Johaan has work in Brill, and it is not the sort of work the living would approve of. The Forsaken need samples, records, and willing hands if they are to learn what can still kill the dead.\n\nBegin with the Royal Apothecary Society's errands in Tirisfal and follow the plague work into Silverpine and Hillsbrad. Each vial answers one question and raises another."] = "Apothecary Johaan has work in Brill, and it is not the sort of work the living would approve of. The Forsaken need samples, records, and willing hands if they are to learn what can still kill the dead.\n\nBegin with the Royal Apothecary Society's errands in Tirisfal and follow the plague work into Silverpine and Hillsbrad. Each vial answers one question and raises another."
L["North of Brill, the Agamand Mills still carry the family name. Coleman Farthing wants proof of what happened there, Deathguard Dillinger wants the dead cleared out, and Magistrate Sevren has heard enough about the family crypt to be concerned.\n\nSearch the old estate, bring back what the Forsaken ask for, and see why Tirisfal's dead rarely stay where they are put."] = "North of Brill, the Agamand Mills still carry the family name. Coleman Farthing wants proof of what happened there, Deathguard Dillinger wants the dead cleared out, and Magistrate Sevren has heard enough about the family crypt to be concerned.\n\nSearch the old estate, bring back what the Forsaken ask for, and see why Tirisfal's dead rarely stay where they are put."
L["Auberdine's watchers have seen trouble near the Tower of Althalaxx. Speak with Balthule Shadowstrike and learn what the Cult of the Dark Strand is doing on the northern coast.\n\nWhat begins as a report from Darkshore pulls you toward Ashenvale, where parchments, gems, and old corruption point to a threat still gathering strength."] = "Auberdine's watchers have seen trouble near the Tower of Althalaxx. Speak with Balthule Shadowstrike and learn what the Cult of the Dark Strand is doing on the northern coast.\n\nWhat begins as a report from Darkshore pulls you toward Ashenvale, where parchments, gems, and old corruption point to a threat still gathering strength."
L["The Sepulcher needs help holding Silverpine Forest. Worgen stalk the roads, Dalaran mages keep their own counsel at Ambermill, and rumors from Shadowfang Keep have not grown quieter.\n\nTake the Forsaken orders one by one and learn why the forest still refuses to be claimed."] = "The Sepulcher needs help holding Silverpine Forest. Worgen stalk the roads, Dalaran mages keep their own counsel at Ambermill, and rumors from Shadowfang Keep have not grown quieter.\n\nTake the Forsaken orders one by one and learn why the forest still refuses to be claimed."
L["High Executor Darthalia has work for anyone willing to serve Tarren Mill. The humans of Hillsbrad still farm, trade, and guard the roads as if the Forsaken claim means nothing.\n\nReport for orders and carry the campaign from the fields to the mines and beyond. Darthalia will tell you who must fall next."] = "High Executor Darthalia has work for anyone willing to serve Tarren Mill. The humans of Hillsbrad still farm, trade, and guard the roads as if the Forsaken claim means nothing.\n\nReport for orders and carry the campaign from the fields to the mines and beyond. Darthalia will tell you who must fall next."
L["Marlene Redpath asks you to find her niece Pamela in Darrowshire. In the ruins, a lonely child is still waiting for family and trying to remember where her doll has gone.\n\nBegin with Pamela's small request and follow the Redpath family through old grief, missing records, and the question of what really happened on Darrowshire's last night."] = "Marlene Redpath asks you to find her niece Pamela in Darrowshire. In the ruins, a lonely child is still waiting for family and trying to remember where her doll has gone.\n\nBegin with Pamela's small request and follow the Redpath family through old grief, missing records, and the question of what really happened on Darrowshire's last night."
L["Deputy Willem has a simple problem near Northshire: thieves in red masks have stolen from the abbey's vineyards. In Elwynn and Westfall, the same masks keep turning up in places they should not.\n\nHelp the local marshals and the People's Militia follow the Defias trail. What begins as bandit work soon asks who is organizing it, and why."] = "Deputy Willem has a simple problem near Northshire: thieves in red masks have stolen from the abbey's vineyards. In Elwynn and Westfall, the same masks keep turning up in places they should not.\n\nHelp the local marshals and the People's Militia follow the Defias trail. What begins as bandit work soon asks who is organizing it, and why."
L["Darkshire keeps its lanterns lit because the woods do not. Commander Althea Ebonlocke needs help with the Night Watch, and every road out of town seems to carry another rumor, corpse, or warning.\n\nTake up the town's work and follow Duskwood's troubles as the locals give them to you: graves, worgen, missing people, and names spoken carefully after dark."] = "Darkshire keeps its lanterns lit because the woods do not. Commander Althea Ebonlocke needs help with the Night Watch, and every road out of town seems to carry another rumor, corpse, or warning.\n\nTake up the town's work and follow Duskwood's troubles as the locals give them to you: graves, worgen, missing people, and names spoken carefully after dark."
L["At the edge of the Blasted Lands, a fallen hero still waits for orders that never came. His regiment is gone, and the demons beyond Nethergarde have had years to make use of what was left behind.\n\nListen to the dead man's account and follow the scattered trail of soldiers, relics, and old command. The road is long because the failure was not simple."] = "At the edge of the Blasted Lands, a fallen hero still waits for orders that never came. His regiment is gone, and the demons beyond Nethergarde have had years to make use of what was left behind.\n\nListen to the dead man's account and follow the scattered trail of soldiers, relics, and old command. The road is long because the failure was not simple."
L["Marshal Dughan has posted a bounty at Westbrook Garrison. A Riverpaw brute named Hogger is prowling the woods, and Stormwind wants proof that the roads are safer.\n\nTake the warrant, follow the ridge southwest, and see why the locals speak his name with more concern than a single gnoll should earn."] = "Marshal Dughan has posted a bounty at Westbrook Garrison. A Riverpaw brute named Hogger is prowling the woods, and Stormwind wants proof that the roads are safer.\n\nTake the warrant, follow the ridge southwest, and see why the locals speak his name with more concern than a single gnoll should earn."
L["Under the broken Thandol Span, a dwarf's remains lie where few travelers think to look. Among them is a letter that never reached the woman waiting in Ironforge.\n\nCarry Sully Balloo's words home and let Sara Balloo decide what should be done with them. Some duties begin after the battle is already over."] = "Under the broken Thandol Span, a dwarf's remains lie where few travelers think to look. Among them is a letter that never reached the woman waiting in Ironforge.\n\nCarry Sully Balloo's words home and let Sara Balloo decide what should be done with them. Some duties begin after the battle is already over."
L["At the Crossroads, Mankrik is looking for his wife. He last saw her on the Gold Road, and the Barrens offers little comfort to anyone searching through quillboar territory.\n\nTake his question with you into the heat and dust. What you find, if you find anything, belongs first to the husband still waiting."] = "At the Crossroads, Mankrik is looking for his wife. He last saw her on the Gold Road, and the Barrens offers little comfort to anyone searching through quillboar territory.\n\nTake his question with you into the heat and dust. What you find, if you find anything, belongs first to the husband still waiting."
L["Feathermoon Stronghold expected a courier from Thalanaar. He never arrived, and the coast of Feralas has a way of swallowing small boats and easy answers.\n\nSearch the wreckage, carry back what proof you can, and follow the signs inland if the sea is not the whole story."] = "Feathermoon Stronghold expected a courier from Thalanaar. He never arrived, and the coast of Feralas has a way of swallowing small boats and easy answers.\n\nSearch the wreckage, carry back what proof you can, and follow the signs inland if the sea is not the whole story."
L["Thomas has been asked to find an adventurer for Bishop DeLavey in Stormwind Keep. The bishop will not speak of the matter openly, only that discretion matters and the kingdom may have need of you.\n\nBegin in Stormwind and follow a quiet trail of favors, contacts, and rumors. What starts as a missing man soon points beyond a simple disappearance."] = "Thomas has been asked to find an adventurer for Bishop DeLavey in Stormwind Keep. The bishop will not speak of the matter openly, only that discretion matters and the kingdom may have need of you.\n\nBegin in Stormwind and follow a quiet trail of favors, contacts, and rumors. What starts as a missing man soon points beyond a simple disappearance."
L["In the Burning Steppes, Helendis Riverhorn has orders for the Alliance against black dragonkin gathering near Morgan's Vigil. In Kargath, Warlord Goretooth has a command for the Horde that points into Blackrock Spire.\n\nTake the first report and follow your faction's chain of summons, proofs, and dangerous names. The amulet at the end of the road is not given to anyone who has only heard rumors of dragons."] = "In the Burning Steppes, Helendis Riverhorn has orders for the Alliance against black dragonkin gathering near Morgan's Vigil. In Kargath, Warlord Goretooth has a command for the Horde that points into Blackrock Spire.\n\nTake the first report and follow your faction's chain of summons, proofs, and dangerous names. The amulet at the end of the road is not given to anyone who has only heard rumors of dragons."
L["In Blackrock Depths, rumors about Princess Moira Bronzebeard have become too serious for Ironforge to ignore. The Dark Irons say one thing, the kingdom fears another, and no one outside the mountain has the full truth.\n\nEnter the depths on your faction's orders and find out what became of the princess. Rescue may not be as simple as it sounds."] = "In Blackrock Depths, rumors about Princess Moira Bronzebeard have become too serious for Ironforge to ignore. The Dark Irons say one thing, the kingdom fears another, and no one outside the mountain has the full truth.\n\nEnter the depths on your faction's orders and find out what became of the princess. Rescue may not be as simple as it sounds."
L["Raene Wolfrunner sends you west from Astranaar to look for Teronis, who went searching for answers to the furbolg attacks and did not return.\n\nFollow his trail through Ashenvale's moonwells, ruins, and old magic. If the forest is being changed, Raene wants to know how, and who is helping it spread."] = "Raene Wolfrunner sends you west from Astranaar to look for Teronis, who went searching for answers to the furbolg attacks and did not return.\n\nFollow his trail through Ashenvale's moonwells, ruins, and old magic. If the forest is being changed, Raene wants to know how, and who is helping it spread."
L["At Grom'gol, Nimboya asks after Yenniku, youngest son of the Darkspear chief. The Bloodscalp trolls may know where he was taken, but Stranglethorn rarely gives up a captive cleanly.\n\nPress the jungle tribes for answers, listen when older spirits are called, and keep following the trail even when it turns toward magic no troll should take lightly."] = "At Grom'gol, Nimboya asks after Yenniku, youngest son of the Darkspear chief. The Bloodscalp trolls may know where he was taken, but Stranglethorn rarely gives up a captive cleanly.\n\nPress the jungle tribes for answers, listen when older spirits are called, and keep following the trail even when it turns toward magic no troll should take lightly."
L["The Scarlet Crusade claims to defend Lordaeron from the undead, but its soldiers do not always wait to ask who is innocent. In Tirisfal, Southshore, and the Plaguelands, people have reasons to send you against the red tabard.\n\nFollow the Crusade's trail through farms, prisons, monasteries, and northern keeps. Each order you uncover makes their righteousness harder to trust."] = "The Scarlet Crusade claims to defend Lordaeron from the undead, but its soldiers do not always wait to ask who is innocent. In Tirisfal, Southshore, and the Plaguelands, people have reasons to send you against the red tabard.\n\nFollow the Crusade's trail through farms, prisons, monasteries, and northern keeps. Each order you uncover makes their righteousness harder to trust."
L["A battered raft in Un'Goro holds a few odd belongings: a compass, a map, a key, and a photograph. No one stands nearby to explain them. Only the crater, the wreckage, and the sense that someone lost more than supplies.\n\nFollow the clues from Marshal's Refuge and help piece together what happened to their owner. The trail is strange, old-fashioned, and not eager to explain itself."] = "A battered raft in Un'Goro holds a few odd belongings: a compass, a map, a key, and a photograph. No one stands nearby to explain them. Only the crater, the wreckage, and the sense that someone lost more than supplies.\n\nFollow the clues from Marshal's Refuge and help piece together what happened to their owner. The trail is strange, old-fashioned, and not eager to explain itself."
L["A small scroll waits aboard a Bloodsail ship south of Booty Bay. It bears no proper patron, only a riddle and enough direction to make you wonder what was hidden.\n\nTake Cortello's words seriously and follow the clues wherever they send you. The treasure is not marked for anyone who wants a straight road."] = "A small scroll waits aboard a Bloodsail ship south of Booty Bay. It bears no proper patron, only a riddle and enough direction to make you wonder what was hidden.\n\nTake Cortello's words seriously and follow the clues wherever they send you. The treasure is not marked for anyone who wants a straight road."
L["Darkshire keeps its lamps lit all night, every night, and after a while there you stop thinking that's odd. Commander Althea Ebonlocke runs the Night Watch, and the Night Watch was short on everything except work.\n\nThe work was Raven Hill Cemetery, where the dead had stopped staying put. Skeletons of every kind: warriors, mages, fiends, horrors, and the names don't get any friendlier. The worst of it was the eastern mausoleum. Twenty plague spreaders, in the dark, in a crypt, and every one of them living up to the name.\n\nI came out filthy, and the commander nodded at me like I'd done a decent day's work. In Darkshire that's practically a parade."] = "Darkshire keeps its lamps lit all night, every night, and after a while there you stop thinking that's odd. Commander Althea Ebonlocke runs the Night Watch, and the Night Watch was short on everything except work.\n\nThe work was Raven Hill Cemetery, where the dead had stopped staying put. Skeletons of every kind: warriors, mages, fiends, horrors, and the names don't get any friendlier. The worst of it was the eastern mausoleum. Twenty plague spreaders, in the dark, in a crypt, and every one of them living up to the name.\n\nI came out filthy, and the commander nodded at me like I'd done a decent day's work. In Darkshire that's practically a parade."
L["Sven Yorgen's family was murdered, and what Sven wanted was a name. Somebody had buried a book behind an old stump on his farm, and I spent a good while showing it around Darkshire. The clues, once I had them all: not from around here, nervous, jittery, gone west. I found him out past town. Folks call him Jitters. Some people just make it easy.\n\nOnce Jitters had finished that journal, we had our name. Morbent Fel, a necromancer out at Raven Hill. Getting at Morbent took a special weapon, and the weapon took a tour of its own: a bishop in Stormwind, a shipwreck in the Wetlands, a forge back in Stormwind. Morbent's Bane, they called it.\n\nMorbent took one look at it and stopped being difficult."] = "Sven Yorgen's family was murdered, and what Sven wanted was a name. Somebody had buried a book behind an old stump on his farm, and I spent a good while showing it around Darkshire. The clues, once I had them all: not from around here, nervous, jittery, gone west. I found him out past town. Folks call him Jitters. Some people just make it easy.\n\nOnce Jitters had finished that journal, we had our name. Morbent Fel, a necromancer out at Raven Hill. Getting at Morbent took a special weapon, and the weapon took a tour of its own: a bishop in Stormwind, a shipwreck in the Wetlands, a forge back in Stormwind. Morbent's Bane, they called it.\n\nMorbent took one look at it and stopped being difficult."
L["Sirra Von'Indi keeps a library in Darkshire, and Sirra had noticed what nobody else had bothered to: Raven Hill's history had died along with its people. I went poking through the ruins and came back with a tome about a family called Valor. Heroes, the whole lot of them. Dead heroes, which in Duskwood means they're still around.\n\nThere were four of them, each one had left a weapon behind, and every weapon came with a chore. One wanted a mace back from a skeleton that had walked off with it. The others wanted Defias, worgs and ogres seen to. Heroes to the last, and still handing out work."] = "Sirra Von'Indi keeps a library in Darkshire, and Sirra had noticed what nobody else had bothered to: Raven Hill's history had died along with its people. I went poking through the ruins and came back with a tome about a family called Valor. Heroes, the whole lot of them. Dead heroes, which in Duskwood means they're still around.\n\nThere were four of them, each one had left a weapon behind, and every weapon came with a chore. One wanted a mace back from a skeleton that had walked off with it. The others wanted Defias, worgs and ogres seen to. Heroes to the last, and still handing out work."
L["None of it put them to rest. So I carried the family's weapons back to Raven Hill, to find out what single night had pinned them all there."] = "None of it put them to rest. So I carried the family's weapons back to Raven Hill, to find out what single night had pinned them all there."
L["North of Raven Hill there's a grave so weathered you can hardly read it. I asked around Darkshire whose it was, and the librarian knew. Morgan Ladimore. It's a sad story, and I'll leave the telling of it to the people who live there. The short of it: something called Mor'Ladim walks the woods near that grave, and the commander of the Night Watch wanted him gone for good.\n\nI brought back his skull. Then the commander told me the rest. One of Ladimore's children had lived, and she was right there in Darkshire, standing watch. Sarah Ladimore.\n\nSarah heard me out, then gave me a ring and asked me to leave it on her father's grave. So I walked back up the hill and did.\n\nSome errands you don't make jokes about. That's one."] = "North of Raven Hill there's a grave so weathered you can hardly read it. I asked around Darkshire whose it was, and the librarian knew. Morgan Ladimore. It's a sad story, and I'll leave the telling of it to the people who live there. The short of it: something called Mor'Ladim walks the woods near that grave, and the commander of the Night Watch wanted him gone for good.\n\nI brought back his skull. Then the commander told me the rest. One of Ladimore's children had lived, and she was right there in Darkshire, standing watch. Sarah Ladimore.\n\nSarah heard me out, then gave me a ring and asked me to leave it on her father's grave. So I walked back up the hill and did.\n\nSome errands you don't make jokes about. That's one."
L["Madame Eva had a vision, and the vision had a name in it. Stalvan. That one word cost me a walk across half the kingdom.\n\nStalvan himself was hard to find. His paperwork was everywhere. A town registry, a footlocker in a Westfall schoolhouse, a letter that never got delivered, a crate of somebody's old things down in the Stormwind canals, a chest upstairs in a marshal's house in Elwynn. Every one of them held a page, and every page was worse than the last. A faded one. A muddy one. A bloody one.\n\nIt ended at Mistmantle Manor with Stalvan Mistmantle, who didn't want visitors. I brought his family ring back to Madame Eva. Eva didn't look surprised. I doubt anything surprises her."] = "Madame Eva had a vision, and the vision had a name in it. Stalvan. That one word cost me a walk across half the kingdom.\n\nStalvan himself was hard to find. His paperwork was everywhere. A town registry, a footlocker in a Westfall schoolhouse, a letter that never got delivered, a crate of somebody's old things down in the Stormwind canals, a chest upstairs in a marshal's house in Elwynn. Every one of them held a page, and every page was worse than the last. A faded one. A muddy one. A bloody one.\n\nIt ended at Mistmantle Manor with Stalvan Mistmantle, who didn't want visitors. I brought his family ring back to Madame Eva. Eva didn't look surprised. I doubt anything surprises her."
L["Duskwood has worgen. Of course it does. The Nightbane prowl Brightwood Grove and the Rotting Orchard, and someone in Darkshire called Calor keeps count of them.\n\nCalor started me on the shadow weavers, which are worgen who cast spells, which is a thing I'd been happier not knowing about. After that it was runners and fangs and whatever else the pack could spare, and every trip out, the woods got a little quieter and I got a little choosier about where I put my back.\n\nWhen it was done, Calor wrote me a note for Master Carevin. I'd been on trial the whole time without being told. Seems I passed. The reward for passing was being trusted with more worgen."] = "Duskwood has worgen. Of course it does. The Nightbane prowl Brightwood Grove and the Rotting Orchard, and someone in Darkshire called Calor keeps count of them.\n\nCalor started me on the shadow weavers, which are worgen who cast spells, which is a thing I'd been happier not knowing about. After that it was runners and fangs and whatever else the pack could spare, and every trip out, the woods got a little quieter and I got a little choosier about where I put my back.\n\nWhen it was done, Calor wrote me a note for Master Carevin. I'd been on trial the whole time without being told. Seems I passed. The reward for passing was being trusted with more worgen."
L["Up past the Raven Hill cemetery, in a shack, lives an old hermit called Abercrombie. Lonely sort, you'd think. Wanted a few things from town, and I was glad to help.\n\nHere's what he wanted. Thread spun from ghost hair. Zombie juice. A pile of ghoul ribs. Looking back, the ghoul ribs were a hint.\n\nThen he gave me a letter for the mayor, and the mayor couldn't read it. It had to be translated, and not long after the mayor read it properly, something called Stitches came lurching down the road toward Darkshire. Abercrombie had been building company.\n\nThere was a bride, too. Eliza, out in the cemetery. Eliza had the Embalmer's heart, so I took it off her and brought it to the mayor. Courting in Duskwood is a grim business."] = "Up past the Raven Hill cemetery, in a shack, lives an old hermit called Abercrombie. Lonely sort, you'd think. Wanted a few things from town, and I was glad to help.\n\nHere's what he wanted. Thread spun from ghost hair. Zombie juice. A pile of ghoul ribs. Looking back, the ghoul ribs were a hint.\n\nThen he gave me a letter for the mayor, and the mayor couldn't read it. It had to be translated, and not long after the mayor read it properly, something called Stitches came lurching down the road toward Darkshire. Abercrombie had been building company.\n\nThere was a bride, too. Eliza, out in the cemetery. Eliza had the Embalmer's heart, so I took it off her and brought it to the mayor. Courting in Duskwood is a grim business."
L["Deputy Willem, outside Northshire Abbey, has a new gang of thieves on his hands. They call themselves the Defias Brotherhood, they've been seen across the river to the east, and nobody knows what they're up to."] = "Deputy Willem, outside Northshire Abbey, has a new gang of thieves on his hands. They call themselves the Defias Brotherhood, they've been seen across the river to the east, and nobody knows what they're up to."
L["Westfall's farmers have been driven off their land, and Gryan Stoutmantle means to win it back. He's raising a People's Militia in the stone tower on Sentinel Hill, and he'll take every recruit who turns up."] = "Westfall's farmers have been driven off their land, and Gryan Stoutmantle means to win it back. He's raising a People's Militia in the stone tower on Sentinel Hill, and he'll take every recruit who turns up."
L["Gryan Stoutmantle knows a good deal more about the Defias now, but not where they hide. One of his scouts has spotted a Defias messenger on the roads around Moonbrook, and messengers carry things."] = "Gryan Stoutmantle knows a good deal more about the Defias now, but not where they hide. One of his scouts has spotted a Defias messenger on the roads around Moonbrook, and messengers carry things."
L["Thanks to a turncoat, Gryan Stoutmantle knows where the Defias hide: the old mine under Moonbrook. Back on Sentinel Hill, Gryan has one task left for you, and it has a name on it. Edwin VanCleef."] = "Thanks to a turncoat, Gryan Stoutmantle knows where the Defias hide: the old mine under Moonbrook. Back on Sentinel Hill, Gryan has one task left for you, and it has a name on it. Edwin VanCleef."
L["Edwin VanCleef was carrying a letter, sealed and never sent. It's addressed to Baros Alexston, the City Architect, at City Hall in Stormwind's Cathedral Square. Somebody ought to deliver it."] = "Edwin VanCleef was carrying a letter, sealed and never sent. It's addressed to Baros Alexston, the City Architect, at City Hall in Stormwind's Cathedral Square. Somebody ought to deliver it."
L["Bazil Thredd had a regular visitor in the Stockade lately: a quiet man whose papers said Maelik. Warden Thelwater had suspicions, wrote down what he looked like, and sealed it for Baros Alexston."] = "Bazil Thredd had a regular visitor in the Stockade lately: a quiet man whose papers said Maelik. Warden Thelwater had suspicions, wrote down what he looked like, and sealed it for Baros Alexston."
L["Up at Stormwind Keep, Tyrion's spybot has its disguise and Tyrion has arranged a meeting. Lord Gregor Lescovar will be waiting in the castle garden for Marzon the Silent Blade. Tyrion wants you close enough to hear every word."] = "Up at Stormwind Keep, Tyrion's spybot has its disguise and Tyrion has arranged a meeting. Lord Gregor Lescovar will be waiting in the castle garden for Marzon the Silent Blade. Tyrion wants you close enough to hear every word."
L["Executor Arren, in Deathknell, has work for the newly risen. Spiders first. But the Scarlet Crusade has made camp at the edge of the valley, and Arren hasn't forgotten them for a moment."] = "Executor Arren, in Deathknell, has work for the newly risen. Spiders first. But the Scarlet Crusade has made camp at the edge of the valley, and Arren hasn't forgotten them for a moment."
L["Brill is at war with the Scarlet Crusade camps west of town, and everyone in Brill has a part for you. Deathguard Simmer starts small: steal some pumpkins off a farm north of Deathknell. Executor Zygand thinks bigger."] = "Brill is at war with the Scarlet Crusade camps west of town, and everyone in Brill has a part for you. Deathguard Simmer starts small: steal some pumpkins off a farm north of Deathknell. Executor Zygand thinks bigger."
L["Brother Crowley, in Stormwind's Cathedral Square, wants you to call on Brother Anton. Anton is at Nijel's Point in Desolace, which is a long way to go for a conversation. Crowley doesn't say what it's about. Anton will."] = "Brother Crowley, in Stormwind's Cathedral Square, wants you to call on Brother Anton. Anton is at Nijel's Point in Desolace, which is a long way to go for a conversation. Crowley doesn't say what it's about. Anton will."
L["Librarian Mae Paledust, in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers, wants one book: the Mythology of the Titans. The copy Mae has in mind sits in the library of the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades, and the Crusade doesn't lend."] = "Librarian Mae Paledust, in Ironforge's Hall of Explorers, wants one book: the Mythology of the Titans. The copy Mae has in mind sits in the library of the Scarlet Monastery in Tirisfal Glades, and the Crusade doesn't lend."
L["Deep in the Scarlet Monastery's graveyard, in Tirisfal Glades, a prisoner named Vorrel Sengutz is waiting for someone to get that far. The library has customers too: Sage Truthseeker, in Thunder Bluff, wants a book off its shelves."] = "Deep in the Scarlet Monastery's graveyard, in Tirisfal Glades, a prisoner named Vorrel Sengutz is waiting for someone to get that far. The library has customers too: Sage Truthseeker, in Thunder Bluff, wants a book off its shelves."
L["Raleigh the Devout, in Southshore, wants the leaders of the Scarlet Monastery dead, in the name of the Light. They would say they do everything in its name too. There are four of them, from the kennels to the Cathedral."] = "Raleigh the Devout, in Southshore, wants the leaders of the Scarlet Monastery dead, in the name of the Light. They would say they do everything in its name too. There are four of them, from the kennels to the Cathedral."
L["Varimathras wants a word in the Undercity's Royal Quarter. The dreadlord has four names for you, the leaders of the Scarlet Monastery from the kennels to the Cathedral, and wants every one of them dead. Say yes quickly."] = "Varimathras wants a word in the Undercity's Royal Quarter. The dreadlord has four names for you, the leaders of the Scarlet Monastery from the kennels to the Cathedral, and wants every one of them dead. Say yes quickly."
L["Duke Nicholas Zverenhoff of the Argent Dawn, at Light's Hope Chapel, wants a fire started. The Scarlet Crusade keeps an archive in Stratholme, and the duke wants it burned, along with the archivist who keeps it."] = "Duke Nicholas Zverenhoff of the Argent Dawn, at Light's Hope Chapel, wants a fire started. The Scarlet Crusade keeps an archive in Stratholme, and the duke wants it burned, along with the archivist who keeps it."
L["Kirsta Deepshadow, in the north of the Western Plaguelands, has unfinished business with the Scarlet Crusade and could use a hand with it. Their outer lines are close by. Behind them sits Hearthglen."] = "Kirsta Deepshadow, in the north of the Western Plaguelands, has unfinished business with the Scarlet Crusade and could use a hand with it. Their outer lines are close by. Behind them sits Hearthglen."
L["Nathanos Blightcaller keeps to Marris Stead in the Eastern Plaguelands and doesn't do small talk. He has orders for you, and they start with a trip home: Varimathras, in the Undercity, is expecting you."] = "Nathanos Blightcaller keeps to Marris Stead in the Eastern Plaguelands and doesn't do small talk. He has orders for you, and they start with a trip home: Varimathras, in the Undercity, is expecting you."
L["At the western edge of the Eastern Plaguelands, an old man named Tirion Fordring lives alone in a cottage, a long way from anybody. He has a story to tell and a favor to ask. Sit down for the story."] = "At the western edge of the Eastern Plaguelands, an old man named Tirion Fordring lives alone in a cottage, a long way from anybody. He has a story to tell and a favor to ask. Sit down for the story."
L["High Executor Derrington, at the Bulwark, wants the Scarlet Crusade busy fighting somebody other than the Forsaken, starting with their camp near Felstone Field. Over in the Eastern Plaguelands, Tirion Fordring lives alone in a cottage and has a favor to ask."] = "High Executor Derrington, at the Bulwark, wants the Scarlet Crusade busy fighting somebody other than the Forsaken, starting with their camp near Felstone Field. Over in the Eastern Plaguelands, Tirion Fordring lives alone in a cottage and has a favor to ask."
L["Belann Windwood in Valanaar has made staves and wands for the High Order's mages for more than a century, and now it's your turn to visit. Belann keeps one of the order's oldest traditions. It asks for a fallen bough, and Belann is particular."] = "Belann Windwood in Valanaar has made staves and wands for the High Order's mages for more than a century, and now it's your turn to visit. Belann keeps one of the order's oldest traditions. It asks for a fallen bough, and Belann is particular."
L["Your trainer says it's time you met Tabetha. She lives out in Dustwallow Marsh, far from any library, and she's where trainers send a mage once book learning stops being enough."] = "Your trainer says it's time you met Tabetha. She lives out in Dustwallow Marsh, far from any library, and she's where trainers send a mage once book learning stops being enough."
L["Archmage Xylem lives in a tower atop the cliffs of Azshara, about as far from anyone as a tower can get. Xylem wants a Prismatic Shell, and a warlord named Krellian has one. Archmages call this research."] = "Archmage Xylem lives in a tower atop the cliffs of Azshara, about as far from anyone as a tower can get. Xylem wants a Prismatic Shell, and a warlord named Krellian has one. Archmages call this research."
L["Deep in Dire Maul, Lorekeeper Lydros still keeps the Athenaeum, and still knows a spell or two worth learning. The price is the essence of Hydrospawn, a water elemental loose in the Warpwood Quarter. Lydros calls that a fair trade."] = "Deep in Dire Maul, Lorekeeper Lydros still keeps the Athenaeum, and still knows a spell or two worth learning. The price is the essence of Hydrospawn, a water elemental loose in the Warpwood Quarter. Lydros calls that a fair trade."
L["Your trainer passes along a summons from a warlock in the city. It's time you called something out of the void and put it to work. It won't want to. They never do."] = "Your trainer passes along a summons from a warlock in the city. It's time you called something out of the void and put it to work. It won't want to. They never do."
L["Your trainer thinks you're ready for a subtler demon than the voidwalker. A succubus takes more than a strong will to bind, and your trainer knows who can tell you what else it takes. Subtle things rarely come cheap."] = "Your trainer thinks you're ready for a subtler demon than the voidwalker. A succubus takes more than a strong will to bind, and your trainer knows who can tell you what else it takes. Subtle things rarely come cheap."
L["Your trainer sends you down to Ratchet to find Strahad Farsan, who knows how to bind a felhunter. A felhunter eats magic. You'll want to be very sure of the binding before you try it."] = "Your trainer sends you down to Ratchet to find Strahad Farsan, who knows how to bind a felhunter. A felhunter eats magic. You'll want to be very sure of the binding before you try it."
L["Your trainer sends you down to Ratchet, to Strahad Farsan, who knows how a warlock's mount is called. No stable sells one. It comes from somewhere a good deal warmer than Ratchet."] = "Your trainer sends you down to Ratchet, to Strahad Farsan, who knows how a warlock's mount is called. No stable sells one. It comes from somewhere a good deal warmer than Ratchet."
L["The demon trainer in the city has a name for you: Mor'zul Bloodbringer, out in the Burning Steppes. Mor'zul knows how to call a dreadsteed of Xoroth, and doesn't share that with just anyone."] = "The demon trainer in the city has a name for you: Mor'zul Bloodbringer, out in the Burning Steppes. Mor'zul knows how to call a dreadsteed of Xoroth, and doesn't share that with just anyone."
L["Shaw doesn't do pep talks. He sends me to Westfall to see his man Kearnen, and Kearnen points me at a Defias tower full of people who'd love to see me dead. Fine. I lift the key, I'm up the stairs, and there's a chest with a lovely lock on it. I open the lovely lock. Whatever was waiting inside opens me right back.\n\nBy the time I'm standing in front of Shaw again I'm a funny color and the floor won't hold still. The Touch of Zanzil, it turns out, and the only cure in Stormwind is a goblin in the barracks cellar called Doc Mixilpixil. First he has me lie down on his floor so he can look at me. Then he calls it the itis and sends me shopping: wildflowers, a vial, a tube, a spool of thread. Still don't know what the thread was for. It worked, though. And I learned something about poison that day. I'd rather be on the handle end."] = "Shaw doesn't do pep talks. He sends me to Westfall to see his man Kearnen, and Kearnen points me at a Defias tower full of people who'd love to see me dead. Fine. I lift the key, I'm up the stairs, and there's a chest with a lovely lock on it. I open the lovely lock. Whatever was waiting inside opens me right back.\n\nBy the time I'm standing in front of Shaw again I'm a funny color and the floor won't hold still. The Touch of Zanzil, it turns out, and the only cure in Stormwind is a goblin in the barracks cellar called Doc Mixilpixil. First he has me lie down on his floor so he can look at me. Then he calls it the itis and sends me shopping: wildflowers, a vial, a tube, a spool of thread. Still don't know what the thread was for. It worked, though. And I learned something about poison that day. I'd rather be on the handle end."
L["Shenthul's idea of hello is a salute you have to learn first. Shattered Hand business. So I learned it, and then he sent me out to the Barrens to fire two flares at a Venture Co. tower and do the salute again for his man inside, Taskmaster Fizzule. Very subtle, the Shattered Hand.\n\nFizzule wanted the tower taken apart, quietly. I lifted the foreman's key, put a few of their people down, and Grand Foreman Gallywix didn't get to keep his head. His lockbox was full of Zanzil's mix, and some of it ended up in me. Shenthul knew the one man who could fix that, Serge Hinott, all the way up in Tarren Mill. Long walk. Then I sat on a stool while he brewed the cure, which is the most patient I have ever been.\n\nFizzule got the head. I got the story."] = "Shenthul's idea of hello is a salute you have to learn first. Shattered Hand business. So I learned it, and then he sent me out to the Barrens to fire two flares at a Venture Co. tower and do the salute again for his man inside, Taskmaster Fizzule. Very subtle, the Shattered Hand.\n\nFizzule wanted the tower taken apart, quietly. I lifted the foreman's key, put a few of their people down, and Grand Foreman Gallywix didn't get to keep his head. His lockbox was full of Zanzil's mix, and some of it ended up in me. Shenthul knew the one man who could fix that, Serge Hinott, all the way up in Tarren Mill. Long walk. Then I sat on a stool while he brewed the cure, which is the most patient I have ever been.\n\nFizzule got the head. I got the story."
L["Best letter I ever got had no name on it. Just a place, a cabin outside Pyrewood, and one letter at the bottom. C.\n\nIn the cabin there's a chest, and in the chest there's a job. Get the Horn of Xelthos out of Shadowfang Keep, and go alone. Alone, into Shadowfang. Sure. A goblin called Veenix up in Stonetalon got me past the courtyard gate, and after that it was me, the worgen and a lot of standing very still in corners. I put the horn in the chest and walked off, and a while later another letter tells me my pay is waiting. It was. Good pay, too.\n\nNext job was the Eye of Bhossca, in the Scarlet Monastery. I found a disguise on the grounds and strolled through the place like I'd been converted. Nobody blinked.\n\nNever met C. Never asked. When the money's that good, you don't."] = "Best letter I ever got had no name on it. Just a place, a cabin outside Pyrewood, and one letter at the bottom. C.\n\nIn the cabin there's a chest, and in the chest there's a job. Get the Horn of Xelthos out of Shadowfang Keep, and go alone. Alone, into Shadowfang. Sure. A goblin called Veenix up in Stonetalon got me past the courtyard gate, and after that it was me, the worgen and a lot of standing very still in corners. I put the horn in the chest and walked off, and a while later another letter tells me my pay is waiting. It was. Good pay, too.\n\nNext job was the Eye of Bhossca, in the Scarlet Monastery. I found a disguise on the grounds and strolled through the place like I'd been converted. Nobody blinked.\n\nNever met C. Never asked. When the money's that good, you don't."
L["Somebody in the city who knows somebody slips me a seal with a raven on it. Ravenholdt. It's a manor up in the hills above Hillsbrad where the best of us keep house, and you don't just walk up and knock. There's a test of cunning on the way in. I'm writing this, so you can guess how that went.\n\nFahrad met me inside. Lovely old place. Everybody's very polite and nobody turns their back on you. I liked it right away."] = "Somebody in the city who knows somebody slips me a seal with a raven on it. Ravenholdt. It's a manor up in the hills above Hillsbrad where the best of us keep house, and you don't just walk up and knock. There's a test of cunning on the way in. I'm writing this, so you can guess how that went.\n\nFahrad met me inside. Lovely old place. Everybody's very polite and nobody turns their back on you. I liked it right away."
L["Somebody in the city who knows somebody slips me a seal with a raven on it. Ravenholdt. It's a manor up in the hills above Hillsbrad, and you don't just walk up and knock. There's a test of cunning on the way in. I'm writing this, so you can guess how that went.\n\nOnce you're in, Myrokos Silentform keeps you busy. The Syndicate has been getting cocky up in Alterac, and Myrokos has opinions about that. Lord Tomas, Durnholde Keep: no longer a problem. A scouting report, lifted right out of a Syndicate pocket. The old Perenolde heirlooms, out of the ruins of Alterac before their diggers got there.\n\nBest one was Stromgarde. I put on a stolen Syndicate uniform and walked into the keep like I owned it. Found Anastasia, found the money she'd rather nobody knew about, and suddenly she was very chatty. Then I broke up their little handoff at Thoradin's Wall and carried the prize home."] = "Somebody in the city who knows somebody slips me a seal with a raven on it. Ravenholdt. It's a manor up in the hills above Hillsbrad, and you don't just walk up and knock. There's a test of cunning on the way in. I'm writing this, so you can guess how that went.\n\nOnce you're in, Myrokos Silentform keeps you busy. The Syndicate has been getting cocky up in Alterac, and Myrokos has opinions about that. Lord Tomas, Durnholde Keep: no longer a problem. A scouting report, lifted right out of a Syndicate pocket. The old Perenolde heirlooms, out of the ruins of Alterac before their diggers got there.\n\nBest one was Stromgarde. I put on a stolen Syndicate uniform and walked into the keep like I owned it. Found Anastasia, found the money she'd rather nobody knew about, and suddenly she was very chatty. Then I broke up their little handoff at Thoradin's Wall and carried the prize home."
L["Here's a fun way to find out you've been played. A letter shows up from Ravenholdt. Fahrad. Come home, we need to talk. So I go, and Fahrad sits me down and tells me C has a name. Caius Blackwood. Some big spellcaster in bed with the Syndicate, the very Syndicate I'd been cutting up for Myrokos, and there I was fetching his trinkets for him like a good dog.\n\nSo we fixed it. C wanted one more thing, the Talisman of Kazdor, out of Zul'Farrak. I went in alone, like always, and brought it out, but this time it went to the manor first. Zan Shivsproket took it down to the basement and put a little surprise inside. Anybody tries to pour magic into it or out of it, boom. Then I set it in the chest, same as ever, and walked away whistling.\n\nFahrad says now we wait. Fine by me. Waiting's half the job."] = "Here's a fun way to find out you've been played. A letter shows up from Ravenholdt. Fahrad. Come home, we need to talk. So I go, and Fahrad sits me down and tells me C has a name. Caius Blackwood. Some big spellcaster in bed with the Syndicate, the very Syndicate I'd been cutting up for Myrokos, and there I was fetching his trinkets for him like a good dog.\n\nSo we fixed it. C wanted one more thing, the Talisman of Kazdor, out of Zul'Farrak. I went in alone, like always, and brought it out, but this time it went to the manor first. Zan Shivsproket took it down to the basement and put a little surprise inside. Anybody tries to pour magic into it or out of it, boom. Then I set it in the chest, same as ever, and walked away whistling.\n\nFahrad says now we wait. Fine by me. Waiting's half the job."
L["So there's this apothecary in Brill. Johaan. Hums while he works. First day, he sends me out to kill dogs for their blood. Then murlocs for their scales, then spiders for their venom, and I'm thinking this had better be a soup.\n\nIt was not a soup. It was a drink, and the lucky guest was a dwarf they were keeping in the tavern cellar. I'll spare you that part. Johaan was thrilled. He wrote it all down, handed me his findings for Silverpine and told me to walk carefully.\n\nI walked very carefully."] = "So there's this apothecary in Brill. Johaan. Hums while he works. First day, he sends me out to kill dogs for their blood. Then murlocs for their scales, then spiders for their venom, and I'm thinking this had better be a soup.\n\nIt was not a soup. It was a drink, and the lucky guest was a dwarf they were keeping in the tavern cellar. I'll spare you that part. Johaan was thrilled. He wrote it all down, handed me his findings for Silverpine and told me to walk carefully.\n\nI walked very carefully."
L["Silverpine is where Johaan's work goes to grow up. Renferrel at the Sepulcher wanted bear hearts and spider blood, which by then felt like ordinary shopping, and I hauled the lot down to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity. He looked at it the way most people look at cake.\n\nTurns out somebody had tried this recipe before. A fellow called Berard, whose journal was still lying around in Pyrewood Village. Lovely place. Do not stay the night. I got the journal out, and then Faranell wanted moss off the things in Lake Lordamere and a hardened tumor, and I stopped asking what any of it was for.\n\nThat's the trick with apothecaries. The less you ask, the better you sleep."] = "Silverpine is where Johaan's work goes to grow up. Renferrel at the Sepulcher wanted bear hearts and spider blood, which by then felt like ordinary shopping, and I hauled the lot down to Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity. He looked at it the way most people look at cake.\n\nTurns out somebody had tried this recipe before. A fellow called Berard, whose journal was still lying around in Pyrewood Village. Lovely place. Do not stay the night. I got the journal out, and then Faranell wanted moss off the things in Lake Lordamere and a hardened tumor, and I stopped asking what any of it was for.\n\nThat's the trick with apothecaries. The less you ask, the better you sleep."
L["Apothecary Lydon runs things in Tarren Mill, and Lydon likes to test his work on whoever's handy. I brought him bear tongues and creeper ichor, he cooked up the Elixir of Suffering, and then he handed it back to me. Not to drink. For Umpi, the poor beast he keeps in his room. Umpi did not enjoy it.\n\nNext came mountain lion blood for the Elixir of Pain, and that one was for Stanley. Stanley lived on a farm down in Hillsbrad Fields and was, until I came along, a very good dog. One sip and he was something else entirely, and we had words, and I won.\n\nLydon was delighted. Lydon is always delighted."] = "Apothecary Lydon runs things in Tarren Mill, and Lydon likes to test his work on whoever's handy. I brought him bear tongues and creeper ichor, he cooked up the Elixir of Suffering, and then he handed it back to me. Not to drink. For Umpi, the poor beast he keeps in his room. Umpi did not enjoy it.\n\nNext came mountain lion blood for the Elixir of Pain, and that one was for Stanley. Stanley lived on a farm down in Hillsbrad Fields and was, until I came along, a very good dog. One sip and he was something else entirely, and we had words, and I won.\n\nLydon was delighted. Lydon is always delighted."
L["The Elixir of Agony was Lydon's masterpiece, and it took half of Hillsbrad to make. Mudsnout blossoms. A trip down to Faranell so he could add his own little touch. Troll's blood, scales off the naga on the coast, eyes off the murlocs. And then the secret ingredient: beer. A keg of Shindigger Stout from Dun Garok.\n\nLydon had some farmers locked upstairs at the inn, and the farmers, he figured, would drink anything. He tainted the keg and I carried it up and set it on their rug, friendly as you like.\n\nThen I went downstairs and had a drink of my own. Not from that keg."] = "The Elixir of Agony was Lydon's masterpiece, and it took half of Hillsbrad to make. Mudsnout blossoms. A trip down to Faranell so he could add his own little touch. Troll's blood, scales off the naga on the coast, eyes off the murlocs. And then the secret ingredient: beer. A keg of Shindigger Stout from Dun Garok.\n\nLydon had some farmers locked upstairs at the inn, and the farmers, he figured, would drink anything. He tainted the keg and I carried it up and set it on their rug, friendly as you like.\n\nThen I went downstairs and had a drink of my own. Not from that keg."
L["Next job for the Society was back home in the Apothecarium, for a fellow named Theodore Griffs. He'd heard about a spider up in the ruins over the city, Witherfang, whose venom eats right through dead flesh. In the Undercity that's not a rumor. That's a job offer.\n\nSo into the Ruins of Lordaeron we went, a few of us, because nobody sane goes in there alone. Witherfang was big and quick and not at all happy to see us. We brought Griffs his Highly Toxic Strain anyway, and he held the vial up to the light like a jeweler.\n\nEverything else I'd fetched them was for killing the living. That one was for the dead. I went up for some air after that, which in the Undercity takes a while."] = "Next job for the Society was back home in the Apothecarium, for a fellow named Theodore Griffs. He'd heard about a spider up in the ruins over the city, Witherfang, whose venom eats right through dead flesh. In the Undercity that's not a rumor. That's a job offer.\n\nSo into the Ruins of Lordaeron we went, a few of us, because nobody sane goes in there alone. Witherfang was big and quick and not at all happy to see us. We brought Griffs his Highly Toxic Strain anyway, and he held the vial up to the light like a jeweler.\n\nEverything else I'd fetched them was for killing the living. That one was for the dead. I went up for some air after that, which in the Undercity takes a while."
L["Shaw sends me to Theramore to meet his man there, Sully McCleary. Sully won't say a word in the inn. Too many ears. Out behind the graveyard he hands me a disguise kit, and next thing I know I'm one of Jaina's marines, standing guard in her tower while she and her people talk.\n\nYou learn a lot standing still in a room full of important people who think you're furniture. A man had washed up on the shore, and what he claimed had them all worried. And there it was again and again in their talk: the island.\n\nI walked out of there slow, like a marine with nowhere to be, and didn't breathe right until I was back in the marsh."] = "Shaw sends me to Theramore to meet his man there, Sully McCleary. Sully won't say a word in the inn. Too many ears. Out behind the graveyard he hands me a disguise kit, and next thing I know I'm one of Jaina's marines, standing guard in her tower while she and her people talk.\n\nYou learn a lot standing still in a room full of important people who think you're furniture. A man had washed up on the shore, and what he claimed had them all worried. And there it was again and again in their talk: the island.\n\nI walked out of there slow, like a marine with nowhere to be, and didn't breathe right until I was back in the marsh."
L["Eitrigg doesn't waste words. A band of Kor'kron went into Dustwallow Marsh and didn't come back. Find out. That's the whole briefing.\n\nAt Brackenwall, Nazeer Bloodpike remembered them all right. Loud, proud, and talking about the island. Which island? Nobody thought to ask a Kor'kron.\n\nThe trail ran out at Sergeant Ulka, sitting by the water with a wound that would have dropped most people, insisting everything was under control. I offered a hand. The sergeant would not take it. That's the Kor'kron for you. You'd admire it if it weren't so tiring."] = "Eitrigg doesn't waste words. A band of Kor'kron went into Dustwallow Marsh and didn't come back. Find out. That's the whole briefing.\n\nAt Brackenwall, Nazeer Bloodpike remembered them all right. Loud, proud, and talking about the island. Which island? Nobody thought to ask a Kor'kron.\n\nThe trail ran out at Sergeant Ulka, sitting by the water with a wound that would have dropped most people, insisting everything was under control. I offered a hand. The sergeant would not take it. That's the Kor'kron for you. You'd admire it if it weren't so tiring."
L["Alcaz Island. An old keep, rotten docks, and under it a prison nobody was ever supposed to know about. By the time I got down there it was already a brawl: guards in blood-red bandanas, naga coming up out of the water, and black dragons, because of course.\n\nI went looking for the king. Found a torturer they called the Questioner instead, and he had a key, and the key fit the chains on a fellow named Mason Connery. Nice man. Very grateful. Then a note on the floor told me the rest. The king had died trying to escape, just before the naga came in.\n\nI read it twice. Then I took it back to Sully, and Sully went very quiet."] = "Alcaz Island. An old keep, rotten docks, and under it a prison nobody was ever supposed to know about. By the time I got down there it was already a brawl: guards in blood-red bandanas, naga coming up out of the water, and black dragons, because of course.\n\nI went looking for the king. Found a torturer they called the Questioner instead, and he had a key, and the key fit the chains on a fellow named Mason Connery. Nice man. Very grateful. Then a note on the floor told me the rest. The king had died trying to escape, just before the naga came in.\n\nI read it twice. Then I took it back to Sully, and Sully went very quiet."
L["Ulka sent me in after Commander Folkar. Alcaz Prison was a mess: guards in blood-red bandanas, naga out of the sea, black dragons for good measure. Folkar was alive, barely, his axe arm broken and most of his elites dead around him, and still every inch a Kor'kron. He told me why they'd come. The Warchief wanted Varian Wrynn, King of Stormwind, brought to Orgrimmar alive.\n\nSo I went looking for a king. I found a torturer called the Questioner, took his key, and let a chained prisoner named Mason Connery out of his irons. Then a note on the ground told me the rest. Varian had died trying to escape, just before the naga hit.\n\nI had to go back and tell Folkar. Longest short walk of my life."] = "Ulka sent me in after Commander Folkar. Alcaz Prison was a mess: guards in blood-red bandanas, naga out of the sea, black dragons for good measure. Folkar was alive, barely, his axe arm broken and most of his elites dead around him, and still every inch a Kor'kron. He told me why they'd come. The Warchief wanted Varian Wrynn, King of Stormwind, brought to Orgrimmar alive.\n\nSo I went looking for a king. I found a torturer called the Questioner, took his key, and let a chained prisoner named Mason Connery out of his irons. Then a note on the ground told me the rest. Varian had died trying to escape, just before the naga hit.\n\nI had to go back and tell Folkar. Longest short walk of my life."
L["Sully begged me to tell no one but Shaw, so I told Shaw, and Shaw took it the way he takes everything, which is to say you'd never know.\n\nConnery had pressed a note into my hand on the way out. A treasure, he said, worth more than gold, buried in Westfall. I dug it up. A box of papers. Worth more than gold to somebody, I'm sure. Renzik, Shaw's goblin, was dying to see them, and a goblin in Booty Bay named Rix Xizzix would have paid me for them instead. I'll let you guess.\n\nConnery only asked one thing in return. If anyone wonders, he's dead. Caught in dragonfire and blown out to sea. So, for the record: tragic. Never met the man."] = "Sully begged me to tell no one but Shaw, so I told Shaw, and Shaw took it the way he takes everything, which is to say you'd never know.\n\nConnery had pressed a note into my hand on the way out. A treasure, he said, worth more than gold, buried in Westfall. I dug it up. A box of papers. Worth more than gold to somebody, I'm sure. Renzik, Shaw's goblin, was dying to see them, and a goblin in Booty Bay named Rix Xizzix would have paid me for them instead. I'll let you guess.\n\nConnery only asked one thing in return. If anyone wonders, he's dead. Caught in dragonfire and blown out to sea. So, for the record: tragic. Never met the man."
L["Folkar couldn't make the trip himself, so I carried it to Thrall: the king was dead, and the Kor'kron had come home with nothing but bad news and a broken arm. Thrall listened. Thrall always listens. Somehow that's worse.\n\nConnery had pressed a note into my hand on the way out. A treasure in Westfall, worth more than gold. It was a box of papers. Nazgrel, who advises the Warchief, wanted a look, and a goblin in Booty Bay named Rix Xizzix would have paid me for them instead. I'll let you guess.\n\nConnery only asked one thing in return. If anyone wonders, he's dead. Caught in dragonfire and blown out to sea. So, for the record: tragic. Never met the man."] = "Folkar couldn't make the trip himself, so I carried it to Thrall: the king was dead, and the Kor'kron had come home with nothing but bad news and a broken arm. Thrall listened. Thrall always listens. Somehow that's worse.\n\nConnery had pressed a note into my hand on the way out. A treasure in Westfall, worth more than gold. It was a box of papers. Nazgrel, who advises the Warchief, wanted a look, and a goblin in Booty Bay named Rix Xizzix would have paid me for them instead. I'll let you guess.\n\nConnery only asked one thing in return. If anyone wonders, he's dead. Caught in dragonfire and blown out to sea. So, for the record: tragic. Never met the man."
L["There's a wrecked raft in a pond in Un'Goro, and if you poke around it you'll find a small pack. Inside: a large compass, a curled map, a key with a lion's head on it, and a photograph of a gnome dressed in green.\n\nWell. Marshal's Refuge isn't far, and there's a gnome there called Linken. You can see where this is going.\n\nLinken knew the things were his. Linken did not know much else. Where he'd been, what the key opened, why he'd been rafting through a crater full of dinosaurs: all gone. What he did have was a sword, and a name to take it to. Donova Snowden, in Winterspring.\n\nThe far end of Kalimdor, naturally. I've noticed that about lost things. They're never lost anywhere close."] = "There's a wrecked raft in a pond in Un'Goro, and if you poke around it you'll find a small pack. Inside: a large compass, a curled map, a key with a lion's head on it, and a photograph of a gnome dressed in green.\n\nWell. Marshal's Refuge isn't far, and there's a gnome there called Linken. You can see where this is going.\n\nLinken knew the things were his. Linken did not know much else. Where he'd been, what the key opened, why he'd been rafting through a crater full of dinosaurs: all gone. What he did have was a sword, and a name to take it to. Donova Snowden, in Winterspring.\n\nThe far end of Kalimdor, naturally. I've noticed that about lost things. They're never lost anywhere close."
L["Donova Snowden took one look at Linken's sword and decided the answer was in a graveyard. Getting there was the strange part.\n\nIt took a trip to Feralas for something called the Videre Elixir, and then the graveyard outside Gadgetzan, where Donova's instructions were very clear. Drink the elixir by the gravestone. I drank it. I died.\n\nThat was the plan, mind you. As a ghost I could meet Gaeriyan, who told me which grave to look at once I had a body again. So I walked back to my body, got up, found the gravestone, and came away with a better sword than the one I'd brought.\n\nFirst time I ever died on instructions. Strangely restful."] = "Donova Snowden took one look at Linken's sword and decided the answer was in a graveyard. Getting there was the strange part.\n\nIt took a trip to Feralas for something called the Videre Elixir, and then the graveyard outside Gadgetzan, where Donova's instructions were very clear. Drink the elixir by the gravestone. I drank it. I died.\n\nThat was the plan, mind you. As a ghost I could meet Gaeriyan, who told me which grave to look at once I had a body again. So I walked back to my body, got up, found the gravestone, and came away with a better sword than the one I'd brought.\n\nFirst time I ever died on instructions. Strangely restful."
L["Eridan Bluewind, up in Felwood, knew what Linken had been trying to make: a silver heart.\n\nThat took a fistful of silvery claws and an irontree heart, which meant arguing with a good deal of local wildlife, and then a book. The Book of Aquor. Carry the lot down to Tanaris, open the book, and out comes Aquementas, a water elemental, standing in the middle of the desert.\n\nAquementas did not care to be summoned. Once we'd settled that, the claws and the heart had become the Silver Totem of Aquementas, and I carried it back to Linken.\n\nA water elemental, called up in the driest place I know, to make a totem for a gnome who couldn't remember where he'd been. You couldn't make that up. I didn't."] = "Eridan Bluewind, up in Felwood, knew what Linken had been trying to make: a silver heart.\n\nThat took a fistful of silvery claws and an irontree heart, which meant arguing with a good deal of local wildlife, and then a book. The Book of Aquor. Carry the lot down to Tanaris, open the book, and out comes Aquementas, a water elemental, standing in the middle of the desert.\n\nAquementas did not care to be summoned. Once we'd settled that, the claws and the heart had become the Silver Totem of Aquementas, and I carried it back to Linken.\n\nA water elemental, called up in the driest place I know, to make a totem for a gnome who couldn't remember where he'd been. You couldn't make that up. I didn't."
L["Linken had one more job, and it was the reason for the totem. Fire Plume Ridge, just south of the Refuge. That's where Blazerunner lives, wrapped in a protective aura nothing gets through. Nothing except a silver totem made out of a water elemental.\n\nSomebody once said it's dangerous to go alone. I went anyway. I held up the totem, the aura went out like a candle in the rain, and Blazerunner turned out to be a lot less impressive without it. In the cave beyond sat the Golden Flame, and I carried it back to Linken.\n\nHe still doesn't remember everything. But he has his sword, his flame and an adventure to show for it. I have a story nobody believes. Tell it in any tavern and watch them smile at you like you've had one too many."] = "Linken had one more job, and it was the reason for the totem. Fire Plume Ridge, just south of the Refuge. That's where Blazerunner lives, wrapped in a protective aura nothing gets through. Nothing except a silver totem made out of a water elemental.\n\nSomebody once said it's dangerous to go alone. I went anyway. I held up the totem, the aura went out like a candle in the rain, and Blazerunner turned out to be a lot less impressive without it. In the cave beyond sat the Golden Flame, and I carried it back to Linken.\n\nHe still doesn't remember everything. But he has his sword, his flame and an adventure to show for it. I have a story nobody believes. Tell it in any tavern and watch them smile at you like you've had one too many."
L["Thomas, in Cathedral Square in Stormwind, has a message for someone who can keep quiet: Bishop DeLavey would like to see you in Stormwind Keep. The bishop isn't saying why. Not out loud, anyway."] = "Thomas, in Cathedral Square in Stormwind, has a message for someone who can keep quiet: Bishop DeLavey would like to see you in Stormwind Keep. The bishop isn't saying why. Not out loud, anyway."
L["Elling Trias's sealed note has brought you to Watcher Backus, on the road north of Darkshire. Now the watcher wants you out at Addle's Stead in western Duskwood, where the Defias may have left some paperwork lying around."] = "Elling Trias's sealed note has brought you to Watcher Backus, on the road north of Darkshire. Now the watcher wants you out at Addle's Stead in western Duskwood, where the Defias may have left some paperwork lying around."
L["Elling Trias, back at the cheese shop in Stormwind's Trade District, has read the Defias docket and picked your next stop before putting it down: Dashel Stonefist, in Old Town."] = "Elling Trias, back at the cheese shop in Stormwind's Trade District, has read the Defias docket and picked your next stop before putting it down: Dashel Stonefist, in Old Town."
L["Mikhail, at the Deepwater Tavern in Menethil Harbor, has had an eye on a patron called Tapoke Jahn. Slim, to his friends. Slim takes a great interest in other people's business, and Mikhail thinks it's time someone returned the favor."] = "Mikhail, at the Deepwater Tavern in Menethil Harbor, has had an eye on a patron called Tapoke Jahn. Slim, to his friends. Slim takes a great interest in other people's business, and Mikhail thinks it's time someone returned the favor."
L["Commander Samaul, at Foothold Citadel in Theramore, has heard what brought you all this way. The commander sends you to Sentry Point, in Dustwallow Marsh, to look for any sign of a Private Hendel."] = "Commander Samaul, at Foothold Citadel in Theramore, has heard what brought you all this way. The commander sends you to Sentry Point, in Dustwallow Marsh, to look for any sign of a Private Hendel."
L["At the Scarab Wall, Anachronos shows the old war and names the broken road a scepter bearer must follow."] = "At the Scarab Wall, Anachronos shows the old war and names the broken road a scepter bearer must follow."
L["Azuregos sends you to Narain Soothfancy, whose ledger runs through goggles, chops, ransom notes, Draconic pages, and the sea."] = "Azuregos sends you to Narain Soothfancy, whose ledger runs through goggles, chops, ransom notes, Draconic pages, and the sea."
L["Baristolth sends you to Anachronos and then to Blackwing Lair. Broodlord Lashlayer's head begins the work of earning Nozdormu's trust."] = "Baristolth sends you to Anachronos and then to Blackwing Lair. Broodlord Lashlayer's head begins the work of earning Nozdormu's trust."
L["Black dragonkin are gathering in the Burning Steppes, uncomfortably close to Morgan's Vigil. Helendis Riverhorn at the Vigil wants their numbers thinned, from the smallest broodling right up to a drake."] = "Black dragonkin are gathering in the Burning Steppes, uncomfortably close to Morgan's Vigil. Helendis Riverhorn at the Vigil wants their numbers thinned, from the smallest broodling right up to a drake."
L["Ragged John's tale ended with Marshal Windsor dragged off to a prison. Marshal Maxwell at Morgan's Vigil wants to know what became of him, and the trail leads into Blackrock Depths, under Blackrock Mountain."] = "Ragged John's tale ended with Marshal Windsor dragged off to a prison. Marshal Maxwell at Morgan's Vigil wants to know what became of him, and the trail leads into Blackrock Depths, under Blackrock Mountain."
L["Marshal Maxwell at Morgan's Vigil has word from Marshal Windsor: he's bound for Stormwind and wants you there when he arrives. Go to the city gates and find Squire Rowe, who will let Windsor know you've come."] = "Marshal Maxwell at Morgan's Vigil has word from Marshal Windsor: he's bound for Stormwind and wants you there when he arrives. Go to the city gates and find Squire Rowe, who will let Windsor know you've come."
L["Highlord Bolvar Fordragon in Stormwind Keep hands you a fragment of the Dragon's Eye. Someone out there can restore its power. That is the whole of what anyone knows about them, so the search may take a while."] = "Highlord Bolvar Fordragon in Stormwind Keep hands you a fragment of the Dragon's Eye. Someone out there can restore its power. That is the whole of what anyone knows about them, so the search may take a while."
L["Warlord Goretooth in Kargath, out in the Badlands, has three names in Lower Blackrock Spire and wants every one of them dead. Whatever papers they're keeping, the warlord wants those brought back too."] = "Warlord Goretooth in Kargath, out in the Badlands, has three names in Lower Blackrock Spire and wants every one of them dead. Whatever papers they're keeping, the warlord wants those brought back too."
L["Thrall, in Grommash Hold, wants you to find Rexxar, the Champion of the Horde. Rexxar walks the paths of Desolace somewhere between Stonetalon and Feralas and doesn't wait for visitors. Expect to walk."] = "Thrall, in Grommash Hold, wants you to find Rexxar, the Champion of the Horde. Rexxar walks the paths of Desolace somewhere between Stonetalon and Feralas and doesn't wait for visitors. Expect to walk."
L["Emberstrife, in a den deep in the Wyrmbog of Dustwallow Marsh, won't take you on faith. The test is skulls: one from a champion of each of the blue, green, bronze and red dragonflights."] = "Emberstrife, in a den deep in the Wyrmbog of Dustwallow Marsh, won't take you on faith. The test is skulls: one from a champion of each of the blue, green, bronze and red dragonflights."
L["Rexxar, back on the roads of Desolace, knows what the amulet still lacks: the blood of General Drakkisath, champion of the black dragonflight. The general keeps his throne at the top of Blackrock Spire, and a great many guards around it."] = "Rexxar, back on the roads of Desolace, knows what the amulet still lacks: the blood of General Drakkisath, champion of the black dragonflight. The general keeps his throne at the top of Blackrock Spire, and a great many guards around it."
L["In Deathknell, Aramis Hammerhand teaches what the Light still asks of a paladin, and Aramis isn't one for long lectures. The Deathguard nearby are hurt, the Light heals, and you know the words. Start there."] = "In Deathknell, Aramis Hammerhand teaches what the Light still asks of a paladin, and Aramis isn't one for long lectures. The Deathguard nearby are hurt, the Light heals, and you know the words. Start there."
L["Shari Stilwell in Brill has directions for you: head west until Tirisfal runs out, and look for Bandarion Keep. Paladins are making a home there. Report to Breton Samuels when you arrive."] = "Shari Stilwell in Brill has directions for you: head west until Tirisfal runs out, and look for Bandarion Keep. Paladins are making a home there. Report to Breton Samuels when you arrive."
L["Danitha Morr has a lesson for you about doing good for its own sake, with nobody watching and nothing to gain. It begins in the Undercity, where Tanis Alderwood could use a hand."] = "Danitha Morr has a lesson for you about doing good for its own sake, with nobody watching and nothing to gain. It begins in the Undercity, where Tanis Alderwood could use a hand."
L["Danitha Morr sees a chance for Bandarion Keep to prove its worth to the Horde: two Earthen Ring travelers have gone missing in Silverpine Forest. Go to the Sepulcher and ask Trevan Rol what's known."] = "Danitha Morr sees a chance for Bandarion Keep to prove its worth to the Horde: two Earthen Ring travelers have gone missing in Silverpine Forest. Go to the Sepulcher and ask Trevan Rol what's known."
L["Duthorian Rall at Stormwind's Cathedral of Light has a tome for you, and a question to go with it: when evil walks into your lands, will you be strong enough? Rall means to find out."] = "Duthorian Rall at Stormwind's Cathedral of Light has a tome for you, and a question to go with it: when evil walks into your lands, will you be strong enough? Rall means to find out."
L["Your trainer has another test for you, and it waits at Jordan Stilwell's forge, just outside the gates of Ironforge. Jordan needs a paladin who doesn't mind a long road and a heavy pack."] = "Your trainer has another test for you, and it waits at Jordan Stilwell's forge, just outside the gates of Ironforge. Jordan needs a paladin who doesn't mind a long road and a heavy pack."
L["A summons from Danitha Morr: come home to Bandarion Keep. You've covered a lot of ground in the Light's service by now, and Danitha has noticed. So, it seems, have your boots."] = "A summons from Danitha Morr: come home to Bandarion Keep. You've covered a lot of ground in the Light's service by now, and Danitha has noticed. So, it seems, have your boots."
L["Your trainer sends you to Lord Grayson Shadowbreaker in Stormwind's Cathedral District. Grayson keeps an old rite for paladins who have carried the Light a long way. It asks a great deal, and Grayson will want to see you're ready."] = "Your trainer sends you to Lord Grayson Shadowbreaker in Stormwind's Cathedral District. Grayson keeps an old rite for paladins who have carried the Light a long way. It asks a great deal, and Grayson will want to see you're ready."
L["Danitha Morr has sent for you again. Your warhorse has carried you a long way, but the order keeps an older rite for paladins who have proven themselves, and Danitha thinks it's your turn. Bandarion Keep, then."] = "Danitha Morr has sent for you again. Your warhorse has carried you a long way, but the order keeps an older rite for paladins who have proven themselves, and Danitha thinks it's your turn. Bandarion Keep, then."
L["Yenniku, youngest son of the Darkspear chief, has been taken somewhere in Stranglethorn. Nimboya at Grom'gol Base Camp is searching for him, and the Bloodscalp trolls in the ruins north of camp are the only trail there is."] = "Yenniku, youngest son of the Darkspear chief, has been taken somewhere in Stranglethorn. Nimboya at Grom'gol Base Camp is searching for him, and the Bloodscalp trolls in the ruins north of camp are the only trail there is."
L["The heads of the two Bloodscalp leaders sit in the cauldron at Grom'gol, and death hasn't stopped them talking. It hasn't made them generous either. Before they say a word about Yenniku, they want paying."] = "The heads of the two Bloodscalp leaders sit in the cauldron at Grom'gol, and death hasn't stopped them talking. It hasn't made them generous either. Before they say a word about Yenniku, they want paying."
L["Nezzliok's head has one last thing to say about the fate of Yenniku. After that it's over to Kin'weelay, who stands beside the cauldron at Grom'gol and doesn't seem the least bit bothered by talking heads."] = "Nezzliok's head has one last thing to say about the fate of Yenniku. After that it's over to Kin'weelay, who stands beside the cauldron at Grom'gol and doesn't seem the least bit bothered by talking heads."
L["Kin'weelay's Soul Gem is ready, and it's no good to anyone sitting in your pack. Yenniku is somewhere out in the jungle, in Zanzil's hold. Nimboya waits at Grom'gol for whatever you bring back."] = "Kin'weelay's Soul Gem is ready, and it's no good to anyone sitting in your pack. Yenniku is somewhere out in the jungle, in Zanzil's hold. Nimboya waits at Grom'gol for whatever you bring back."
L["Everybody has a bit of old dungeon armor that's seen better days. I took my bracers to Deliana on the High Seat in Ironforge, and Deliana said they could be a great deal better. All it would cost was the bracers, twenty gold, and fifteen blood samples out of Winterspring.\n\nI paid, and they came back better. Fair's fair. But Deliana wasn't done with me. There was a sealed container of blood that needed to go to a goblin called Mux Manascrambler, all the way down in Gadgetzan, and somehow I was already the one carrying it.\n\nAn earnest proposition, they call this sort of thing. In my experience, earnest means long."] = "Everybody has a bit of old dungeon armor that's seen better days. I took my bracers to Deliana on the High Seat in Ironforge, and Deliana said they could be a great deal better. All it would cost was the bracers, twenty gold, and fifteen blood samples out of Winterspring.\n\nI paid, and they came back better. Fair's fair. But Deliana wasn't done with me. There was a sealed container of blood that needed to go to a goblin called Mux Manascrambler, all the way down in Gadgetzan, and somehow I was already the one carrying it.\n\nAn earnest proposition, they call this sort of thing. In my experience, earnest means long."
L["Everybody has a bit of old dungeon armor that's seen better days. I took my bracers to Mokvar in Orgrimmar, and Mokvar said they could be a great deal better. All it would cost was the bracers, twenty gold, and fifteen venom samples out of Silithus, which are about as much fun to collect as they sound.\n\nI paid, and they came back better. Fair's fair. But Mokvar wasn't done with me. There was a sealed container of venom that needed to go to a goblin called Mux Manascrambler, down in Gadgetzan, and somehow I was already the one carrying it.\n\nAn earnest proposition, they call this sort of thing. In my experience, earnest means long."] = "Everybody has a bit of old dungeon armor that's seen better days. I took my bracers to Mokvar in Orgrimmar, and Mokvar said they could be a great deal better. All it would cost was the bracers, twenty gold, and fifteen venom samples out of Silithus, which are about as much fun to collect as they sound.\n\nI paid, and they came back better. Fair's fair. But Mokvar wasn't done with me. There was a sealed container of venom that needed to go to a goblin called Mux Manascrambler, down in Gadgetzan, and somehow I was already the one carrying it.\n\nAn earnest proposition, they call this sort of thing. In my experience, earnest means long."
L["Mux Manascrambler is a goblin with a plan, and a goblin's plan always starts with your money. Forty gold and a cartload of reagents bought me the Ectoplasmic Distiller, and then I had to go out and use it on ghosts in three different corners of the world. Ghosts do not enjoy being distilled.\n\nThe best part was the shifty merchant: an imp in a cave in Winterspring, selling Fel Elemental Rods. Doing business with an imp is exactly as much fun as it sounds. I counted my fingers afterward.\n\nOut of all of it came the Extra-Dimensional Ghost Revealer. I carried it home, and my belt and gloves got the same treatment the bracers had. Just compensation, they called it. I'd call it a start."] = "Mux Manascrambler is a goblin with a plan, and a goblin's plan always starts with your money. Forty gold and a cartload of reagents bought me the Ectoplasmic Distiller, and then I had to go out and use it on ghosts in three different corners of the world. Ghosts do not enjoy being distilled.\n\nThe best part was the shifty merchant: an imp in a cave in Winterspring, selling Fel Elemental Rods. Doing business with an imp is exactly as much fun as it sounds. I counted my fingers afterward.\n\nOut of all of it came the Extra-Dimensional Ghost Revealer. I carried it home, and my belt and gloves got the same treatment the bracers had. Just compensation, they called it. I'd call it a start."
L["The Ghost Revealer, it turns out, works. I switched it on at the gates of Stratholme, and there stood Anthion Harmon, dead and waiting, with a plea. Baron Rivendare had Ysida Harmon somewhere inside, and time was short.\n\nSo we ran Stratholme. Not walked. Ran, through the whole rotten city to the Baron himself, and we got there in time. Ysida gave me a locket to carry out, so Anthion would know it was true.\n\nThe dead don't stay sentimental for long when there's work left. Next thing I knew, Anthion had me fetching iron, leather and cloth for a banner, and the unfinished banner was going to an old friend of his in Dire Maul. These jobs never get shorter."] = "The Ghost Revealer, it turns out, works. I switched it on at the gates of Stratholme, and there stood Anthion Harmon, dead and waiting, with a plea. Baron Rivendare had Ysida Harmon somewhere inside, and time was short.\n\nSo we ran Stratholme. Not walked. Ran, through the whole rotten city to the Baron himself, and we got there in time. Ysida gave me a locket to carry out, so Anthion would know it was true.\n\nThe dead don't stay sentimental for long when there's work left. Next thing I knew, Anthion had me fetching iron, leather and cloth for a banner, and the unfinished banner was going to an old friend of his in Dire Maul. These jobs never get shorter."
L["Anthion's old friend was Falrin Treeshaper, in the library at Dire Maul. Falrin looked at the unfinished banner and agreed to help, right after a small matter of ogres. Falrin has a vendetta. I never got the whole story. I got a number, twenty-five, and that's how many warbeads I took off ogres before Falrin was satisfied.\n\nAn enchantment and some more shopping later, I was holding the Banner of Provocation, which is exactly what it sounds like. Plant it in the right place and somebody comes looking for a fight.\n\nFalrin even had the place picked out: the Ring of Law, in Blackrock Depths. Some people send flowers."] = "Anthion's old friend was Falrin Treeshaper, in the library at Dire Maul. Falrin looked at the unfinished banner and agreed to help, right after a small matter of ogres. Falrin has a vendetta. I never got the whole story. I got a number, twenty-five, and that's how many warbeads I took off ogres before Falrin was satisfied.\n\nAn enchantment and some more shopping later, I was holding the Banner of Provocation, which is exactly what it sounds like. Plant it in the right place and somebody comes looking for a fight.\n\nFalrin even had the place picked out: the Ring of Law, in Blackrock Depths. Some people send flowers."
L["Here's how you pick a fight in the Ring of Law on purpose. You walk into Blackrock Depths, let the Dark Irons sentence you, and while they're at it you plant the Banner of Provocation in the middle of the ring.\n\nTheldren took the hint. Theldren and his whole team of gladiators, all at once, while the crowd cheered for anybody but me. We beat them anyway. Theldren had been carrying the top piece of an amulet that once belonged to a Lord Valthalak. That name would come up again. Often.\n\nAnthion had a few parting words for me after that, and then it was home, where my boots, trousers and mantle got their turn."] = "Here's how you pick a fight in the Ring of Law on purpose. You walk into Blackrock Depths, let the Dark Irons sentence you, and while they're at it you plant the Banner of Provocation in the middle of the ring.\n\nTheldren took the hint. Theldren and his whole team of gladiators, all at once, while the crowd cheered for anybody but me. We beat them anyway. Theldren had been carrying the top piece of an amulet that once belonged to a Lord Valthalak. That name would come up again. Often.\n\nAnthion had a few parting words for me after that, and then it was home, where my boots, trousers and mantle got their turn."
L["Back home they pointed me at Blackrock Mountain, Ghost Revealer in hand. Somewhere near the Spire, they said, I'd find somebody. I switched it on, and there was Bodley. Dead, like most of my acquaintances that season, and with a great deal to say about Lord Valthalak.\n\nWhat it came down to was fire. Three kings of flame, Bodley called them. I fetched a piece of each, and a hallowed brazier to hold them, and brought the lot back to a ghost inside a mountain.\n\nWhat came out of that was a brazier for calling up the dead. Bodley seemed to think that was good news. Bodley's standards have changed."] = "Back home they pointed me at Blackrock Mountain, Ghost Revealer in hand. Somewhere near the Spire, they said, I'd find somebody. I switched it on, and there was Bodley. Dead, like most of my acquaintances that season, and with a great deal to say about Lord Valthalak.\n\nWhat it came down to was fire. Three kings of flame, Bodley called them. I fetched a piece of each, and a hallowed brazier to hold them, and brought the lot back to a ghost inside a mountain.\n\nWhat came out of that was a brazier for calling up the dead. Bodley seemed to think that was good news. Bodley's standards have changed."
L["Theldren had given me the top of Lord Valthalak's amulet. Bodley wanted the left and the right, and there's only one way to get them. Bodley names some old relic, you fetch it, and you light the brazier in the right dungeon. Somebody long dead comes out of it, angry, carrying a piece of the amulet. You kill them again. Then you do the whole thing over for the other side.\n\nSomewhere in the middle, Bodley gave me a long look and announced, I see Alcaz Island in your future. Bodley was right. Twenty bloodkelp, off the naga out there.\n\nI never did find out what a ghost wants with twenty bloodkelp."] = "Theldren had given me the top of Lord Valthalak's amulet. Bodley wanted the left and the right, and there's only one way to get them. Bodley names some old relic, you fetch it, and you light the brazier in the right dungeon. Somebody long dead comes out of it, angry, carrying a piece of the amulet. You kill them again. Then you do the whole thing over for the other side.\n\nSomewhere in the middle, Bodley gave me a long look and announced, I see Alcaz Island in your future. Bodley was right. Twenty bloodkelp, off the naga out there.\n\nI never did find out what a ghost wants with twenty bloodkelp."
L["With the amulet whole again, Bodley wanted it used. A Flask of Supreme Power and a sack of Blackrock bracers later, I was in Upper Blackrock Spire, in the Beast's old room, lighting the brazier one last time.\n\nLord Valthalak answered. Lord Valthalak was not happy to be called. We put him down, and then came the strangest part of the whole business: I held his own amulet over his corpse, then handed it to his ghost, who sent me back to Bodley.\n\nBodley called this part Mea Culpa. I took it for an apology. Bodley's, not mine."] = "With the amulet whole again, Bodley wanted it used. A Flask of Supreme Power and a sack of Blackrock bracers later, I was in Upper Blackrock Spire, in the Beast's old room, lighting the brazier one last time.\n\nLord Valthalak answered. Lord Valthalak was not happy to be called. We put him down, and then came the strangest part of the whole business: I held his own amulet over his corpse, then handed it to his ghost, who sent me back to Bodley.\n\nBodley called this part Mea Culpa. I took it for an apology. Bodley's, not mine."
L["The last stop was the first one. I went back to where all of this started, with the cap and tunic of my old set, and handed them over. Saving the best for last, apparently.\n\nWhat came back finished the set. To anyone else it's just good armor. But I know what's in it: a goblin's ghost machine, a race through Stratholme, a brawl in the Ring of Law, and one dead lord who got his amulet back from my own hand.\n\nPeople ask where I got it. I tell them it's a long story. Then, if they're buying, I tell them this one."] = "The last stop was the first one. I went back to where all of this started, with the cap and tunic of my old set, and handed them over. Saving the best for last, apparently.\n\nWhat came back finished the set. To anyone else it's just good armor. But I know what's in it: a goblin's ghost machine, a race through Stratholme, a brawl in the Ring of Law, and one dead lord who got his amulet back from my own hand.\n\nPeople ask where I got it. I tell them it's a long story. Then, if they're buying, I tell them this one."
L["A ghost waits on the road where the Swamp of Sorrows gives way to the Blasted Lands. The Fallen Hero of the Horde, they call him, and he has a story for anyone willing to stop and listen."] = "A ghost waits on the road where the Swamp of Sorrows gives way to the Blasted Lands. The Fallen Hero of the Horde, they call him, and he has a story for anyone willing to stop and listen."
L["Ambassador Ardalan at Nethergarde Keep has an odd errand for you: go and hear out a Horde ghost. The Fallen Hero of the Horde waits on the road where the Blasted Lands meet the swamp, with a story to tell."] = "Ambassador Ardalan at Nethergarde Keep has an odd errand for you: go and hear out a Horde ghost. The Fallen Hero of the Horde waits on the road where the Blasted Lands meet the swamp, with a story to tell."
L["The Fallen Hero of the Horde isn't done talking. On his road at the edge of the Blasted Lands, he has the rest of his story to tell: what became of the soldiers who crossed over."] = "The Fallen Hero of the Horde isn't done talking. On his road at the edge of the Blasted Lands, he has the rest of his story to tell: what became of the soldiers who crossed over."
L["The Fallen Hero of the Horde has two names for you. Corporal Thund Splithoof is waiting near the road into the Blasted Lands. Lieutenant Kirith is another matter: nobody knows what became of the lieutenant."] = "The Fallen Hero of the Horde has two names for you. Corporal Thund Splithoof is waiting near the road into the Blasted Lands. Lieutenant Kirith is another matter: nobody knows what became of the lieutenant."
L["The Fallen Hero of the Horde has a name for you, and it's a long way off: Loramus Thalipedes, a demon hunter who lives by the Bay of Storms in Azshara. Loramus has a story of his own."] = "The Fallen Hero of the Horde has a name for you, and it's a long way off: Loramus Thalipedes, a demon hunter who lives by the Bay of Storms in Azshara. Loramus has a story of his own."
L["Loramus Thalipedes has packed your Azsharite into a shipment for Galvan the Ancient, in northern Stranglethorn Vale. It's most of the world away from Azshara, and Loramus has very particular plans for what's inside."] = "Loramus Thalipedes has packed your Azsharite into a shipment for Galvan the Ancient, in northern Stranglethorn Vale. It's most of the world away from Azshara, and Loramus has very particular plans for what's inside."
L["The Fallen Hero of the Horde has a use for Felbane. Razelikh the Defiler keeps three lieutenants in the Blasted Lands, each wearing a piece of a shattered amulet, and ordinary steel won't finish any of them."] = "The Fallen Hero of the Horde has a use for Felbane. Razelikh the Defiler keeps three lieutenants in the Blasted Lands, each wearing a piece of a shattered amulet, and ordinary steel won't finish any of them."
L["Deliana, in Ironforge, has an earnest proposition for anyone whose old dungeon bracers have seen better days. Bring them along, with twenty gold and fifteen blood samples from Winterspring, and hear the rest."] = "Deliana, in Ironforge, has an earnest proposition for anyone whose old dungeon bracers have seen better days. Bring them along, with twenty gold and fifteen blood samples from Winterspring, and hear the rest."
L["Mokvar, in Orgrimmar, has an earnest proposition for anyone whose old dungeon bracers have seen better days. Bring them along, with twenty gold and fifteen venom samples from Silithus, and hear the rest."] = "Mokvar, in Orgrimmar, has an earnest proposition for anyone whose old dungeon bracers have seen better days. Bring them along, with twenty gold and fifteen venom samples from Silithus, and hear the rest."
L["Mux Manascrambler, a goblin in Gadgetzan, has a supernatural device in mind. Like every goblin plan, it starts with your money: forty gold, and a pile of reagents to go with it."] = "Mux Manascrambler, a goblin in Gadgetzan, has a supernatural device in mind. Like every goblin plan, it starts with your money: forty gold, and a pile of reagents to go with it."
L["Deliana in Ironforge wants Mux's new Ghost Revealer put to work. Take it to the gates of Stratholme, switch it on, and see whether the ghost of Anthion Harmon has anything to say."] = "Deliana in Ironforge wants Mux's new Ghost Revealer put to work. Take it to the gates of Stratholme, switch it on, and see whether the ghost of Anthion Harmon has anything to say."
L["Mokvar in Orgrimmar wants Mux's new Ghost Revealer put to work. Take it to the gates of Stratholme, switch it on, and see whether the ghost of Anthion Harmon has anything to say."] = "Mokvar in Orgrimmar wants Mux's new Ghost Revealer put to work. Take it to the gates of Stratholme, switch it on, and see whether the ghost of Anthion Harmon has anything to say."
L["Anthion's unfinished banner is in the hands of Falrin Treeshaper, in the Athenaeum at Dire Maul. Before anything else, Falrin has a vendetta against the ogres to settle, and it runs to twenty-five warbeads."] = "Anthion's unfinished banner is in the hands of Falrin Treeshaper, in the Athenaeum at Dire Maul. Before anything else, Falrin has a vendetta against the ogres to settle, and it runs to twenty-five warbeads."
L["The Banner of Provocation is ready, and Falrin Treeshaper knows where it belongs: the middle of the Ring of Law in Blackrock Depths, planted while the Dark Irons sentence you. Somebody is bound to take offense."] = "The Banner of Provocation is ready, and Falrin Treeshaper knows where it belongs: the middle of the Ring of Law in Blackrock Depths, planted while the Dark Irons sentence you. Somebody is bound to take offense."
L["Deliana in Ironforge has one more name for you: Bodley. Look near Blackrock Spire, in Blackrock Mountain, with the Ghost Revealer switched on. Without it, you won't see Bodley at all."] = "Deliana in Ironforge has one more name for you: Bodley. Look near Blackrock Spire, in Blackrock Mountain, with the Ghost Revealer switched on. Without it, you won't see Bodley at all."
L["Mokvar in Orgrimmar has one more name for you: Bodley. Look near Blackrock Spire, in Blackrock Mountain, with the Ghost Revealer switched on. Without it, you won't see Bodley at all."] = "Mokvar in Orgrimmar has one more name for you: Bodley. Look near Blackrock Spire, in Blackrock Mountain, with the Ghost Revealer switched on. Without it, you won't see Bodley at all."
L["Bodley, in Blackrock Mountain, wants Lord Valthalak's amulet whole again. You have the top piece; the left and right are still missing. Bodley's plan for finding them begins, as these things do, with fetching something old."] = "Bodley, in Blackrock Mountain, wants Lord Valthalak's amulet whole again. You have the top piece; the left and right are still missing. Bodley's plan for finding them begins, as these things do, with fetching something old."
L["Lord Valthalak's amulet is whole again, and Bodley isn't finished with it. First come the final preparations: forty Blackrock bracers and a Flask of Supreme Power, carried back to Blackrock Mountain."] = "Lord Valthalak's amulet is whole again, and Bodley isn't finished with it. First come the final preparations: forty Blackrock bracers and a Flask of Supreme Power, carried back to Blackrock Mountain."
L["Back where it all began. Deliana in Ironforge is waiting for the last pieces of your old dungeon set, the cap and the tunic. After everything since the bracers, it seems a small thing to ask."] = "Back where it all began. Deliana in Ironforge is waiting for the last pieces of your old dungeon set, the cap and the tunic. After everything since the bracers, it seems a small thing to ask."
L["Back where it all began. Mokvar in Orgrimmar is waiting for the last pieces of your old dungeon set, the cap and the tunic. After everything since the bracers, it seems a small thing to ask."] = "Back where it all began. Mokvar in Orgrimmar is waiting for the last pieces of your old dungeon set, the cap and the tunic. After everything since the bracers, it seems a small thing to ask."
L["Your trainer thinks you're ready to meet the earth. The earth is patient and expects the same of you, so there will be an errand or two before it has anything to say."] = "Your trainer thinks you're ready to meet the earth. The earth is patient and expects the same of you, so there will be an errand or two before it has anything to say."
L["Your trainer says it's time to call fire. Fire is the least patient of the elements and the quickest to test you, so keep your wits about you. Your trainer will tell you who to find first."] = "Your trainer says it's time to call fire. Fire is the least patient of the elements and the quickest to test you, so keep your wits about you. Your trainer will tell you who to find first."
L["Water is next, and the way to it starts with Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan. Of the four elements, water is the patient one, and it likes its students patient too. Pack for a long trip."] = "Water is next, and the way to it starts with Norric Lochthane in Loch Modan. Of the four elements, water is the patient one, and it likes its students patient too. Pack for a long trip."
L["Your trainer says water is next and sends you to Islen Waterseer in the Barrens. Of the four elements, water is the patient one, and it likes its students patient too. Pack for a long trip."] = "Your trainer says water is next and sends you to Islen Waterseer in the Barrens. Of the four elements, water is the patient one, and it likes its students patient too. Pack for a long trip."
L["Air is the last of the four elements, and the hardest to pin down. Your road to it starts with Veyric Thunderhame in the Arathi Highlands, who has work for a shaman."] = "Air is the last of the four elements, and the hardest to pin down. Your road to it starts with Veyric Thunderhame in the Arathi Highlands, who has work for a shaman."
L["Air is the last of the four elements, and your trainer sends you to Thousand Needles to find Prate Cloudseer. Air goes where it likes and listens when it feels like it. Go and see if it feels like it."] = "Air is the last of the four elements, and your trainer sends you to Thousand Needles to find Prate Cloudseer. Air goes where it likes and listens when it feels like it. Go and see if it feels like it."
L["Kardris Dreamseeker in Orgrimmar thinks you're ready for the hardest lesson yet: all four elements at once. That lesson belongs to Bath'rah the Windwatcher in the Alterac Mountains, who will want a sample of each."] = "Kardris Dreamseeker in Orgrimmar thinks you're ready for the hardest lesson yet: all four elements at once. That lesson belongs to Bath'rah the Windwatcher in the Alterac Mountains, who will want a sample of each."
L["Eldrun Stormbreaker in Ironforge is building a scryer meant to see into the Great Ossuary beneath Scholomance. First it needs two fine diamonds. After that, it needs someone willing to take it down there."] = "Eldrun Stormbreaker in Ironforge is building a scryer meant to see into the Great Ossuary beneath Scholomance. First it needs two fine diamonds. After that, it needs someone willing to take it down there."
L["Feathermoon Stronghold is missing a courier. Raschal set out from Thalanaar and never arrived. Latronicus Moonspear has already marked him missing in action, but upstairs in the stronghold, Ginro Hearthkindle hasn't given up."] = "Feathermoon Stronghold is missing a courier. Raschal set out from Thalanaar and never arrived. Latronicus Moonspear has already marked him missing in action, but upstairs in the stronghold, Ginro Hearthkindle hasn't given up."
L["All the wreck gave up was a knife, and Ginro Hearthkindle thinks it can still tell you something. Take it to Quintis Jonespyre, up in Feathermoon's treehouse, who can read where a thing has been."] = "All the wreck gave up was a knife, and Ginro Hearthkindle thinks it can still tell you something. Take it to Quintis Jonespyre, up in Feathermoon's treehouse, who can read where a thing has been."
L["The knife points inland, to the Woodpaw gnolls of southern Feralas. Ginro Hearthkindle wants their camps searched for Raschal's belongings, or for Raschal. Gnolls are not known for returning lost property."] = "The knife points inland, to the Woodpaw gnolls of southern Feralas. Ginro Hearthkindle wants their camps searched for Raschal's belongings, or for Raschal. Gnolls are not known for returning lost property."
L["Raschal's note points south of the Woodpaw camps, to a place called the Writhing Deep. Whatever he went to look at down there, he hasn't come back from it. Go and find him, or what's left of him."] = "Raschal's note points south of the Woodpaw camps, to a place called the Writhing Deep. Whatever he went to look at down there, he hasn't come back from it. Go and find him, or what's left of him."
L["Your trainer says a hunter shouldn't hunt alone, and knows who has a taming rod for you. There are beasts to try it on, and none of them asked to be tamed. Whether any of them follows you home is up to you."] = "Your trainer says a hunter shouldn't hunt alone, and knows who has a taming rod for you. There are beasts to try it on, and none of them asked to be tamed. Whether any of them follows you home is up to you."
L["If you ever bring the Ancient Petrified Leaf out of Molten Core, know that it belongs to someone. It looks like nothing much, and it's a big world. Start your search in Felwood."] = "If you ever bring the Ancient Petrified Leaf out of Molten Core, know that it belongs to someone. It looks like nothing much, and it's a big world. Start your search in Felwood."
L["Marlene Redpath asked me to look in on her niece, Pamela, in Darrowshire. Darrowshire's over in the Eastern Plaguelands, and nobody has lived there in a long time. I went anyway.\n\nPamela was there. A little girl at her old home in the ruins, waiting for her family to come back and very worried about her doll. She was also a ghost. She didn't bring it up, so neither did I.\n\nThe doll had come apart, and the pieces were scattered about the town. I picked through Darrowshire until I had the lot, and for a minute there, with Pamela hugging that doll, I almost forgot where we were standing.\n\nThen she asked me to go and see her Auntie Marlene, and I started to understand how long she'd been waiting."] = "Marlene Redpath asked me to look in on her niece, Pamela, in Darrowshire. Darrowshire's over in the Eastern Plaguelands, and nobody has lived there in a long time. I went anyway.\n\nPamela was there. A little girl at her old home in the ruins, waiting for her family to come back and very worried about her doll. She was also a ghost. She didn't bring it up, so neither did I.\n\nThe doll had come apart, and the pieces were scattered about the town. I picked through Darrowshire until I had the lot, and for a minute there, with Pamela hugging that doll, I almost forgot where we were standing.\n\nThen she asked me to go and see her Auntie Marlene, and I started to understand how long she'd been waiting."
L["Pamela's aunt had a job waiting for me: a wedding ring, for a historian who'd been asking around about Darrowshire.\n\nThe historian was Chromie. A gnome, cheerful, and a little too well informed about things that hadn't happened yet. Chromie wanted the Annals of Darrowshire, an old history of the town that took some finding, and once she had it she added to it. Extended, she called it. I didn't ask where the extra pages came from. I'm not sure I'd have liked the answer.\n\nThe extended version went to Pamela's uncle, Carlin Redpath, at Light's Hope Chapel."] = "Pamela's aunt had a job waiting for me: a wedding ring, for a historian who'd been asking around about Darrowshire.\n\nThe historian was Chromie. A gnome, cheerful, and a little too well informed about things that hadn't happened yet. Chromie wanted the Annals of Darrowshire, an old history of the town that took some finding, and once she had it she added to it. Extended, she called it. I didn't ask where the extra pages came from. I'm not sure I'd have liked the answer.\n\nThe extended version went to Pamela's uncle, Carlin Redpath, at Light's Hope Chapel."
L["Carlin wanted Darrowshire's old defenders freed from that town, and I freed fifteen of them. The dead of Darrowshire have waited long enough for everything."] = "Carlin wanted Darrowshire's old defenders freed from that town, and I freed fifteen of them. The dead of Darrowshire have waited long enough for everything."
L["Carlin Redpath had the annals now, and Carlin wanted the last battle of Darrowshire put right. Putting it right, it turned out, takes a great many relics off the dead.\n\nA villain's skull and another villain's broken sword. A hero's libram, and the shield of Pamela's father. And five skulls that resonated, which meant sorting through a great many skulls that didn't. Most skulls, it turns out, have nothing to say.\n\nWhen it was all gathered, Carlin wrapped the lot in one bundle and sent me back to Chromie with it. Every hero and villain of that night, packed into one sack on my back. It was heavier than it looked."] = "Carlin Redpath had the annals now, and Carlin wanted the last battle of Darrowshire put right. Putting it right, it turned out, takes a great many relics off the dead.\n\nA villain's skull and another villain's broken sword. A hero's libram, and the shield of Pamela's father. And five skulls that resonated, which meant sorting through a great many skulls that didn't. Most skulls, it turns out, have nothing to say.\n\nWhen it was all gathered, Carlin wrapped the lot in one bundle and sent me back to Chromie with it. Every hero and villain of that night, packed into one sack on my back. It was heavier than it looked."
L["Chromie sent me back to Darrowshire with the bundle. I set it down in the town square, and the night Darrowshire fell started again all around me. The dead got up. Both sides.\n\nMy job was to keep the right people standing long enough, and mostly that meant Captain Redpath, Pamela's father, who had to hold on until his own corrupted self came to meet him. Then it did. I put it down, and the captain's spirit came walking into the square, himself again.\n\nAfter that I went to see Pamela at her home. For once, somebody had good news for her: her father had come home.\n\nShe gave me the key to his old chest by way of thanks. There was treasure in it. Some stories end well, and I'm not about to argue."] = "Chromie sent me back to Darrowshire with the bundle. I set it down in the town square, and the night Darrowshire fell started again all around me. The dead got up. Both sides.\n\nMy job was to keep the right people standing long enough, and mostly that meant Captain Redpath, Pamela's father, who had to hold on until his own corrupted self came to meet him. Then it did. I put it down, and the captain's spirit came walking into the square, himself again.\n\nAfter that I went to see Pamela at her home. For once, somebody had good news for her: her father had come home.\n\nShe gave me the key to his old chest by way of thanks. There was treasure in it. Some stories end well, and I'm not about to argue."
L["Sentinel Elissa Starbreeze in Auberdine sent a scout, Balthule Shadowstrike, to watch some strange goings-on at the Tower of Althalaxx to the northeast. Balthule should have been back by now. Elissa would like someone to go and look."] = "Sentinel Elissa Starbreeze in Auberdine sent a scout, Balthule Shadowstrike, to watch some strange goings-on at the Tower of Althalaxx to the northeast. Balthule should have been back by now. Elissa would like someone to go and look."
L["Delgren the Purifier, at Maestra's Post in Ashenvale, has read Balthule's letter and calls it dire. The Dark Strand has cultists at Ordil'Aran, and one of them carries a soul gem Delgren would very much like to study."] = "Delgren the Purifier, at Maestra's Post in Ashenvale, has read Balthule's letter and calls it dire. The Dark Strand has cultists at Ordil'Aran, and one of them carries a soul gem Delgren would very much like to study."
L["Balthule Shadowstrike, back at the Tower of Althalaxx, says the time to strike is now. Athrikus Narassin is at the top of the tower, and the warlocks around him are deadlier than any you've met so far."] = "Balthule Shadowstrike, back at the Tower of Althalaxx, says the time to strike is now. Athrikus Narassin is at the top of the tower, and the warlocks around him are deadlier than any you've met so far."
L["There's a wrecked raft in a pond in Un'Goro Crater, out in the southeastern marshes. Nobody aboard, nobody waiting nearby. Poke around in the water and you might learn whose it was."] = "There's a wrecked raft in a pond in Un'Goro Crater, out in the southeastern marshes. Nobody aboard, nobody waiting nearby. Poke around in the water and you might learn whose it was."
L["Donova Snowden at Frostfire Hot Springs in Winterspring has an idea about Linken's sword, and it needs a rare brew, the Videre Elixir. Gregan Brewspewer, in northern Feralas, knows how to come by one."] = "Donova Snowden at Frostfire Hot Springs in Winterspring has an idea about Linken's sword, and it needs a rare brew, the Videre Elixir. Gregan Brewspewer, in northern Feralas, knows how to come by one."
L["Eridan Bluewind, at the Emerald Sanctuary in southern Felwood, has the next piece of Linken's story. It starts with a silver heart, made from silvery claws and an irontree heart. Felwood's wildlife is still using both."] = "Eridan Bluewind, at the Emerald Sanctuary in southern Felwood, has the next piece of Linken's story. It starts with a silver heart, made from silvery claws and an irontree heart. Felwood's wildlife is still using both."
L["Linken, back at Marshal's Refuge, has one last job, and it's what the Silver Totem is for. On Fire Plume Ridge, Blazerunner guards a cave behind an aura nothing gets through. Nearly nothing. It's dangerous to go alone."] = "Linken, back at Marshal's Refuge, has one last job, and it's what the Silver Totem is for. On Fire Plume Ridge, Blazerunner guards a cave behind an aura nothing gets through. Nearly nothing. It's dangerous to go alone."
L["Royal Historian Archesonus, by the High Seat in Ironforge, has a long history of the Dark Iron dwarves to tell. Hear it out. King Magni has questions about his daughter, Moira, and the answers lie in Blackrock Depths."] = "Royal Historian Archesonus, by the High Seat in Ironforge, has a long history of the Dark Iron dwarves to tell. Hear it out. King Magni has questions about his daughter, Moira, and the answers lie in Blackrock Depths."
L["King Magni has heard enough. His orders from the High Seat are plain: back into Blackrock Depths, through the Dark Iron city to Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, and bring Princess Moira home."] = "King Magni has heard enough. His orders from the High Seat are plain: back into Blackrock Depths, through the Dark Iron city to Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, and bring Princess Moira home."
L["Galamav the Marksman in Kargath shows you a crude drawing of an orc, bars scrawled across the face. The orc is Commander Gor'shak, and the bars suggest a prison. Blackrock Depths has a big one."] = "Galamav the Marksman in Kargath shows you a crude drawing of an orc, bars scrawled across the face. The orc is Commander Gor'shak, and the bars suggest a prison. Blackrock Depths has a big one."
L["Your trainer says you swing well enough. Time for your first real trial. Your trainer will tell you who to see, and like most warrior business, it ends with somebody on the ground. Make sure it isn't you."] = "Your trainer says you swing well enough. Time for your first real trial. Your trainer will tell you who to see, and like most warrior business, it ends with somebody on the ground. Make sure it isn't you."
L["Your trainer sends you to Klannoc Macleod, the Islander, who runs a fighting pit on Fray Island in the Barrens. Warriors go there to find out what they're made of. Bring a good weapon and a thick skull."] = "Your trainer sends you to Klannoc Macleod, the Islander, who runs a fighting pit on Fray Island in the Barrens. Warriors go there to find out what they're made of. Bring a good weapon and a thick skull."
L["Silverpine Forest is crawling with worgen, and Dalar Dawnweaver at the Sepulcher calls it Arugal's mess. Dalar means to clean it up for the Dark Lady and needs a capable aide. First, you'll have to prove you are one."] = "Silverpine Forest is crawling with worgen, and Dalar Dawnweaver at the Sepulcher calls it Arugal's mess. Dalar means to clean it up for the Dark Lady and needs a capable aide. First, you'll have to prove you are one."
L["Shadow Priest Allister at the Sepulcher has a new worry: Dalaran. Its wizards have taken an interest in Ambermill, and their crates keep turning up at a camp north of Pyrewood Village. Allister wants to know what's inside."] = "Shadow Priest Allister at the Sepulcher has a new worry: Dalaran. Its wizards have taken an interest in Ambermill, and their crates keep turning up at a camp north of Pyrewood Village. Allister wants to know what's inside."
L["Everyone at the Sepulcher has business in Shadowfang Keep. High Executor Hadrec sent two deathstalkers in, and they haven't come back. Dalar Dawnweaver wants Arugal's head. In the Undercity's Apothecarium, Keeper Bel'dugur wants a book."] = "Everyone at the Sepulcher has business in Shadowfang Keep. High Executor Hadrec sent two deathstalkers in, and they haven't come back. Dalar Dawnweaver wants Arugal's head. In the Undercity's Apothecarium, Keeper Bel'dugur wants a book."
L["Arugal is the reason Silverpine has worgen. Dalar Dawnweaver, down at the Sepulcher, wanted proof the curse was still at work. I could have saved us both some time. Stand in that forest after dark for a minute. That's your proof.\n\nDalar wanted it done properly, though, and properly mostly meant shackles. Shackles off the gluttons, shackles off the darksouls, shackles out of Pyrewood, until I could have opened a smithy. Somewhere along the way there was a thing called the Remedy of Arugal. Judging by the forest, it remedied nothing. And there was Grimson the Pale, whose head I brought back. Paler, by then.\n\nDalar took all of it and looked pleased, in a grim sort of way. Grim is about as pleased as anyone gets in Silverpine."] = "Arugal is the reason Silverpine has worgen. Dalar Dawnweaver, down at the Sepulcher, wanted proof the curse was still at work. I could have saved us both some time. Stand in that forest after dark for a minute. That's your proof.\n\nDalar wanted it done properly, though, and properly mostly meant shackles. Shackles off the gluttons, shackles off the darksouls, shackles out of Pyrewood, until I could have opened a smithy. Somewhere along the way there was a thing called the Remedy of Arugal. Judging by the forest, it remedied nothing. And there was Grimson the Pale, whose head I brought back. Paler, by then.\n\nDalar took all of it and looked pleased, in a grim sort of way. Grim is about as pleased as anyone gets in Silverpine."
L["Shadow Priest Allister had a different worry: Dalaran. A band of their wizards had settled in at Ambermill, and they had crates moving along the road north of Pyrewood. I helped myself to one. Inside was a pendant scratched all over with runes.\n\nDalar Dawnweaver had a look and decided the wizards were digging into old magic under Silverpine. The kind Dalaran gets very excited about and everybody else comes to regret. Allister wanted more of those pendants, which meant a good few wizards who no longer needed them.\n\nAt the top of it all was the Weaver, Archmage Ataeric. I took his staff off him the direct way and handed it to Allister. Very nice staff. It seemed a shame to hand it over."] = "Shadow Priest Allister had a different worry: Dalaran. A band of their wizards had settled in at Ambermill, and they had crates moving along the road north of Pyrewood. I helped myself to one. Inside was a pendant scratched all over with runes.\n\nDalar Dawnweaver had a look and decided the wizards were digging into old magic under Silverpine. The kind Dalaran gets very excited about and everybody else comes to regret. Allister wanted more of those pendants, which meant a good few wizards who no longer needed them.\n\nAt the top of it all was the Weaver, Archmage Ataeric. I took his staff off him the direct way and handed it to Allister. Very nice staff. It seemed a shame to hand it over."
L["Everything in Silverpine ends up pointing at Shadowfang Keep sooner or later, so I stopped arguing and went in.\n\nEverybody wanted something out of there. The High Executor wanted to know what had become of two missing deathstalkers. A keeper in the Apothecarium wanted a book called the Book of Ur. And Dalar Dawnweaver wanted Arugal's head. Just the head. Dalar was very clear on that.\n\nI found the deathstalkers. Only one of them was in any shape to talk. I found the book. And I found Arugal at the top of his keep, with worgen at every turn on the way up, and he made me work for every step.\n\nThe book went to the Undercity and the head went to the Sepulcher. That's about as tidy as Silverpine ever gets."] = "Everything in Silverpine ends up pointing at Shadowfang Keep sooner or later, so I stopped arguing and went in.\n\nEverybody wanted something out of there. The High Executor wanted to know what had become of two missing deathstalkers. A keeper in the Apothecarium wanted a book called the Book of Ur. And Dalar Dawnweaver wanted Arugal's head. Just the head. Dalar was very clear on that.\n\nI found the deathstalkers. Only one of them was in any shape to talk. I found the book. And I found Arugal at the top of his keep, with worgen at every turn on the way up, and he made me work for every step.\n\nThe book went to the Undercity and the head went to the Sepulcher. That's about as tidy as Silverpine ever gets."
L["High Executor Darthalia runs Tarren Mill like a war room, because it is one. Down in Hillsbrad the humans were still farming and trading as if the Forsaken weren't sitting up the hill, and Darthalia meant to correct that.\n\nFirst orders were the northern farms, with a couple of farmers named outright. Darthalia likes names on a list. After that, the peasants. Grim work. It got done.\n\nThat was the opening move. Darthalia already had the next one picked out."] = "High Executor Darthalia runs Tarren Mill like a war room, because it is one. Down in Hillsbrad the humans were still farming and trading as if the Forsaken weren't sitting up the hill, and Darthalia meant to correct that.\n\nFirst orders were the northern farms, with a couple of farmers named outright. Darthalia likes names on a list. After that, the peasants. Grim work. It got done.\n\nThat was the opening move. Darthalia already had the next one picked out."
L["Deathguard Samsa had a sideline going too: thirty human skulls. Thirty. Samsa didn't explain, and in Tarren Mill you learn quickly which questions aren't worth the answer."] = "Deathguard Samsa had a sideline going too: thirty human skulls. Thirty. Samsa didn't explain, and in Tarren Mill you learn quickly which questions aren't worth the answer."
L["After the farms, the town. Darthalia took Hillsbrad apart like a clock: the smithy, the town hall, the mine. I walked out of the town hall with the registry under my arm and the proclamation destroyed behind me. Hard to run a town without a smith, a council or its paperwork."] = "After the farms, the town. Darthalia took Hillsbrad apart like a clock: the smithy, the town hall, the mine. I walked out of the town hall with the registry under my arm and the proclamation destroyed behind me. Hard to run a town without a smith, a council or its paperwork."
L["The best bit, though, was the wanted poster outside the inn in Tarren Mill. Four names on it: a clerk, a citizen, a miner and a farmer, every one of them marked dangerous. A farmer, I can see. A miner, sure. But I'd have paid good money to see the clerk be dangerous."] = "The best bit, though, was the wanted poster outside the inn in Tarren Mill. Four names on it: a clerk, a citizen, a miner and a farmer, every one of them marked dangerous. A farmer, I can see. A miner, sure. But I'd have paid good money to see the clerk be dangerous."
L["Dun Garok is a dwarven fort down in the south of the foothills, and it was the last thing standing between Darthalia and a finished campaign. Ten mountaineers, eight riflemen, four priests and Captain Ironhill. The riflemen were the worst of it. Dwarves can shoot, and they've had a lot of practice.\n\nOnce Ironhill fell, Darthalia sealed up a commendation and sent me down to the Undercity to hand it to Varimathras. My own praise, sealed, and not for my eyes. I carried it the whole way without peeking and handed it over with a perfectly straight face.\n\nI like to think it was flattering. I'll never know. That's the trouble with sealed letters. They're all about you, and none of them are for you."] = "Dun Garok is a dwarven fort down in the south of the foothills, and it was the last thing standing between Darthalia and a finished campaign. Ten mountaineers, eight riflemen, four priests and Captain Ironhill. The riflemen were the worst of it. Dwarves can shoot, and they've had a lot of practice.\n\nOnce Ironhill fell, Darthalia sealed up a commendation and sent me down to the Undercity to hand it to Varimathras. My own praise, sealed, and not for my eyes. I carried it the whole way without peeking and handed it over with a perfectly straight face.\n\nI like to think it was flattering. I'll never know. That's the trouble with sealed letters. They're all about you, and none of them are for you."
L["Sentinel Elissa Starbreeze in Auberdine had a scout who'd gone quiet up the coast, near an old tower called Althalaxx. Would I go and look? I went and looked.\n\nThe scout, Balthule Shadowstrike, was fine. Keeping an eye on the tower from a safe distance, which was wise, because the tower was full of warlocks. The Cult of the Dark Strand, they call themselves. Anyone who picks a name like that is hoping you'll ask about it, so I didn't. I took their papers instead. Four worn parchments, which their owners had no more use for.\n\nBalthule didn't like what was written on them. Not one bit. A letter went off to Delgren the Purifier in Ashenvale, and I went with it. Whatever the cult was up to, the tower was only the part you could see from the beach."] = "Sentinel Elissa Starbreeze in Auberdine had a scout who'd gone quiet up the coast, near an old tower called Althalaxx. Would I go and look? I went and looked.\n\nThe scout, Balthule Shadowstrike, was fine. Keeping an eye on the tower from a safe distance, which was wise, because the tower was full of warlocks. The Cult of the Dark Strand, they call themselves. Anyone who picks a name like that is hoping you'll ask about it, so I didn't. I took their papers instead. Four worn parchments, which their owners had no more use for.\n\nBalthule didn't like what was written on them. Not one bit. A letter went off to Delgren the Purifier in Ashenvale, and I went with it. Whatever the cult was up to, the tower was only the part you could see from the beach."
L["Delgren the Purifier keeps watch at Maestra's Post in Ashenvale, and Delgren read the letter and handed me a list. Soul gems, a tome somebody didn't want to part with, the usual cult leftovers.\n\nThe odd one out was the Highborne. There were souls trapped in Night Run and in Satyrnaar, bound up and waiting for somebody to come along and cut them loose. So I cut them loose. I can't tell you what a Highborne soul does with its freedom, but they didn't hang about to discuss it.\n\nEvery piece I brought back, Delgren said, weakened whatever was holding court at the top of Althalaxx. When there was nothing left to bring, it was back up to Darkshore to tell the scout the time had come."] = "Delgren the Purifier keeps watch at Maestra's Post in Ashenvale, and Delgren read the letter and handed me a list. Soul gems, a tome somebody didn't want to part with, the usual cult leftovers.\n\nThe odd one out was the Highborne. There were souls trapped in Night Run and in Satyrnaar, bound up and waiting for somebody to come along and cut them loose. So I cut them loose. I can't tell you what a Highborne soul does with its freedom, but they didn't hang about to discuss it.\n\nEvery piece I brought back, Delgren said, weakened whatever was holding court at the top of Althalaxx. When there was nothing left to bring, it was back up to Darkshore to tell the scout the time had come."
L["The Tower of Althalaxx has a great many stairs and a warlock on most of them. Balthule's orders were short: Athrikus Narassin, top of the tower, bring back the head.\n\nSo I took the stairs one at a time and the warlocks the same way. At the top was Narassin, the one who'd gathered the whole Dark Strand there in the first place. All my running about in Ashenvale had taken the edge off whatever power was behind him. There was enough edge left to keep things lively.\n\nI came down with the head and gave it to Balthule, and Balthule sent me straight back to Ashenvale to tell Delgren. That's how it works in those woods, I've learned. Nothing is really finished until somebody in another forest has heard about it."] = "The Tower of Althalaxx has a great many stairs and a warlock on most of them. Balthule's orders were short: Athrikus Narassin, top of the tower, bring back the head.\n\nSo I took the stairs one at a time and the warlocks the same way. At the top was Narassin, the one who'd gathered the whole Dark Strand there in the first place. All my running about in Ashenvale had taken the edge off whatever power was behind him. There was enough edge left to keep things lively.\n\nI came down with the head and gave it to Balthule, and Balthule sent me straight back to Ashenvale to tell Delgren. That's how it works in those woods, I've learned. Nothing is really finished until somebody in another forest has heard about it."
L["Morgan's Vigil had a dragonkin problem, and for about an afternoon I was the solution. Then the letters started. The Vigil wrote to the magistrate in Lakeshire, the magistrate wrote to Stormwind, and I carried every one of them up and down the kingdom like a well-armed pigeon.\n\nIn the keep, Lady Katrana Prestor had a little advice for me before the Highlord gave his answer. The answer was no. A very polite no, on very good paper. The magistrate read it twice and stopped being polite himself.\n\nWhich is how I ended up in a cave up north of the Ruins of Thaurissan, listening to a fellow called Ragged John tell me what had become of a marshal named Windsor. The ragged part, I'll say, was earned."] = "Morgan's Vigil had a dragonkin problem, and for about an afternoon I was the solution. Then the letters started. The Vigil wrote to the magistrate in Lakeshire, the magistrate wrote to Stormwind, and I carried every one of them up and down the kingdom like a well-armed pigeon.\n\nIn the keep, Lady Katrana Prestor had a little advice for me before the Highlord gave his answer. The answer was no. A very polite no, on very good paper. The magistrate read it twice and stopped being polite himself.\n\nWhich is how I ended up in a cave up north of the Ruins of Thaurissan, listening to a fellow called Ragged John tell me what had become of a marshal named Windsor. The ragged part, I'll say, was earned."
L["Ragged John's tale ended in a Dark Iron prison, so down into Blackrock Depths I went, and there was Marshal Windsor in a cell, finished. They'd taken his proof, and with it whatever had been holding him up. He sent me back to give Maxwell the bad news.\n\nHe'd have stayed finished, too, if a crumpled note hadn't turned up in the Depths. One look at it and the marshal was back. His papers were with a golem lord and a general down there, and neither of them wanted to give anything back. I didn't take it personally.\n\nOnce Windsor had his papers, he wanted his gear and his friends out of their cells, in that order. Blackrock Depths had opinions about that. We got both anyway."] = "Ragged John's tale ended in a Dark Iron prison, so down into Blackrock Depths I went, and there was Marshal Windsor in a cell, finished. They'd taken his proof, and with it whatever had been holding him up. He sent me back to give Maxwell the bad news.\n\nHe'd have stayed finished, too, if a crumpled note hadn't turned up in the Depths. One look at it and the marshal was back. His papers were with a golem lord and a general down there, and neither of them wanted to give anything back. I didn't take it personally.\n\nOnce Windsor had his papers, he wanted his gear and his friends out of their cells, in that order. Blackrock Depths had opinions about that. We got both anyway."
L["A squire at the gates of Stormwind had one job that day: let Marshal Windsor know I'd come. Then Windsor arrived, and we walked into the city together, and I kept him alive all the way up to the keep. That took some doing.\n\nIn the throne room he faced Lady Katrana Prestor, the most trusted voice at court, and called her by her real name. Onyxia. The whole court got a good look at what had been whispering in the Highlord's ear all this time.\n\nShe killed him for it, right there. But the secret was out, and there's no spell in the world that puts that back.\n\nI never got to buy him a drink. So this one's his."] = "A squire at the gates of Stormwind had one job that day: let Marshal Windsor know I'd come. Then Windsor arrived, and we walked into the city together, and I kept him alive all the way up to the keep. That took some doing.\n\nIn the throne room he faced Lady Katrana Prestor, the most trusted voice at court, and called her by her real name. Onyxia. The whole court got a good look at what had been whispering in the Highlord's ear all this time.\n\nShe killed him for it, right there. But the secret was out, and there's no spell in the world that puts that back.\n\nI never got to buy him a drink. So this one's his."
L["Afterward the Highlord handed me a fragment of something called the Dragon's Eye and told me to find someone who could restore its power. Who? Nobody knew. Where? Nobody knew that either. All anyone could say for certain was that such a someone existed. I've had better directions from drunks.\n\nThe someone turned out to be Haleh, up at Mazthoril in Winterspring, and Haleh's price for the Drakefire Amulet was the blood of the black dragonflight's champion. General Drakkisath, on his throne at the top of Blackrock Spire.\n\nI went up with friends. Drakkisath had a great many guards and a bad temper, and by the end a lot less blood. Haleh made the amulet. Onyxia keeps a lair down in Dustwallow Marsh, and now I can let myself in."] = "Afterward the Highlord handed me a fragment of something called the Dragon's Eye and told me to find someone who could restore its power. Who? Nobody knew. Where? Nobody knew that either. All anyone could say for certain was that such a someone existed. I've had better directions from drunks.\n\nThe someone turned out to be Haleh, up at Mazthoril in Winterspring, and Haleh's price for the Drakefire Amulet was the blood of the black dragonflight's champion. General Drakkisath, on his throne at the top of Blackrock Spire.\n\nI went up with friends. Drakkisath had a great many guards and a bad temper, and by the end a lot less blood. Haleh made the amulet. Onyxia keeps a lair down in Dustwallow Marsh, and now I can let myself in."
L["Warlord Goretooth in Kargath doesn't give orders so much as read out a list. Three names in Lower Blackrock Spire, all to be dead, and whatever papers they were sitting on brought back. What was in those papers got me sent straight to Orgrimmar.\n\nThe Warchief's answer was short. Go to Blackrock Spire. Kill Rend Blackhand. Bring back his head. Rend called himself a warchief, and Orgrimmar only has room for one.\n\nSo I went, and Rend's head came home with me. Then Thrall sat me down and told me what the wind had been carrying, and none of it was good. Dragons, mostly. Black ones."] = "Warlord Goretooth in Kargath doesn't give orders so much as read out a list. Three names in Lower Blackrock Spire, all to be dead, and whatever papers they were sitting on brought back. What was in those papers got me sent straight to Orgrimmar.\n\nThe Warchief's answer was short. Go to Blackrock Spire. Kill Rend Blackhand. Bring back his head. Rend called himself a warchief, and Orgrimmar only has room for one.\n\nSo I went, and Rend's head came home with me. Then Thrall sat me down and told me what the wind had been carrying, and none of it was good. Dragons, mostly. Black ones."
L["Finding Rexxar is a job in itself. He walks the roads of Desolace somewhere between Stonetalon and Feralas, which is a great deal of road. I walked most of it before I caught him walking the rest.\n\nHe sent me with his testament to Myranda the Hag in the Western Plaguelands. Myranda deals in deception, and deception has ingredients. This batch needed twenty eyes off the black dragonspawn in Blackrock Spire. I didn't ask why eyes. Myranda didn't say.\n\nWhat came back was an amulet. Wear it into Emberstrife's den in Dustwallow Marsh, and a black dragon looks at you and sees family. I stood there in somebody else's scales making polite conversation with a dragon. Still one of my better performances."] = "Finding Rexxar is a job in itself. He walks the roads of Desolace somewhere between Stonetalon and Feralas, which is a great deal of road. I walked most of it before I caught him walking the rest.\n\nHe sent me with his testament to Myranda the Hag in the Western Plaguelands. Myranda deals in deception, and deception has ingredients. This batch needed twenty eyes off the black dragonspawn in Blackrock Spire. I didn't ask why eyes. Myranda didn't say.\n\nWhat came back was an amulet. Wear it into Emberstrife's den in Dustwallow Marsh, and a black dragon looks at you and sees family. I stood there in somebody else's scales making polite conversation with a dragon. Still one of my better performances."
L["Emberstrife wanted proof, and dragons keep their proof in skulls. Four of them, one from the champion of each of the other flights: blue up in Winterspring, green in the Swamp of Sorrows, red on the road to Grim Batol, and bronze guarding the Caverns of Time. That's a lot of map for one shopping list, and not one of them went down easy.\n\nI hauled the lot back to the Wyrmbog, still wearing my borrowed face, and came away with a dull old amulet and one large problem. The next step was inside Blackrock Spire, and the disguise wouldn't hold up in there.\n\nThe charade was over. So I went to find Rexxar again, which, as you'll remember, takes a while."] = "Emberstrife wanted proof, and dragons keep their proof in skulls. Four of them, one from the champion of each of the other flights: blue up in Winterspring, green in the Swamp of Sorrows, red on the road to Grim Batol, and bronze guarding the Caverns of Time. That's a lot of map for one shopping list, and not one of them went down easy.\n\nI hauled the lot back to the Wyrmbog, still wearing my borrowed face, and came away with a dull old amulet and one large problem. The next step was inside Blackrock Spire, and the disguise wouldn't hold up in there.\n\nThe charade was over. So I went to find Rexxar again, which, as you'll remember, takes a while."
L["Rexxar took one look at the dull amulet and knew what it needed: blood. General Drakkisath's, to be exact, champion of the black dragonflight, who keeps his throne at the very top of Blackrock Spire.\n\nNo disguise this time. We went up the hard way, through everything the Spire had, and into the throne room behind the Halls of Ascension. Drakkisath did not want to part with any of his blood. By the end he'd parted with all of it.\n\nRexxar did the rest and handed me the Drakefire Amulet. Four skulls, twenty eyes, one borrowed face and a dead general went into that thing. If Onyxia ever wonders who's knocking, it's me."] = "Rexxar took one look at the dull amulet and knew what it needed: blood. General Drakkisath's, to be exact, champion of the black dragonflight, who keeps his throne at the very top of Blackrock Spire.\n\nNo disguise this time. We went up the hard way, through everything the Spire had, and into the throne room behind the Halls of Ascension. Drakkisath did not want to part with any of his blood. By the end he'd parted with all of it.\n\nRexxar did the rest and handed me the Drakefire Amulet. Four skulls, twenty eyes, one borrowed face and a dead general went into that thing. If Onyxia ever wonders who's knocking, it's me."
L["The Eye of Divinity came out of Molten Core and into my hands, and it led me to Eris Havenfire in the Eastern Plaguelands. Eris had a test in mind.\n\nPeasants. A whole stream of them, running for their lives with the Scourge behind them. My task was to get fifty through alive. If fifteen fell first, I'd failed. A post nearby keeps count of the dead, and you learn quickly not to look at it.\n\nYou learn other things, too. That frightened people run straight into trouble. That you can't heal everyone at once, so you choose, and keep choosing. That shadow is very good at clearing a path, and the Light is for whoever's on it.\n\nFifty made it. Eris gave me what I'd earned, and with the Eye it became Benediction. I never learned a single one of their names. I remember a good many faces."] = "The Eye of Divinity came out of Molten Core and into my hands, and it led me to Eris Havenfire in the Eastern Plaguelands. Eris had a test in mind.\n\nPeasants. A whole stream of them, running for their lives with the Scourge behind them. My task was to get fifty through alive. If fifteen fell first, I'd failed. A post nearby keeps count of the dead, and you learn quickly not to look at it.\n\nYou learn other things, too. That frightened people run straight into trouble. That you can't heal everyone at once, so you choose, and keep choosing. That shadow is very good at clearing a path, and the Light is for whoever's on it.\n\nFifty made it. Eris gave me what I'd earned, and with the Eye it became Benediction. I never learned a single one of their names. I remember a good many faces."
L["When the battle of Darrowshire began, Marlene Redpath hid her niece Pamela, and never learned what became of her. Marlene, at Sorrow Hill in the Western Plaguelands, wants someone to go and look. So does Pamela's sister Jessica, in Everlook."] = "When the battle of Darrowshire began, Marlene Redpath hid her niece Pamela, and never learned what became of her. Marlene, at Sorrow Hill in the Western Plaguelands, wants someone to go and look. So does Pamela's sister Jessica, in Everlook."
L["In Darrowshire, Pamela Redpath is lonely. Auntie Marlene told her to stay put and never came back, and her father hasn't come home either. Maybe Marlene knows where he is. She lives on a hill south of Andorhal."] = "In Darrowshire, Pamela Redpath is lonely. Auntie Marlene told her to stay put and never came back, and her father hasn't come home either. Maybe Marlene knows where he is. She lives on a hill south of Andorhal."
L["Carlin Redpath, at Light's Hope Chapel, has read the strange new pages at the end of the Annals of Darrowshire and wants the truth of them. The annals also say where the heroes and villains of that last battle left their relics."] = "Carlin Redpath, at Light's Hope Chapel, has read the strange new pages at the end of the Annals of Darrowshire and wants the truth of them. The annals also say where the heroes and villains of that last battle left their relics."
L["Chromie, at the ruined inn in Andorhal, has bound the relics you gathered into one powerful spell and believes Joseph Redpath can still be saved. The bundle has to go to Darrowshire's town square, and you're the one carrying it."] = "Chromie, at the ruined inn in Andorhal, has bound the relics you gathered into one powerful spell and believes Joseph Redpath can still be saved. The bundle has to go to Darrowshire's town square, and you're the one carrying it."
L["Master Mathias Shaw at SI:7 has a job for you in Westfall. Find Agent Kearnen out there for a briefing. Kearnen has been watching the Defias, and Shaw wants to see how you handle yourself."] = "Master Mathias Shaw at SI:7 has a job for you in Westfall. Find Agent Kearnen out there for a briefing. Kearnen has been watching the Defias, and Shaw wants to see how you handle yourself."
L["Shenthul, down in the Cleft of Shadow in Orgrimmar, has Shattered Hand work for a rogue. Before anything else, you learn the salute. The Shattered Hand likes its business quiet and its greetings complicated."] = "Shenthul, down in the Cleft of Shadow in Orgrimmar, has Shattered Hand work for a rogue. Before anything else, you learn the salute. The Shattered Hand likes its business quiet and its greetings complicated."
L["A letter turns up in your mailbox. No name. Just a cabin outside Pyrewood Village and one letter at the bottom: C. Somebody wants a rogue who works alone."] = "A letter turns up in your mailbox. No name. Just a cabin outside Pyrewood Village and one letter at the bottom: C. Somebody wants a rogue who works alone."
L["Your trainer slips you a seal with a raven on it. Take it to Fahrad at Ravenholdt Manor, hidden in the hills of Hillsbrad. Be ready: your cunning gets tested on the way."] = "Your trainer slips you a seal with a raven on it. Take it to Fahrad at Ravenholdt Manor, hidden in the hills of Hillsbrad. Be ready: your cunning gets tested on the way."
L["Your trainer slips you a seal with a raven on it, for Fahrad at Ravenholdt Manor in the hills of Hillsbrad. Mind the test on the way in. Inside, Myrokos Silentform has a quarrel with the Syndicate, and work."] = "Your trainer slips you a seal with a raven on it, for Fahrad at Ravenholdt Manor in the hills of Hillsbrad. Mind the test on the way in. Inside, Myrokos Silentform has a quarrel with the Syndicate, and work."
L["A letter from Ravenholdt this time. Fahrad wants a word about the jobs you've been taking. He doesn't say why."] = "A letter from Ravenholdt this time. Fahrad wants a word about the jobs you've been taking. He doesn't say why."
L["Earth is the first element a shaman meets, and the slowest to say anything. I respect that. I don't always enjoy it.\n\nAfter a small errand to prove I'd do as I was asked, I was handed the Earth Sapta and sent to a holy place to drink it. The world goes very quiet when you do. And then the earth was there in front of me, a spirit of it made small enough to talk to.\n\nIt didn't give a speech. It gave me a rock. A piece of rough quartz, plain as you like, and I carried it home as though it might break.\n\nFrom that rock came my first totem. I set it in the ground, and the ground answered. I've argued with the earth plenty since, mostly about how long things take. It has never once failed to show up."] = "Earth is the first element a shaman meets, and the slowest to say anything. I respect that. I don't always enjoy it.\n\nAfter a small errand to prove I'd do as I was asked, I was handed the Earth Sapta and sent to a holy place to drink it. The world goes very quiet when you do. And then the earth was there in front of me, a spirit of it made small enough to talk to.\n\nIt didn't give a speech. It gave me a rock. A piece of rough quartz, plain as you like, and I carried it home as though it might break.\n\nFrom that rock came my first totem. I set it in the ground, and the ground answered. I've argued with the earth plenty since, mostly about how long things take. It has never once failed to show up."
L["Fire is the loud one. Ask it a question and it shouts the answer, usually at your eyebrows.\n\nThe rite starts with a torch that won't light, the Torch of the Dormant Flame, and you carry it about feeling foolish. Along the way there's a spirit to face, and it wanted a fight. Fire generally does. I gave it one and won, which is the only way that conversation ends well.\n\nAt the end there's a brazier at a shrine, and a flame that doesn't go out. I carried it back like it was the last fire in the world. Whatever the name says, I was not about to be the shaman who let the eternal flame go out.\n\nAfter that, fire came when I called. Not politely. But it came, and it's been at my side ever since, crackling away and offering opinions nobody asked for."] = "Fire is the loud one. Ask it a question and it shouts the answer, usually at your eyebrows.\n\nThe rite starts with a torch that won't light, the Torch of the Dormant Flame, and you carry it about feeling foolish. Along the way there's a spirit to face, and it wanted a fight. Fire generally does. I gave it one and won, which is the only way that conversation ends well.\n\nAt the end there's a brazier at a shrine, and a flame that doesn't go out. I carried it back like it was the last fire in the world. Whatever the name says, I was not about to be the shaman who let the eternal flame go out.\n\nAfter that, fire came when I called. Not politely. But it came, and it's been at my side ever since, crackling away and offering opinions nobody asked for."
L["Water doesn't hurry, and it doesn't want you to either.\n\nHervdana Saegrund in the Wetlands gave me an empty waterskin and directions. When I brought it back full, she gave me another, and then another. One of them had to be filled at Stonewatch Falls, right among the Nightcrawler murlocs, and I told the water the murlocs were its problem, not mine. It disagreed. Another meant crossing the sea to Astranaar. Out of the three, she made a vial of the purest water I've ever seen.\n\nAfter that came Stendel's Pond in Westfall, where something had gone foul. I fought the corruption out of it, and when the pond ran clean, its spirit rose and gave me a shard of itself for my totem.\n\nWater gives freely once it trusts you. Getting it to trust you takes about half of Azeroth."] = "Water doesn't hurry, and it doesn't want you to either.\n\nHervdana Saegrund in the Wetlands gave me an empty waterskin and directions. When I brought it back full, she gave me another, and then another. One of them had to be filled at Stonewatch Falls, right among the Nightcrawler murlocs, and I told the water the murlocs were its problem, not mine. It disagreed. Another meant crossing the sea to Astranaar. Out of the three, she made a vial of the purest water I've ever seen.\n\nAfter that came Stendel's Pond in Westfall, where something had gone foul. I fought the corruption out of it, and when the pond ran clean, its spirit rose and gave me a shard of itself for my totem.\n\nWater gives freely once it trusts you. Getting it to trust you takes about half of Azeroth."
L["Water teaches patience, and it teaches it by sending you everywhere.\n\nBrine keeps a hut above a watering hole in the southern Barrens. Brine gave me an empty waterskin and told me where to fill it. When I brought it back, I got another, and then another. One of them had to come from the well in Tarren Mill, which is a very long way to go for a drink. Out of the three, she made a vial of the purest water.\n\nWhat was left of it went to the Brazier of Everfount in Silverpine, where a corrupted spirit of water was waiting. I beat it, and the water's true spirit rose clean and gave me a shard of itself to take home.\n\nI grumbled about the walk. The water, as far as I could tell, found that funny. Water goes everywhere and never complains."] = "Water teaches patience, and it teaches it by sending you everywhere.\n\nBrine keeps a hut above a watering hole in the southern Barrens. Brine gave me an empty waterskin and told me where to fill it. When I brought it back, I got another, and then another. One of them had to come from the well in Tarren Mill, which is a very long way to go for a drink. Out of the three, she made a vial of the purest water.\n\nWhat was left of it went to the Brazier of Everfount in Silverpine, where a corrupted spirit of water was waiting. I beat it, and the water's true spirit rose clean and gave me a shard of itself to take home.\n\nI grumbled about the walk. The water, as far as I could tell, found that funny. Water goes everywhere and never complains."
L["Air was the last of the four, and the one that made me work hardest for it.\n\nThe quilboar had been working a ritual with twisted totems, one for each element, and Veyric up in the Arathi Highlands wanted it stopped. Charlga Razorflank down in Razorfen Kraul had the notes on it, so I went down and took the notes, the usual way. Then I collected their totems and turned them on their own ritual. What was left when it broke was the Eye of the Tempest, and I carried it to Bath'rah the Windwatcher in the Alterac Mountains.\n\nSo now I have all four. They argue constantly, mostly through me. I wouldn't have it any other way."] = "Air was the last of the four, and the one that made me work hardest for it.\n\nThe quilboar had been working a ritual with twisted totems, one for each element, and Veyric up in the Arathi Highlands wanted it stopped. Charlga Razorflank down in Razorfen Kraul had the notes on it, so I went down and took the notes, the usual way. Then I collected their totems and turned them on their own ritual. What was left when it broke was the Eye of the Tempest, and I carried it to Bath'rah the Windwatcher in the Alterac Mountains.\n\nSo now I have all four. They argue constantly, mostly through me. I wouldn't have it any other way."
L["After earth, fire and water, I braced myself for air. Air, it turns out, mostly wants you to show up.\n\nMy trainer sent me to Thousand Needles to find Prate Cloudseer, and that was the trial: go there. Mind you, it's a long way down and a long way back, and the wind up on those mesas will push you off one if you let it, so perhaps the walk was the point.\n\nPrate gave me the last totem, and the circle was closed. Earth under my feet, fire at my side, water in my hand and air at my back.\n\nFour elements, and not one of them likes the others. Earth thinks air is flighty. Fire thinks water is dull. Water thinks everybody's in too much of a hurry. And there's me in the middle, keeping the peace, which is about what a shaman is for."] = "After earth, fire and water, I braced myself for air. Air, it turns out, mostly wants you to show up.\n\nMy trainer sent me to Thousand Needles to find Prate Cloudseer, and that was the trial: go there. Mind you, it's a long way down and a long way back, and the wind up on those mesas will push you off one if you let it, so perhaps the walk was the point.\n\nPrate gave me the last totem, and the circle was closed. Earth under my feet, fire at my side, water in my hand and air at my back.\n\nFour elements, and not one of them likes the others. Earth thinks air is flighty. Fire thinks water is dull. Water thinks everybody's in too much of a hurry. And there's me in the middle, keeping the peace, which is about what a shaman is for."
L["Long after my first totems, Kardris Dreamseeker in Orgrimmar decided I was ready for the harder lesson: holding all four elements at once instead of one at a time.\n\nThat meant Bath'rah the Windwatcher, up in the Alterac Mountains, and Bath'rah wanted a sample of each element. The real thing. You don't buy elemental fire off a cart. You take it from something made of fire, and the something objects.\n\nThen came the spirit totem, which wanted eight spider eyes and eight black claws. I asked the spirits what those had to do with anything. The spirits did not say. Spirits rarely do when you most want them to.\n\nMastery, they call it. I'd call it a truce. Four elements that can't stand one another, agreeing to work through me at the same time. Some days they even manage it."] = "Long after my first totems, Kardris Dreamseeker in Orgrimmar decided I was ready for the harder lesson: holding all four elements at once instead of one at a time.\n\nThat meant Bath'rah the Windwatcher, up in the Alterac Mountains, and Bath'rah wanted a sample of each element. The real thing. You don't buy elemental fire off a cart. You take it from something made of fire, and the something objects.\n\nThen came the spirit totem, which wanted eight spider eyes and eight black claws. I asked the spirits what those had to do with anything. The spirits did not say. Spirits rarely do when you most want them to.\n\nMastery, they call it. I'd call it a truce. Four elements that can't stand one another, agreeing to work through me at the same time. Some days they even manage it."
L["Eldrun Stormbreaker was building a scryer to see through the shadows of the Great Ossuary in Scholomance, and a scryer like that wants diamonds. Two good ones. I found them. That was the easy part.\n\nThen I took the scryer down to the Ossuary's basement and set it going. The spirits came first, every one of them bound to a death knight called Darkreaver, and none of them pleased to be seen. I fought them down. When the last went, Darkreaver came himself.\n\nEarth held my feet, fire did the shouting, and between us we finished him. I carried his head back to Eldrun, and the restless dead under Scholomance are a good deal less restless now.\n\nMost of my life I've listened to spirits. Some of them, I've learned, you just have to shout over."] = "Eldrun Stormbreaker was building a scryer to see through the shadows of the Great Ossuary in Scholomance, and a scryer like that wants diamonds. Two good ones. I found them. That was the easy part.\n\nThen I took the scryer down to the Ossuary's basement and set it going. The spirits came first, every one of them bound to a death knight called Darkreaver, and none of them pleased to be seen. I fought them down. When the last went, Darkreaver came himself.\n\nEarth held my feet, fire did the shouting, and between us we finished him. I carried his head back to Eldrun, and the restless dead under Scholomance are a good deal less restless now.\n\nMost of my life I've listened to spirits. Some of them, I've learned, you just have to shout over."
L["Stormwind has pulled its guards out of Darkshire, and Commander Althea Ebonlocke's Night Watch is all the town has left. The commander needs volunteers. Duskwood's dead have stopped staying in their graves."] = "Stormwind has pulled its guards out of Darkshire, and Commander Althea Ebonlocke's Night Watch is all the town has left. The commander needs volunteers. Duskwood's dead have stopped staying in their graves."
L["Sven Yorgen lost his family to the Dark Riders of Deadwind Pass. Coming home, he saw a shadowy figure burying something behind the stump by his barn, and never found out what. Sven is camped at the far western edge of Duskwood."] = "Sven Yorgen lost his family to the Dark Riders of Deadwind Pass. Coming home, he saw a shadowy figure burying something behind the stump by his barn, and never found out what. Sven is camped at the far western edge of Duskwood."
L["Sirra Von'Indi keeps the library in Darkshire Town Hall, and it has next to nothing on Raven Hill. The village's history died with its people. If you turn up anything in the ruins, Sirra would like to see it."] = "Sirra Von'Indi keeps the library in Darkshire Town Hall, and it has next to nothing on Raven Hill. The village's history died with its people. If you turn up anything in the ruins, Sirra would like to see it."
L["On the hill north of Raven Hill Cemetery stands a weathered grave marked Morgan Ladimore, and someone has disturbed the dirt on it. Sirra Von'Indi in Darkshire might remember the name."] = "On the hill north of Raven Hill Cemetery stands a weathered grave marked Morgan Ladimore, and someone has disturbed the dirt on it. Sirra Von'Indi in Darkshire might remember the name."
L["Madame Eva read the cards in Darkshire last night, and Death stared back. Eva fears for her granddaughter, and the trance gave up only one clue: a name, Stalvan. Clerk Daltry at the Town Hall might know it."] = "Madame Eva read the cards in Darkshire last night, and Death stared back. Eva fears for her granddaughter, and the trance gave up only one clue: a name, Stalvan. Clerk Daltry at the Town Hall might know it."
L["Calor, in Darkshire, gave up on the Night Watch and joined Master Carevin, who goes after Duskwood's evil instead of waiting for it. If you want in, Calor says, prove it on the worgen in Brightwood Grove."] = "Calor, in Darkshire, gave up on the Night Watch and joined Master Carevin, who goes after Duskwood's evil instead of waiting for it. If you want in, Calor says, prove it on the worgen in Brightwood Grove."
L["An old man who lives in a shack above Raven Hill Cemetery used to come into Darkshire for supplies. Elaine Carevin hasn't seen him in a long while and thinks somebody should make sure nothing's wrong."] = "An old man who lives in a shack above Raven Hill Cemetery used to come into Darkshire for supplies. Elaine Carevin hasn't seen him in a long while and thinks somebody should make sure nothing's wrong."
L["Raene Wolfrunner, in Astranaar, is worried about Teronis, who went west looking for a way to stop the furbolg attacks and hasn't come back. Raene would like Teronis found. Ashenvale is a big forest."] = "Raene Wolfrunner, in Astranaar, is worried about Teronis, who went west looking for a way to stop the furbolg attacks and hasn't come back. Raene would like Teronis found. Ashenvale is a big forest."
L["Raene has read Teronis' journal, and it points to Shael'dryn, who keeps a moonwell east of Iris Lake. Shael'dryn knows what Teronis was after. Getting it will take you into some of Ashenvale's less pleasant corners."] = "Raene has read Teronis' journal, and it points to Shael'dryn, who keeps a moonwell east of Iris Lake. Shael'dryn knows what Teronis was after. Getting it will take you into some of Ashenvale's less pleasant corners."
L["Raene Wolfrunner, back in Astranaar, has a use for Dartol's Rod. A few of Ashenvale's furbolg still aren't corrupted, and Raene wants you to talk to one. The rod, apparently, will help with the introductions."] = "Raene Wolfrunner, back in Astranaar, has a use for Dartol's Rod. A few of Ashenvale's furbolg still aren't corrupted, and Raene wants you to talk to one. The rod, apparently, will help with the introductions."
L["Your trainer says the call has come: the spirit of the bear will see you. Spirits are never in a hurry, and a bear's spirit least of all. Go where you're sent, and take your time. It will."] = "Your trainer says the call has come: the spirit of the bear will see you. Spirits are never in a hurry, and a bear's spirit least of all. Go where you're sent, and take your time. It will."
L["Your trainer sends you to Moonglade, where Dendrite Starblaze has a new lesson waiting. This one is about healing, and the Circle doesn't teach healing from a book. Bring patience, and good boots."] = "Your trainer sends you to Moonglade, where Dendrite Starblaze has a new lesson waiting. This one is about healing, and the Circle doesn't teach healing from a book. Bring patience, and good boots."
L["Your trainer has another lesson for you, and Dendrite Starblaze in Moonglade will give it. This one is about water, so you will get wet. Druids teach the way water moves: slowly, and never in a straight line."] = "Your trainer has another lesson for you, and Dendrite Starblaze in Moonglade will give it. This one is about water, so you will get wet. Druids teach the way water moves: slowly, and never in a straight line."
L["Dendrite Starblaze wants you in Moonglade. There's someone to meet: the spirit of a great cat, out in the southern hills. Cats are particular about whom they teach, and they take their time deciding."] = "Dendrite Starblaze wants you in Moonglade. There's someone to meet: the spirit of a great cat, out in the southern hills. Cats are particular about whom they teach, and they take their time deciding."
L["Aboard one of the Bloodsail ships off Stranglethorn's Wild Shore lies a small scroll with a riddle on it, signed Cortello. Nobody seems to know who Cortello was. Nobody writes a riddle about nothing, either."] = "Aboard one of the Bloodsail ships off Stranglethorn's Wild Shore lies a small scroll with a riddle on it, signed Cortello. Nobody seems to know who Cortello was. Nobody writes a riddle about nothing, either."
L["Under a bridge west of Stonard, in the Swamp of Sorrows, Cortello left another scroll. The swamp has not been kind to it. The verse is still readable, just about, and it has somewhere new in mind."] = "Under a bridge west of Stonard, in the Swamp of Sorrows, Cortello left another scroll. The swamp has not been kind to it. The verse is still readable, just about, and it has somewhere new in mind."
L["The musty scroll from Bloodfen Burrow has another verse on it. By now you've boarded a pirate ship, crawled under a bridge and walked into a raptor den on Cortello's say-so. Why stop now?"] = "The musty scroll from Bloodfen Burrow has another verse on it. By now you've boarded a pirate ship, crawled under a bridge and walked into a raptor den on Cortello's say-so. Why stop now?"
L["There's a ghost on the road where the Swamp of Sorrows gives way to the Blasted Lands. The Fallen Hero of the Horde, they call him, and his is a sorry tale. He took his soldiers toward the Blasted Lands and waited for orders. The orders never came.\n\nStonard knew the story. A few questions there, and I knew where the orders had actually gone: out to the murloc camps, into the keeping of something called the Swamp Talker. I went and took them back. The Warchief's own orders, long overdue and a bit damp.\n\nI carried them to the ghost. A dead man, finally reading the orders he'd waited for. I let him have a minute."] = "There's a ghost on the road where the Swamp of Sorrows gives way to the Blasted Lands. The Fallen Hero of the Horde, they call him, and his is a sorry tale. He took his soldiers toward the Blasted Lands and waited for orders. The orders never came.\n\nStonard knew the story. A few questions there, and I knew where the orders had actually gone: out to the murloc camps, into the keeping of something called the Swamp Talker. I went and took them back. The Warchief's own orders, long overdue and a bit damp.\n\nI carried them to the ghost. A dead man, finally reading the orders he'd waited for. I let him have a minute."
L["An ambassador at Nethergarde Keep had an errand for me I didn't see coming: go and talk to a Horde ghost. With demons camped on the doorstep, I suppose the old squabbles can wait.\n\nThe Fallen Hero of the Horde stands where the swamp gives way to the Blasted Lands, and he told me all of it. Soldiers who crossed over, and what Razelikh the Defiler's people did with them after. Out there the dead don't get to rest. They get bound to stones and put to work.\n\nSo he gave me a job: set them loose, eighteen of them, at the Stones of Binding. You have to stay right by the stone while you work or it all comes apart, and fighting well while rooted to one spot is harder than it sounds.\n\nOdd work for the Alliance. The dead don't seem to mind whose colors you wear."] = "An ambassador at Nethergarde Keep had an errand for me I didn't see coming: go and talk to a Horde ghost. With demons camped on the doorstep, I suppose the old squabbles can wait.\n\nThe Fallen Hero of the Horde stands where the swamp gives way to the Blasted Lands, and he told me all of it. Soldiers who crossed over, and what Razelikh the Defiler's people did with them after. Out there the dead don't get to rest. They get bound to stones and put to work.\n\nSo he gave me a job: set them loose, eighteen of them, at the Stones of Binding. You have to stay right by the stone while you work or it all comes apart, and fighting well while rooted to one spot is harder than it sounds.\n\nOdd work for the Alliance. The dead don't seem to mind whose colors you wear."
L["With the orders read, the Fallen Hero told me the rest of it. What happened to the soldiers who crossed into the Blasted Lands, and what Razelikh the Defiler's people did with them after. Out there the dead don't get to rest. They get bound to stones and put to work.\n\nSo he gave me a job: set them loose, eighteen of them, at the Stones of Binding. The catch is that you have to stay close to the stone while you work, or the whole thing comes apart. Nobody tells you how hard it is to fight well while rooted to one spot.\n\nWhen it was done, he didn't thank me, exactly. He started remembering names."] = "With the orders read, the Fallen Hero told me the rest of it. What happened to the soldiers who crossed into the Blasted Lands, and what Razelikh the Defiler's people did with them after. Out there the dead don't get to rest. They get bound to stones and put to work.\n\nSo he gave me a job: set them loose, eighteen of them, at the Stones of Binding. The catch is that you have to stay close to the stone while you work, or the whole thing comes apart. Nobody tells you how hard it is to fight well while rooted to one spot.\n\nWhen it was done, he didn't thank me, exactly. He started remembering names."
L["Once the stones were quiet, the Fallen Hero gave me a name: Corporal Thund Splithoof, one of his own. Thund was waiting near the road into the Blasted Lands, with a spectral lockbox and something inside it for me. The dead, it turns out, pay their debts.\n\nThe second name was Lieutenant Kirith. Find out what became of Kirith, the Fallen Hero said. So I went looking, and out at the Serpent's Coil I found what was left: a spirit, with news.\n\nI carried the news back to the road. The Fallen Hero took it in, and then said two words I didn't expect from an old soldier of the Horde. Demon hunter."] = "Once the stones were quiet, the Fallen Hero gave me a name: Corporal Thund Splithoof, one of his own. Thund was waiting near the road into the Blasted Lands, with a spectral lockbox and something inside it for me. The dead, it turns out, pay their debts.\n\nThe second name was Lieutenant Kirith. Find out what became of Kirith, the Fallen Hero said. So I went looking, and out at the Serpent's Coil I found what was left: a spirit, with news.\n\nI carried the news back to the road. The Fallen Hero took it in, and then said two words I didn't expect from an old soldier of the Horde. Demon hunter."
L["The demon hunter was Loramus Thalipedes, who lives on the coast of Azshara by the Bay of Storms. Loramus told me his story, then worked a spell of enlightenment while I stood there waiting, and when it was done I understood the problem. Razelikh is warded, and getting through that ward takes the beast's name.\n\nThe name came by way of a temple down by the water, whose lord wanted Hetaera dead first and the heads brought as proof. All three of them. Bloodied, beaten and bruised, which tells you how that fight went.\n\nLast came twenty chunks of Azsharite, hidden all over southern Azshara, and a felhound to sniff them out. Best partner I had that year. Never said a word."] = "The demon hunter was Loramus Thalipedes, who lives on the coast of Azshara by the Bay of Storms. Loramus told me his story, then worked a spell of enlightenment while I stood there waiting, and when it was done I understood the problem. Razelikh is warded, and getting through that ward takes the beast's name.\n\nThe name came by way of a temple down by the water, whose lord wanted Hetaera dead first and the heads brought as proof. All three of them. Bloodied, beaten and bruised, which tells you how that fight went.\n\nLast came twenty chunks of Azsharite, hidden all over southern Azshara, and a felhound to sniff them out. Best partner I had that year. Never said a word."
L["The demon hunter packed the Azsharite into a shipment and sent me off with it to Galvan the Ancient, in northern Stranglethorn. That's one end of the world to the other, near enough, hauling a crate of crystal somebody had very particular plans for.\n\nGalvan is called the Ancient for a reason, and nobody hurries Galvan. I stood by while the forge did its work, and what came out of the fire was Felbane, made for the one job ordinary steel can't do. Razelikh's lieutenants can't be killed by anything else.\n\nThen I carried it all the way back to the Blasted Lands, to the Fallen Hero on his road. I'd like to think it was the first good news he'd had since he died."] = "The demon hunter packed the Azsharite into a shipment and sent me off with it to Galvan the Ancient, in northern Stranglethorn. That's one end of the world to the other, near enough, hauling a crate of crystal somebody had very particular plans for.\n\nGalvan is called the Ancient for a reason, and nobody hurries Galvan. I stood by while the forge did its work, and what came out of the fire was Felbane, made for the one job ordinary steel can't do. Razelikh's lieutenants can't be killed by anything else.\n\nThen I carried it all the way back to the Blasted Lands, to the Fallen Hero on his road. I'd like to think it was the first good news he'd had since he died."
L["Razelikh kept three lieutenants, and each of them wore a piece of a shattered amulet. None of them could die until Felbane had weakened them, and the weapons only had so much in them. Use them wisely, the Fallen Hero said. I did. Mostly.\n\nThree pieces made one, and with that I went up the Rise of the Defiler to see the demon himself. The Fallen Hero had a name for him by then. Rakh'likh.\n\nI came back with the demon's severed horn, and the Ward of the Defiler with it, so the Fallen Hero could destroy that ward before it fell into the wrong hands. He didn't get his soldiers back. He got the horn."] = "Razelikh kept three lieutenants, and each of them wore a piece of a shattered amulet. None of them could die until Felbane had weakened them, and the weapons only had so much in them. Use them wisely, the Fallen Hero said. I did. Mostly.\n\nThree pieces made one, and with that I went up the Rise of the Defiler to see the demon himself. The Fallen Hero had a name for him by then. Rakh'likh.\n\nI came back with the demon's severed horn, and the Ward of the Defiler with it, so the Fallen Hero could destroy that ward before it fell into the wrong hands. He didn't get his soldiers back. He got the horn."
L["Apothecary Johaan, at the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, needs darkhound blood. Then other things. He's working on something new for the Royal Apothecary Society, and he hums while he works, which is never a good sign."] = "Apothecary Johaan, at the Gallows' End Tavern in Brill, needs darkhound blood. Then other things. He's working on something new for the Royal Apothecary Society, and he hums while he works, which is never a good sign."
L["Johaan's findings are with Apothecary Renferrel now, at the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest. Renferrel has a shopping list of bear hearts and spider blood, all bound for Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity. Don't ask what for."] = "Johaan's findings are with Apothecary Renferrel now, at the Sepulcher in Silverpine Forest. Renferrel has a shopping list of bear hearts and spider blood, all bound for Master Apothecary Faranell in the Undercity. Don't ask what for."
L["Apothecary Lydon, in Tarren Mill, wants gray bear tongues and creeper ichor for a new elixir. Lydon likes to see his work tested, on somebody other than Lydon. That's where you come in."] = "Apothecary Lydon, in Tarren Mill, wants gray bear tongues and creeper ichor for a new elixir. Lydon likes to see his work tested, on somebody other than Lydon. That's where you come in."
L["Apothecary Lydon, in Tarren Mill, has his eye on a greater work: the Elixir of Agony. It starts with mudsnout blossoms. It does not end there. Lydon's recipes never do."] = "Apothecary Lydon, in Tarren Mill, has his eye on a greater work: the Elixir of Agony. It starts with mudsnout blossoms. It does not end there. Lydon's recipes never do."
L["Theodore Griffs, in the Undercity's Apothecarium, has heard of a spider in the ruins above the city, Witherfang, whose venom eats through undead flesh. He wants a sample. In the Undercity, that isn't a rumor. It's a job offer."] = "Theodore Griffs, in the Undercity's Apothecarium, has heard of a spider in the ruins above the city, Witherfang, whose venom eats through undead flesh. He wants a sample. In the Undercity, that isn't a rumor. It's a job offer."
L["The Scourge are trying to dig in at the Agamand Mills, northwest of Brill, and Deathguard Dillinger wants them beaten back. Coleman Farthing, at the Gallows' End Tavern, once worked those mills and has his own business with the family that owned them."] = "The Scourge are trying to dig in at the Agamand Mills, northwest of Brill, and Deathguard Dillinger wants them beaten back. Coleman Farthing, at the Gallows' End Tavern, once worked those mills and has his own business with the family that owned them."
L["Coleman Farthing has had his revenge on the Agamands, but says the mills still threaten every Forsaken in Tirisfal. Magistrate Sevren in Brill has heard about your work out there and wants a word."] = "Coleman Farthing has had his revenge on the Agamands, but says the mills still threaten every Forsaken in Tirisfal. Magistrate Sevren in Brill has heard about your work out there and wants a word."
L["Northshire was supposed to be the quiet part. I'd barely found the abbey before the deputy had me pulling red bandanas off thieves, and every thief had a friend. In the vineyards, across the river, out at the logging camp: same masks, same red. Apparently they have a dress code.\n\nThe best of it was the Collector. A schedule turned up with that name on it, and Marshal Dughan read it and sent me hunting. A thief who goes by the Collector is asking for a certain kind of visitor. The Collector had a ring, and the Collector had my full attention, and only one of those two made it through the day.\n\nThat's a lot of matching masks for a few bandits. Bandits don't coordinate."] = "Northshire was supposed to be the quiet part. I'd barely found the abbey before the deputy had me pulling red bandanas off thieves, and every thief had a friend. In the vineyards, across the river, out at the logging camp: same masks, same red. Apparently they have a dress code.\n\nThe best of it was the Collector. A schedule turned up with that name on it, and Marshal Dughan read it and sent me hunting. A thief who goes by the Collector is asking for a certain kind of visitor. The Collector had a ring, and the Collector had my full attention, and only one of those two made it through the day.\n\nThat's a lot of matching masks for a few bandits. Bandits don't coordinate."
L["Gryan Stoutmantle counts in fifteens. Fifteen trappers, fifteen smugglers, fifteen pillagers, fifteen looters, fifteen highwaymen, and a few extra so I wouldn't get bored. For a while there, the People's Militia was mostly me.\n\nBest errand of the lot was a note. Gryan sent me to Lakeshire to see someone called Wiley the Black, and Wiley gave me a note. I carried it back to Westfall. Gryan had me carry it on to Mathias Shaw in Stormwind. Shaw had me carry his report back to Westfall. Three zones and four trips for some paper, and nobody once thought to hire a courier. I was the courier.\n\nStill, Shaw's report gave Gryan a name to put to all those masks. That changes a fight."] = "Gryan Stoutmantle counts in fifteens. Fifteen trappers, fifteen smugglers, fifteen pillagers, fifteen looters, fifteen highwaymen, and a few extra so I wouldn't get bored. For a while there, the People's Militia was mostly me.\n\nBest errand of the lot was a note. Gryan sent me to Lakeshire to see someone called Wiley the Black, and Wiley gave me a note. I carried it back to Westfall. Gryan had me carry it on to Mathias Shaw in Stormwind. Shaw had me carry his report back to Westfall. Three zones and four trips for some paper, and nobody once thought to hire a courier. I was the courier.\n\nStill, Shaw's report gave Gryan a name to put to all those masks. That changes a fight."
L["A name's a fine thing, but Gryan wanted an address. So I watched the roads south of Moonbrook until a Defias messenger came strolling along with a letter, and we settled the question of who'd be delivering it. Me. To Gryan.\n\nWhat really helped was the Defias Traitor, who turned up at Sentinel Hill ready to show us where the rest of them were hiding. All it took was walking a turncoat down to Moonbrook alive, and the road to Moonbrook is not a friendly road. I kept the Traitor breathing right up to the hole in the ground they'd been using for a front door.\n\nThe old mine. Of course it was the mine."] = "A name's a fine thing, but Gryan wanted an address. So I watched the roads south of Moonbrook until a Defias messenger came strolling along with a letter, and we settled the question of who'd be delivering it. Me. To Gryan.\n\nWhat really helped was the Defias Traitor, who turned up at Sentinel Hill ready to show us where the rest of them were hiding. All it took was walking a turncoat down to Moonbrook alive, and the road to Moonbrook is not a friendly road. I kept the Traitor breathing right up to the hole in the ground they'd been using for a front door.\n\nThe old mine. Of course it was the mine."
L["The Deadmines. You go in expecting a mine, and you get one, and then a goblin foundry, and then a cave so big there's a whole ship sitting in it. A proper ship, cannons and all, under Westfall. Whoever dug that place out knew their trade.\n\nEdwin VanCleef was up on the deck, and he did not go quietly. Gryan wanted his head, and I carried it out into the daylight and all the way up to Sentinel Hill.\n\nThere was a letter on VanCleef, too. Addressed to the City Architect in Stormwind, and never sent. That's another story."] = "The Deadmines. You go in expecting a mine, and you get one, and then a goblin foundry, and then a cave so big there's a whole ship sitting in it. A proper ship, cannons and all, under Westfall. Whoever dug that place out knew their trade.\n\nEdwin VanCleef was up on the deck, and he did not go quietly. Gryan wanted his head, and I carried it out into the daylight and all the way up to Sentinel Hill.\n\nThere was a letter on VanCleef, too. Addressed to the City Architect in Stormwind, and never sent. That's another story."
L["Shoni the Shilent in Stormwind had asked me to fetch a Gnoam Sprecklesprocket while I was down there. I couldn't tell you what one is. Shoni knew, and Shoni was paying, and that was enough for me."] = "Shoni the Shilent in Stormwind had asked me to fetch a Gnoam Sprecklesprocket while I was down there. I couldn't tell you what one is. Shoni knew, and Shoni was paying, and that was enough for me."
L["VanCleef's letter was for Baros Alexston, the City Architect. Baros had known the Stonemasons back when they were still just masons, and I got the feeling that letter landed somewhere sore. Next thing I knew, I was on my way to the Stockade.\n\nOne of the Brotherhood's leaders, Bazil Thredd, was locked up in there, and the prisoners had picked that moment to riot. The warden wanted it put down and Thredd's head brought out. So in I went: a whole building full of people with nothing to lose and a grudge against anyone holding a key. Thredd was waiting deep in the back. I came out with the head.\n\nThe warden thanked me, and then mentioned, as if it were nothing, that Thredd had had a visitor."] = "VanCleef's letter was for Baros Alexston, the City Architect. Baros had known the Stonemasons back when they were still just masons, and I got the feeling that letter landed somewhere sore. Next thing I knew, I was on my way to the Stockade.\n\nOne of the Brotherhood's leaders, Bazil Thredd, was locked up in there, and the prisoners had picked that moment to riot. The warden wanted it put down and Thredd's head brought out. So in I went: a whole building full of people with nothing to lose and a grudge against anyone holding a key. Thredd was waiting deep in the back. I came out with the head.\n\nThe warden thanked me, and then mentioned, as if it were nothing, that Thredd had had a visitor."
L["The warden had written down what that visitor looked like and sealed it, and I carried the envelope across Stormwind like it might go off. It went from hand to hand until it reached Shaw, and Shaw told me to look up an old friend.\n\nThe old friend sold cheese. Elling Trias, in the Trade District, behind a counter stacked with wheels of the stuff, and SI:7 the whole time. Half the city has bought cheddar off that man and never known.\n\nTrias sent me up to the castle to meet Tyrion, who had a plan and a little mechanical spy to carry it out. The plan needed silk cloth and two fresh apples. I've been handed some strange shopping lists in my time. That one was for catching a traitor, and it had apples on it."] = "The warden had written down what that visitor looked like and sealed it, and I carried the envelope across Stormwind like it might go off. It went from hand to hand until it reached Shaw, and Shaw told me to look up an old friend.\n\nThe old friend sold cheese. Elling Trias, in the Trade District, behind a counter stacked with wheels of the stuff, and SI:7 the whole time. Half the city has bought cheddar off that man and never known.\n\nTrias sent me up to the castle to meet Tyrion, who had a plan and a little mechanical spy to carry it out. The plan needed silk cloth and two fresh apples. I've been handed some strange shopping lists in my time. That one was for catching a traitor, and it had apples on it."
L["The spybot did its work, and I ended up crouched in the castle garden, waiting on a meeting. Lord Gregor Lescovar, an actual lord of Stormwind, came out to talk business with Marzon the Silent Blade. For somebody called the Silent Blade, Marzon talked plenty. I let them get it all said.\n\nThen I stood up. Neither of them enjoyed that part.\n\nAfter that the story went up the ladder, one important person at a time, until I was carrying a report into the keep for the king. It started with thieves in a Northshire vineyard. It ended in the royal garden with a lord.\n\nI don't know what the king made of it. I do know there was one fewer lord at dinner."] = "The spybot did its work, and I ended up crouched in the castle garden, waiting on a meeting. Lord Gregor Lescovar, an actual lord of Stormwind, came out to talk business with Marzon the Silent Blade. For somebody called the Silent Blade, Marzon talked plenty. I let them get it all said.\n\nThen I stood up. Neither of them enjoyed that part.\n\nAfter that the story went up the ladder, one important person at a time, until I was carrying a report into the keep for the king. It started with thieves in a Northshire vineyard. It ended in the royal garden with a lord.\n\nI don't know what the king made of it. I do know there was one fewer lord at dinner."
L["If the Eye of Divinity comes into your possession, seek Eris Havenfire in the Eastern Plaguelands. She has a trial that asks for more than quick hands and bright prayers.\n\nStand between panic and shadow, save who you can, and learn whether your faith holds when every mistake has a cost."] = "If the Eye of Divinity comes into your possession, seek Eris Havenfire in the Eastern Plaguelands. She has a trial that asks for more than quick hands and bright prayers.\n\nStand between panic and shadow, save who you can, and learn whether your faith holds when every mistake has a cost."
L["In Silithus, the Bronze Dragonflight does not hand trust to strangers. Before anyone can challenge what waits behind Ahn'Qiraj's gates, someone must prove they are more than another mortal asking for legends.\n\nBegin with the Brood of Nozdormu and follow the old scepter's trail through dragonflights, hidden lairs, bargains, and errands that sound impossible until someone does them."] = "In Silithus, the Bronze Dragonflight does not hand trust to strangers. Before anyone can challenge what waits behind Ahn'Qiraj's gates, someone must prove they are more than another mortal asking for legends.\n\nBegin with the Brood of Nozdormu and follow the old scepter's trail through dragonflights, hidden lairs, bargains, and errands that sound impossible until someone does them."
L["Under the broken Thandol Span, where few travelers think to look, lie a dwarf's remains and a waterlogged envelope. It's addressed to Sara Balloo in Ironforge. It never got there."] = "Under the broken Thandol Span, where few travelers think to look, lie a dwarf's remains and a waterlogged envelope. It's addressed to Sara Balloo in Ironforge. It never got there."
L["Sara Balloo's plea is in King Magni's hands now, up on the High Seat in Ironforge. The king has read it and made up his mind. It involves the Grand Mason, and it involves you."] = "Sara Balloo's plea is in King Magni's hands now, up on the High Seat in Ironforge. The king has read it and made up his mind. It involves the Grand Mason, and it involves you."
L["Every mage needs a focus, and the clever ones have theirs made by the best. In Valanaar, the best is Belann Windwood, who has been shaping staves and wands for the High Order for more than a century.\n\nThe price is one of the order's oldest traditions: a fallen bough from the oldest trees on the isle, the kind the wind has been working on longer than anyone has kept count. A Wind-Infused Bough, to be precise, and I always am.\n\nYou'd think a fallen branch would be easy to find. Fallen branches are everywhere. The right one is not. I found it anyway, because I'm good at this.\n\nOut of it Belann made a focus that answers to me and nobody else. It's a tradition, apparently. I prefer to think of it as overdue recognition."] = "Every mage needs a focus, and the clever ones have theirs made by the best. In Valanaar, the best is Belann Windwood, who has been shaping staves and wands for the High Order for more than a century.\n\nThe price is one of the order's oldest traditions: a fallen bough from the oldest trees on the isle, the kind the wind has been working on longer than anyone has kept count. A Wind-Infused Bough, to be precise, and I always am.\n\nYou'd think a fallen branch would be easy to find. Fallen branches are everywhere. The right one is not. I found it anyway, because I'm good at this.\n\nOut of it Belann made a focus that answers to me and nobody else. It's a tradition, apparently. I prefer to think of it as overdue recognition."
L["Tabetha lives out in Dustwallow Marsh, and she's who the trainers send you to when you're ready for a wand. Practical sort. Knows her business.\n\nShe wanted a book called Rituals of Power, which Magus Tirth keeps in a strongbox out on the Shimmering Flats. The box only opens to the right phrase, and nobody was going to just tell me the phrase. I had to go and find it. Frankly, that was the easiest part of the whole affair. Words are what I do.\n\nAfter a little shopping, jade and a bramble with lightning still crackling in it, Tabetha did her ritual. I watched very closely. I walked out with a wand of my own school.\n\nMost people think a wand is a stick that throws sparks. Most people are right. But it's my stick, and the sparks go exactly where I tell them."] = "Tabetha lives out in Dustwallow Marsh, and she's who the trainers send you to when you're ready for a wand. Practical sort. Knows her business.\n\nShe wanted a book called Rituals of Power, which Magus Tirth keeps in a strongbox out on the Shimmering Flats. The box only opens to the right phrase, and nobody was going to just tell me the phrase. I had to go and find it. Frankly, that was the easiest part of the whole affair. Words are what I do.\n\nAfter a little shopping, jade and a bramble with lightning still crackling in it, Tabetha did her ritual. I watched very closely. I walked out with a wand of my own school.\n\nMost people think a wand is a stick that throws sparks. Most people are right. But it's my stick, and the sparks go exactly where I tell them."
L["Archmage Xylem keeps a tower on the cliffs of Azshara, and when an archmage that far from anywhere asks for help, it's never boring.\n\nPolymorph does something odd to the Spitelash naga. Turn one into a sheep, and a few seconds later a copy of it falls out of the spell, as though the magic can't decide what it's holding. You kill the copy. Xylem needed fifty.\n\nFifty. I sheeped half the coast. It was the most scientific afternoon of my life.\n\nAnd what do fifty scraps of broken magic teach you? How to turn someone into a pig. Is a pig better than a sheep? It's funnier, and I've come to believe that counts."] = "Archmage Xylem keeps a tower on the cliffs of Azshara, and when an archmage that far from anywhere asks for help, it's never boring.\n\nPolymorph does something odd to the Spitelash naga. Turn one into a sheep, and a few seconds later a copy of it falls out of the spell, as though the magic can't decide what it's holding. You kill the copy. Xylem needed fifty.\n\nFifty. I sheeped half the coast. It was the most scientific afternoon of my life.\n\nAnd what do fifty scraps of broken magic teach you? How to turn someone into a pig. Is a pig better than a sheep? It's funnier, and I've come to believe that counts."
L["The errand was a Prismatic Shell, and a warlord called Krellian had one. Had. The real work came after."] = "The errand was a Prismatic Shell, and a warlord called Krellian had one. Had. The real work came after."
L["Dire Maul has a library, the Athenaeum, and in it sits Lorekeeper Lydros, who looks after a great many books and a few very good spells. I liked Lydros at once. Anyone who stays in a ruin for the reading has their priorities in order.\n\nThe spell's price was the essence of Hydrospawn, a water elemental loose in the Warpwood Quarter. Hydrospawn didn't care to be anybody's price. It was a damp afternoon. I brought the essence back.\n\nWhat I got for it isn't flashy. No fire, no frost, nothing anyone will write a song about. But a fight is a short thing between two long rests. Anyone can throw a fireball. Not everyone can bring refreshments, and at the end of a long day underground, you'd be amazed which one people remember."] = "Dire Maul has a library, the Athenaeum, and in it sits Lorekeeper Lydros, who looks after a great many books and a few very good spells. I liked Lydros at once. Anyone who stays in a ruin for the reading has their priorities in order.\n\nThe spell's price was the essence of Hydrospawn, a water elemental loose in the Warpwood Quarter. Hydrospawn didn't care to be anybody's price. It was a damp afternoon. I brought the essence back.\n\nWhat I got for it isn't flashy. No fire, no frost, nothing anyone will write a song about. But a fight is a short thing between two long rests. Anyone can throw a fireball. Not everyone can bring refreshments, and at the end of a long day underground, you'd be amazed which one people remember."
L["The Agamands owned the mills north of Brill, back when they were alive. They're still there. They just don't run the place anymore.\n\nThe deathguard wanted the grounds cleared, and Coleman Farthing wanted something more personal: the family. Devlin Agamand haunts those mills, and Coleman wanted Devlin's remains, and then the remains of the rest of them, one after another. The whole family, one sack at a time.\n\nColeman had wanted proof of what became of the Agamands. I brought back most of the Agamands. That seemed to settle the question."] = "The Agamands owned the mills north of Brill, back when they were alive. They're still there. They just don't run the place anymore.\n\nThe deathguard wanted the grounds cleared, and Coleman Farthing wanted something more personal: the family. Devlin Agamand haunts those mills, and Coleman wanted Devlin's remains, and then the remains of the rest of them, one after another. The whole family, one sack at a time.\n\nColeman had wanted proof of what became of the Agamands. I brought back most of the Agamands. That seemed to settle the question."
L["Somewhere in the middle of it I came across a letter one of the Agamands had written and never got round to delivering. So I delivered it. Late, admittedly. The post in Tirisfal has never been quick."] = "Somewhere in the middle of it I came across a letter one of the Agamands had written and never got round to delivering. So I delivered it. Late, admittedly. The post in Tirisfal has never been quick."
L["Coleman sent me on to Magistrate Sevren, and Sevren had heard things about the crypt under the mills. Turns out the Agamands I'd already met weren't the only Agamands. There were older ones down in the dark, generations of them, and not one of them was resting.\n\nSo down I went. Wailing ancestors and rotting ancestors, and the wailing ones are worse. Take my word for it. A rotting ancestor goes down easily enough, but a wailing one stays in your ears for days.\n\nAt the back of it all was Captain Dargol. I brought the captain's skull back to Sevren, and that was the end of the Agamand family's trouble, as far as Brill was concerned. One grave at a time, every one of them left quieter than I found it."] = "Coleman sent me on to Magistrate Sevren, and Sevren had heard things about the crypt under the mills. Turns out the Agamands I'd already met weren't the only Agamands. There were older ones down in the dark, generations of them, and not one of them was resting.\n\nSo down I went. Wailing ancestors and rotting ancestors, and the wailing ones are worse. Take my word for it. A rotting ancestor goes down easily enough, but a wailing one stays in your ears for days.\n\nAt the back of it all was Captain Dargol. I brought the captain's skull back to Sevren, and that was the end of the Agamand family's trouble, as far as Brill was concerned. One grave at a time, every one of them left quieter than I found it."
L["Quillboar fell on Mankrik and his wife at a small tauren camp, and the fighting split them up. Mankrik woke on the Gold Road with a tauren druid tending his wounds. Now he waits at the Crossroads, asking for any sign of her."] = "Quillboar fell on Mankrik and his wife at a small tauren camp, and the fighting split them up. Mankrik woke on the Gold Road with a tauren druid tending his wounds. Now he waits at the Crossroads, asking for any sign of her."
L["Mankrik waits at the Crossroads. A big orc, built for a fight, and he'd lost one: quillboar hit him on the Gold Road, and his wife didn't come back from it. He asked me to find her. That was the whole of it. Find her.\n\nThe Barrens is a great deal of nothing in every direction, and I walked a lot of it. South of the Crossroads, I found her. Too late, by a long way.\n\nI went back and told him. You don't dress that kind of thing up.\n\nThen he asked for tusks. Bristleback tusks, sixty of them. I didn't argue the number. Grief keeps its own count, and Mankrik's came to sixty.\n\nHer name was Olgra. Somebody ought to say it."] = "Mankrik waits at the Crossroads. A big orc, built for a fight, and he'd lost one: quillboar hit him on the Gold Road, and his wife didn't come back from it. He asked me to find her. That was the whole of it. Find her.\n\nThe Barrens is a great deal of nothing in every direction, and I walked a lot of it. South of the Crossroads, I found her. Too late, by a long way.\n\nI went back and told him. You don't dress that kind of thing up.\n\nThen he asked for tusks. Bristleback tusks, sixty of them. I didn't argue the number. Grief keeps its own count, and Mankrik's came to sixty.\n\nHer name was Olgra. Somebody ought to say it."
L["High Executor Darthalia runs Tarren Mill like a war room. Down the hill, the humans of Hillsbrad still farm and trade as if the Forsaken weren't there. Darthalia means to change that, and the farms come first."] = "High Executor Darthalia runs Tarren Mill like a war room. Down the hill, the humans of Hillsbrad still farm and trade as if the Forsaken weren't there. Darthalia means to change that, and the farms come first."
L["Darthalia's list moves on to Hillsbrad itself: the blacksmith, the town hall, and the mine that keeps the town supplied. Report to the High Executor in Tarren Mill for the next set of orders."] = "Darthalia's list moves on to Hillsbrad itself: the blacksmith, the town hall, and the mine that keeps the town supplied. Report to the High Executor in Tarren Mill for the next set of orders."
L["Next on Darthalia's map is Dun Garok, the dwarven fort in the south of the foothills. Dwarves dig in deep and shoot straight, and these have had years of practice. The High Executor in Tarren Mill has your orders."] = "Next on Darthalia's map is Dun Garok, the dwarven fort in the south of the foothills. Dwarves dig in deep and shoot straight, and these have had years of practice. The High Executor in Tarren Mill has your orders."
L["It began with a quiet word outside the cathedral: Bishop DeLavey wanted to see me up in Stormwind Keep. From the way it was said, I gathered it wasn't about my soul.\n\nThe bishop told me exactly as much as I needed and not a word more. A diplomat had gone missing, and it was not to be talked about. A contact in the Valley of Heroes handed me a sealed note for Elling Trias, a man with a cheese shop and, it turns out, a good deal more than that. Trias read it, sealed up a note of his own, and sent me off to a watchman in Duskwood.\n\nThat's how you know it's serious. Nobody says anything out loud. They write it down, seal it, and make you carry it."] = "It began with a quiet word outside the cathedral: Bishop DeLavey wanted to see me up in Stormwind Keep. From the way it was said, I gathered it wasn't about my soul.\n\nThe bishop told me exactly as much as I needed and not a word more. A diplomat had gone missing, and it was not to be talked about. A contact in the Valley of Heroes handed me a sealed note for Elling Trias, a man with a cheese shop and, it turns out, a good deal more than that. Trias read it, sealed up a note of his own, and sent me off to a watchman in Duskwood.\n\nThat's how you know it's serious. Nobody says anything out loud. They write it down, seal it, and make you carry it."
L["Watcher Backus walks the road north of Darkshire. Backus read the note and sent me out to Addle's Stead, an old farm in western Duskwood, where a little house had some paperwork in it that the Defias wouldn't want found. The Defias Docket.\n\nHere's a thing about the Defias nobody warns you about. They keep records. Whether that's very organized or very stupid, I've never decided. It does make my job easier.\n\nBackus took one look at it and sent me straight back to Stormwind. Trias read it the way a man reads a bill he already knew was coming, and had my next stop picked out before he'd put it down."] = "Watcher Backus walks the road north of Darkshire. Backus read the note and sent me out to Addle's Stead, an old farm in western Duskwood, where a little house had some paperwork in it that the Defias wouldn't want found. The Defias Docket.\n\nHere's a thing about the Defias nobody warns you about. They keep records. Whether that's very organized or very stupid, I've never decided. It does make my job easier.\n\nBackus took one look at it and sent me straight back to Stormwind. Trias read it the way a man reads a bill he already knew was coming, and had my next stop picked out before he'd put it down."
L["The next lead was Dashel Stonefist, in Old Town. With a name like that, the conversation was always going to start with fists. Dashel didn't want to talk. Dashel wanted to settle things the old way, so we did, and when it was over Dashel was a good deal more reasonable.\n\nWhat Dashel told me sent me back to Trias, and Trias sent me out of the city altogether: north to the Wetlands, to Menethil Harbor and a contact there named Mikhail.\n\nI've always liked that part of a job. One minute you're trading punches in Old Town, the next you're on the road with a name in your pocket and a long way to go."] = "The next lead was Dashel Stonefist, in Old Town. With a name like that, the conversation was always going to start with fists. Dashel didn't want to talk. Dashel wanted to settle things the old way, so we did, and when it was over Dashel was a good deal more reasonable.\n\nWhat Dashel told me sent me back to Trias, and Trias sent me out of the city altogether: north to the Wetlands, to Menethil Harbor and a contact there named Mikhail.\n\nI've always liked that part of a job. One minute you're trading punches in Old Town, the next you're on the road with a name in your pocket and a long way to go."
L["Mikhail works out of the Deepwater Tavern in Menethil Harbor and notices things. Lately Mikhail had noticed a fellow who listened to other people's business a little too hard.\n\nThat was Tapoke Jahn. Slim, to his friends, if he has any. The moment Slim saw me coming, he did what guilty men always do. He ran. He didn't get far. I subdued him, and after that Slim was very keen to be helpful.\n\nSlim's confession pointed south, across the sea, to Theramore. So Mikhail sent me to find a commander in Dustwallow Marsh, and I took the next boat out."] = "Mikhail works out of the Deepwater Tavern in Menethil Harbor and notices things. Lately Mikhail had noticed a fellow who listened to other people's business a little too hard.\n\nThat was Tapoke Jahn. Slim, to his friends, if he has any. The moment Slim saw me coming, he did what guilty men always do. He ran. He didn't get far. I subdued him, and after that Slim was very keen to be helpful.\n\nSlim's confession pointed south, across the sea, to Theramore. So Mikhail sent me to find a commander in Dustwallow Marsh, and I took the next boat out."
L["Theramore is Jaina Proudmoore's city. The commander there had me search Sentry Point for any sign of a Private Hendel. No Hendel. When a soldier isn't at his post, that tells you plenty.\n\nAn archmage in Jaina's tower knew where to look instead: some tents out northwest of the city. Hendel was there, and Hendel was not in a talking mood, so we had it out. When he finally yielded, Lady Jaina Proudmoore herself was there to take him off my hands, with a quiet word of thanks for me.\n\nQuiet. That was the whole job, start to finish. I never did learn the diplomat's name. But considering who sent me looking and who turned up to say thank you, I have a fair idea."] = "Theramore is Jaina Proudmoore's city. The commander there had me search Sentry Point for any sign of a Private Hendel. No Hendel. When a soldier isn't at his post, that tells you plenty.\n\nAn archmage in Jaina's tower knew where to look instead: some tents out northwest of the city. Hendel was there, and Hendel was not in a talking mood, so we had it out. When he finally yielded, Lady Jaina Proudmoore herself was there to take him off my hands, with a quiet word of thanks for me.\n\nQuiet. That was the whole job, start to finish. I never did learn the diplomat's name. But considering who sent me looking and who turned up to say thank you, I have a fair idea."
L["If you want to understand Princess Moira, the Royal Historian in Ironforge will explain, and it takes a while. The Dark Irons, the War of the Three Hammers, and how the Burning Steppes came to be burning. Then, so I'd really understand, I was sent out to the Ruins of Thaurissan to read the old relics myself. History lessons, in the hottest classroom in the Eastern Kingdoms.\n\nThat was the warm-up. King Magni wanted me to find Kharan Mighthammer, a prisoner deep in Blackrock Depths. Kharan had a tale about Moira. I listened. It was not the tale Magni wanted.\n\nThen I had to go back to Ironforge and tell him. Here's some free advice. If you're ever handed bad news for a king about his daughter, see if anyone else is going that way."] = "If you want to understand Princess Moira, the Royal Historian in Ironforge will explain, and it takes a while. The Dark Irons, the War of the Three Hammers, and how the Burning Steppes came to be burning. Then, so I'd really understand, I was sent out to the Ruins of Thaurissan to read the old relics myself. History lessons, in the hottest classroom in the Eastern Kingdoms.\n\nThat was the warm-up. King Magni wanted me to find Kharan Mighthammer, a prisoner deep in Blackrock Depths. Kharan had a tale about Moira. I listened. It was not the tale Magni wanted.\n\nThen I had to go back to Ironforge and tell him. Here's some free advice. If you're ever handed bad news for a king about his daughter, see if anyone else is going that way."
L["Magni's orders were plain. Back into Blackrock Depths, kill Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, and bring Moira home.\n\nBlackrock Depths is a city, not a cellar, and the emperor sits at the far end of it. We got there. We did the first part. The emperor went down, and I turned around to rescue the princess.\n\nThe princess did not want rescuing. She'd married him, and she was mourning her husband. She also had news. She was carrying his child, and that child would be heir to Ironforge.\n\nThat news went back to her father by way of me. Of every report I've ever made, that's the one where I most wished I'd taken the long road. Magni wanted his daughter back. What he got was a grandchild, and a problem nobody could kill."] = "Magni's orders were plain. Back into Blackrock Depths, kill Emperor Dagran Thaurissan, and bring Moira home.\n\nBlackrock Depths is a city, not a cellar, and the emperor sits at the far end of it. We got there. We did the first part. The emperor went down, and I turned around to rescue the princess.\n\nThe princess did not want rescuing. She'd married him, and she was mourning her husband. She also had news. She was carrying his child, and that child would be heir to Ironforge.\n\nThat news went back to her father by way of me. Of every report I've ever made, that's the one where I most wished I'd taken the long road. Magni wanted his daughter back. What he got was a grandchild, and a problem nobody could kill."
L["I was shown a drawing of an orc. Crude, with bars scribbled over the face. That was the whole clue. So I went looking for a prison, and Blackrock Depths has a big one, and in it was Commander Gor'shak.\n\nThe Dark Irons didn't care for us talking, and halfway through they came at us in waves. I made sure the commander lived to finish the story. Another prisoner nearby had the real news: Princess Moira Bronzebeard, kidnapped by the Dark Irons.\n\nThrall heard me out and sent me back in to kill the emperor and free the princess from his spell.\n\nI did the killing. The princess did not look like anyone under a spell. She looked like a widow, carrying the emperor's child, an heir with a claim on Ironforge. I took that back to Orgrimmar and watched the Warchief do some very careful thinking."] = "I was shown a drawing of an orc. Crude, with bars scribbled over the face. That was the whole clue. So I went looking for a prison, and Blackrock Depths has a big one, and in it was Commander Gor'shak.\n\nThe Dark Irons didn't care for us talking, and halfway through they came at us in waves. I made sure the commander lived to finish the story. Another prisoner nearby had the real news: Princess Moira Bronzebeard, kidnapped by the Dark Irons.\n\nThrall heard me out and sent me back in to kill the emperor and free the princess from his spell.\n\nI did the killing. The princess did not look like anyone under a spell. She looked like a widow, carrying the emperor's child, an heir with a claim on Ironforge. I took that back to Orgrimmar and watched the Warchief do some very careful thinking."
L["Every warlock remembers their first voidwalker. Mine came out of the dark looking about as pleased as you'd expect.\n\nFirst my teacher sent me to fetch something the summoning couldn't be done without. Teachers like mine are never in a hurry to tell you why. I fetched it.\n\nThen the binding. You call, and something big and blue and hollow comes through and makes it plain it would rather not work for you. That's the part nobody can teach. You subdue it. You keep at it until it stops arguing and starts listening.\n\nIt's been with me ever since. Doesn't eat, doesn't sleep, doesn't talk. Stands between me and whatever's angry, takes the beating and never once asks about wages. I'd call it a friend, but I don't think it would know what to do with that."] = "Every warlock remembers their first voidwalker. Mine came out of the dark looking about as pleased as you'd expect.\n\nFirst my teacher sent me to fetch something the summoning couldn't be done without. Teachers like mine are never in a hurry to tell you why. I fetched it.\n\nThen the binding. You call, and something big and blue and hollow comes through and makes it plain it would rather not work for you. That's the part nobody can teach. You subdue it. You keep at it until it stops arguing and starts listening.\n\nIt's been with me ever since. Doesn't eat, doesn't sleep, doesn't talk. Stands between me and whatever's angry, takes the beating and never once asks about wages. I'd call it a friend, but I don't think it would know what to do with that."
L["Gakin the Darkbinder is a patient teacher, as long as you don't mind being sent across the sea for your lessons.\n\nA succubus needs the right focus, and Gakin sent me to Takar the Seer in the Barrens to learn what it was. Takar sent me north into Ashenvale for heartswood. I came back with it, and Gakin turned it into the Heartswood Core.\n\nThen the circle. I called, and a succubus answered. They always look you over first, as though they're the ones doing the choosing. She looked. I subdued her. We've had an understanding ever since.\n\nShe's charming, vicious and doesn't like me one bit, and I wouldn't trade her for any bodyguard in Stormwind. You always know where you stand with a demon. Usually it's slightly too close."] = "Gakin the Darkbinder is a patient teacher, as long as you don't mind being sent across the sea for your lessons.\n\nA succubus needs the right focus, and Gakin sent me to Takar the Seer in the Barrens to learn what it was. Takar sent me north into Ashenvale for heartswood. I came back with it, and Gakin turned it into the Heartswood Core.\n\nThen the circle. I called, and a succubus answered. They always look you over first, as though they're the ones doing the choosing. She looked. I subdued her. We've had an understanding ever since.\n\nShe's charming, vicious and doesn't like me one bit, and I wouldn't trade her for any bodyguard in Stormwind. You always know where you stand with a demon. Usually it's slightly too close."
L["For a succubus you need hearts. Pure ones. That narrows the field more than you'd think.\n\nGodrick Farsan in the Temple of the Damned gave me two names: Dalin Forgewright and Corma Villard. I came back with their hearts. I didn't ask what made them pure, and they weren't in a position to tell me.\n\nThen the circle. I called, and a succubus came through and looked me up and down as though she were the one doing the hiring. They all do, the first time. I subdued her, which is the polite word for it, and since then she's been the most charming thing in my employ.\n\nPeople ask whether she's dangerous. Of course she is. That's rather the point of her."] = "For a succubus you need hearts. Pure ones. That narrows the field more than you'd think.\n\nGodrick Farsan in the Temple of the Damned gave me two names: Dalin Forgewright and Corma Villard. I came back with their hearts. I didn't ask what made them pure, and they weren't in a position to tell me.\n\nThen the circle. I called, and a succubus came through and looked me up and down as though she were the one doing the hiring. They all do, the first time. I subdued her, which is the polite word for it, and since then she's been the most charming thing in my employ.\n\nPeople ask whether she's dangerous. Of course she is. That's rather the point of her."
L["A felhunter eats magic. That's the whole appeal. The trouble is you need a book to bind one, and the book was in pieces.\n\nStrahad Farsan down in Ratchet knew the binding, and Krom Stoutarm back in Ironforge knew books, and I spent a good while carrying scraps of moldy paper between the two of them. Eventually Krom had the thing put back together, and Strahad put the Tome of the Cabal in my hands.\n\nI drew the circle, and a felhunter came through starving, and I am, to a felhunter, a walking supper. I got the binding on it before it got a taste.\n\nNow it trots at my heel and sniffs at every mage we pass. I keep my own spellbooks off the floor."] = "A felhunter eats magic. That's the whole appeal. The trouble is you need a book to bind one, and the book was in pieces.\n\nStrahad Farsan down in Ratchet knew the binding, and Krom Stoutarm back in Ironforge knew books, and I spent a good while carrying scraps of moldy paper between the two of them. Eventually Krom had the thing put back together, and Strahad put the Tome of the Cabal in my hands.\n\nI drew the circle, and a felhunter came through starving, and I am, to a felhunter, a walking supper. I got the binding on it before it got a taste.\n\nNow it trots at my heel and sniffs at every mage we pass. I keep my own spellbooks off the floor."
L["A felhunter takes the Tome of the Cabal, and the Tome of the Cabal, naturally, was in pieces.\n\nStrahad Farsan down in Ratchet knew the binding, and Jorah Annison back in the Undercity knew books, and I carried scraps of moldy paper between the two of them until I knew that road better than some caravans do. Eventually Jorah had the thing put back together, and Strahad handed it over.\n\nI drew the circle, and a felhunter came through hungry. Magic is what they eat, and I'm made of the stuff. I got the binding on it before it got a bite.\n\nNow it walks at my heel and makes mages nervous. I count that in its favor."] = "A felhunter takes the Tome of the Cabal, and the Tome of the Cabal, naturally, was in pieces.\n\nStrahad Farsan down in Ratchet knew the binding, and Jorah Annison back in the Undercity knew books, and I carried scraps of moldy paper between the two of them until I knew that road better than some caravans do. Eventually Jorah had the thing put back together, and Strahad handed it over.\n\nI drew the circle, and a felhunter came through hungry. Magic is what they eat, and I'm made of the stuff. I got the binding on it before it got a bite.\n\nNow it walks at my heel and makes mages nervous. I count that in its favor."
L["Most people buy a horse. Warlocks get sent to Ratchet.\n\nMy trainer pointed me back down to Strahad Farsan, who by then was practically family, to learn to summon a felsteed. It isn't bought and it isn't bred. You call it, it comes, and it carries you, and it would very much like everybody to know it's from somewhere worse than here.\n\nI've never named mine. It wouldn't answer to it anyway. But it doesn't tire, it doesn't spook, and it has never once wanted a stable or a bucket of oats. The hooves are on fire, mind. Keep it away from anybody's haystack.\n\nA warlock's mount ought to frighten people a little. It saves on conversation."] = "Most people buy a horse. Warlocks get sent to Ratchet.\n\nMy trainer pointed me back down to Strahad Farsan, who by then was practically family, to learn to summon a felsteed. It isn't bought and it isn't bred. You call it, it comes, and it carries you, and it would very much like everybody to know it's from somewhere worse than here.\n\nI've never named mine. It wouldn't answer to it anyway. But it doesn't tire, it doesn't spook, and it has never once wanted a stable or a bucket of oats. The hooves are on fire, mind. Keep it away from anybody's haystack.\n\nA warlock's mount ought to frighten people a little. It saves on conversation."
L["Anyone can call a felsteed. A dreadsteed of Xoroth you earn, and Mor'zul Bloodbringer in the Burning Steppes made very sure I knew it.\n\nThe shopping list for that ritual would make a dragon weep, partly because thirty-five black dragonscales were on it. But my favorite errand was the imp. Gorzeeki Wildeyes handed me an imp in a jar and had me take it to school. The Scholomance, I mean. It needed their alchemy lab to make a parchment. Only imp I know with an education.\n\nAt the end, in Dire Maul, I set it all out and called. The dreadsteed came through furious. I put it down, and when its spirit rose, I bound it.\n\nIt's still cross about that. We get along fine."] = "Anyone can call a felsteed. A dreadsteed of Xoroth you earn, and Mor'zul Bloodbringer in the Burning Steppes made very sure I knew it.\n\nThe shopping list for that ritual would make a dragon weep, partly because thirty-five black dragonscales were on it. But my favorite errand was the imp. Gorzeeki Wildeyes handed me an imp in a jar and had me take it to school. The Scholomance, I mean. It needed their alchemy lab to make a parchment. Only imp I know with an education.\n\nAt the end, in Dire Maul, I set it all out and called. The dreadsteed came through furious. I put it down, and when its spirit rose, I bound it.\n\nIt's still cross about that. We get along fine."
L["There's a wanted poster outside Westbrook Garrison. A huge gnoll called Hogger is prowling the woods of southwestern Elwynn, and he has beaten every attempt to catch him. Marshal Dughan in Goldshire pays the bounty."] = "There's a wanted poster outside Westbrook Garrison. A huge gnoll called Hogger is prowling the woods of southwestern Elwynn, and he has beaten every attempt to catch him. Marshal Dughan in Goldshire pays the bounty."
L["Every hunter starts the same way. Your trainer hands you a rod, points at something with teeth and tells you to go and make friends.\n\nThe Taming Rod does half the talking. You get close, you hold steady, and if you've got it right the beast stops trying to eat you and starts following you about instead. Then you work together a while, so you both learn the job. I did that three times, with three different beasts, and handed the rod back each time with all my fingers.\n\nAfter that I was sent to a proper trainer to learn how to keep a beast of my own. I've had a partner at my side ever since. Finds the trail, takes the first bite, holds them still while I shoot. I get paid. It gets fed. Best bargain either of us ever made."] = "Every hunter starts the same way. Your trainer hands you a rod, points at something with teeth and tells you to go and make friends.\n\nThe Taming Rod does half the talking. You get close, you hold steady, and if you've got it right the beast stops trying to eat you and starts following you about instead. Then you work together a while, so you both learn the job. I did that three times, with three different beasts, and handed the rod back each time with all my fingers.\n\nAfter that I was sent to a proper trainer to learn how to keep a beast of my own. I've had a partner at my side ever since. Finds the trail, takes the first bite, holds them still while I shoot. I get paid. It gets fed. Best bargain either of us ever made."
L["Out of Molten Core, if you're lucky, comes a leaf. Ancient Petrified Leaf. Looks like nothing. Its owner turned out to be Vartrus, an ancient up in Felwood, and Vartrus wanted four demons dead.\n\nThey weren't hard to kill. They were hard to find, because they were living among ordinary folk under friendly names. Artorius the Amiable, for one. Very amiable. Less so after the first arrow.\n\nIt had to be done alone, the ancients said. My pet and I agreed the pet didn't count. The pet did most of the tracking, so that seemed only fair.\n\nThe other two ancients wanted sinew from a grown black dragon and a grown blue one. Neither dragon was in a giving mood. What I got for it all was Rhok'delar, the longbow of the Ancient Keepers. My pet still hasn't forgiven me for not getting one too."] = "Out of Molten Core, if you're lucky, comes a leaf. Ancient Petrified Leaf. Looks like nothing. Its owner turned out to be Vartrus, an ancient up in Felwood, and Vartrus wanted four demons dead.\n\nThey weren't hard to kill. They were hard to find, because they were living among ordinary folk under friendly names. Artorius the Amiable, for one. Very amiable. Less so after the first arrow.\n\nIt had to be done alone, the ancients said. My pet and I agreed the pet didn't count. The pet did most of the tracking, so that seemed only fair.\n\nThe other two ancients wanted sinew from a grown black dragon and a grown blue one. Neither dragon was in a giving mood. What I got for it all was Rhok'delar, the longbow of the Ancient Keepers. My pet still hasn't forgiven me for not getting one too."
L["The blue shard hid grave purpose beneath impossible errands. Azuregos sent you to Narain, and Narain's ledger demanded Molten Core goggles, a great chimaerok meal, a stolen book, a ransom drop, pages from dragons, the fires of Onyxia and Ragnaros, and Neptulon's wrath off Azshara's coast. Strange as it was, each step pulled the shard closer to the scepter."] = "The blue shard hid grave purpose beneath impossible errands. Azuregos sent you to Narain, and Narain's ledger demanded Molten Core goggles, a great chimaerok meal, a stolen book, a ransom drop, pages from dragons, the fires of Onyxia and Ragnaros, and Neptulon's wrath off Azshara's coast. Strange as it was, each step pulled the shard closer to the scepter."
L["My training started in Deathknell, under Aramis Hammerhand. What I learned there: the Light doesn't care what you were, only what you do with it. Also, it takes some getting used to.\n\nAramis put me to work on the injured Deathguard. Five of them, five times the Holy Light, five soldiers back on their feet. Not glamorous. It's the job, and I've always liked the job.\n\nThen there was a frightened paladin in Deathknell, a brother of the order who couldn't make his peace with what he'd become. I didn't have a speech ready. I offered what help I could and hoped it was a start. Some wounds the Light reaches slowly.\n\nAfter that, Aramis pointed me down the road to Brill, where Shari Stilwell was waiting to take over my education. I'm good at going where I'm pointed. It's one of my better qualities, and I have several."] = "My training started in Deathknell, under Aramis Hammerhand. What I learned there: the Light doesn't care what you were, only what you do with it. Also, it takes some getting used to.\n\nAramis put me to work on the injured Deathguard. Five of them, five times the Holy Light, five soldiers back on their feet. Not glamorous. It's the job, and I've always liked the job.\n\nThen there was a frightened paladin in Deathknell, a brother of the order who couldn't make his peace with what he'd become. I didn't have a speech ready. I offered what help I could and hoped it was a start. Some wounds the Light reaches slowly.\n\nAfter that, Aramis pointed me down the road to Brill, where Shari Stilwell was waiting to take over my education. I'm good at going where I'm pointed. It's one of my better qualities, and I have several."
L["Bandarion Keep sits at the far western edge of Tirisfal, where a company of paladins is building a home for itself. Old stone, good walls, and murlocs at the gate. We sorted out the murlocs first. You can't make anywhere a home with murlocs at the gate.\n\nDanitha Morr showed me round and told me what the place could become, and I believed every word. Not everyone in paladin's armor out there is a friend, though. The Tarnished still turn their blades on their own kind. I dealt with a good many of them, and then with their commander, Rudolph Gelhardt.\n\nDanitha had me carry Gelhardt's head to Sylvanas Windrunner, a token of good faith from an order she barely trusts. I'm not sure a severed head builds much trust. Still, it's the thought that counts."] = "Bandarion Keep sits at the far western edge of Tirisfal, where a company of paladins is building a home for itself. Old stone, good walls, and murlocs at the gate. We sorted out the murlocs first. You can't make anywhere a home with murlocs at the gate.\n\nDanitha Morr showed me round and told me what the place could become, and I believed every word. Not everyone in paladin's armor out there is a friend, though. The Tarnished still turn their blades on their own kind. I dealt with a good many of them, and then with their commander, Rudolph Gelhardt.\n\nDanitha had me carry Gelhardt's head to Sylvanas Windrunner, a token of good faith from an order she barely trusts. I'm not sure a severed head builds much trust. Still, it's the thought that counts."
L["Danitha Morr holds that a paladin does good because it's good, not because anybody's watching. To make the point, I was sent to the Undercity to help Tanis Alderwood.\n\nTanis needed linen. Ten pieces, to hold his wife together, because she was failing and he was doing what he could. He couldn't work out why a paladin would bother with something that small. Ten linen is a small thing. That was rather the point.\n\nThe bigger lesson came with the Symbol of Life. Out at Venomweb Vale, Deathguard Falgan lay dead for the second time, and I brought him back. The Scarlet Crusade were camped right there with their attack plans for Brill, so I took those too, and the Deathguard were ready before the blow fell.\n\nLinen for a stranger, plans off the Crusade. Turns out it's the same lesson."] = "Danitha Morr holds that a paladin does good because it's good, not because anybody's watching. To make the point, I was sent to the Undercity to help Tanis Alderwood.\n\nTanis needed linen. Ten pieces, to hold his wife together, because she was failing and he was doing what he could. He couldn't work out why a paladin would bother with something that small. Ten linen is a small thing. That was rather the point.\n\nThe bigger lesson came with the Symbol of Life. Out at Venomweb Vale, Deathguard Falgan lay dead for the second time, and I brought him back. The Scarlet Crusade were camped right there with their attack plans for Brill, so I took those too, and the Deathguard were ready before the blow fell.\n\nLinen for a stranger, plans off the Crusade. Turns out it's the same lesson."
L["Two Earthen Ring travelers had gone missing in Silverpine. The keep saw a chance to prove itself to the Horde. I saw two people who needed finding. Either way, I went.\n\nFenris Isle held half the answer. One of the travelers lay dead. The other, Lumina Windsinger, was locked up in Fenris Keep. I took the key off the gnolls, opened the door, and we fought our way out together. Lumina is a Windshaper. You should see what a Windshaper does to a gnoll.\n\nA worgen had struck them on the road, Lumina said, and one of those beasts would not stay dead. That calls for a special sort of blade. Ott in Tarren Mill forged it while I stood by, and I'll say this for Ott: the talent is badly underrated.\n\nThen I went looking for Old Fire-Eye. Some things will stay dead if you ask them properly."] = "Two Earthen Ring travelers had gone missing in Silverpine. The keep saw a chance to prove itself to the Horde. I saw two people who needed finding. Either way, I went.\n\nFenris Isle held half the answer. One of the travelers lay dead. The other, Lumina Windsinger, was locked up in Fenris Keep. I took the key off the gnolls, opened the door, and we fought our way out together. Lumina is a Windshaper. You should see what a Windshaper does to a gnoll.\n\nA worgen had struck them on the road, Lumina said, and one of those beasts would not stay dead. That calls for a special sort of blade. Ott in Tarren Mill forged it while I stood by, and I'll say this for Ott: the talent is badly underrated.\n\nThen I went looking for Old Fire-Eye. Some things will stay dead if you ask them properly."
L["Duthorian Rall teaches at the Cathedral of Light, and Rall's view is that having the Light with you is the easy part. The hard part is what you do when evil walks into your lands and you're the one standing there.\n\nSo Rall sent me to Westfall, to Daphne Stilwell, and the Defias picked that very afternoon to come for her. They came in waves. The rule was simple: she lives and I live, or the test is failed. Nobody gets to fall over and try again.\n\nI won't pretend it was tidy. The Defias don't fight fair, and there were more of them than I'd have liked. But I stayed in front of her and kept the Light going, and when it was over we were both still standing.\n\nThat, apparently, is valor. I'd have called it a long afternoon. I suppose it can be both."] = "Duthorian Rall teaches at the Cathedral of Light, and Rall's view is that having the Light with you is the easy part. The hard part is what you do when evil walks into your lands and you're the one standing there.\n\nSo Rall sent me to Westfall, to Daphne Stilwell, and the Defias picked that very afternoon to come for her. They came in waves. The rule was simple: she lives and I live, or the test is failed. Nobody gets to fall over and try again.\n\nI won't pretend it was tidy. The Defias don't fight fair, and there were more of them than I'd have liked. But I stayed in front of her and kept the Light going, and when it was over we were both still standing.\n\nThat, apparently, is valor. I'd have called it a long afternoon. I suppose it can be both."
L["Jordan Stilwell works a forge outside the gates of Ironforge, and Jordan's idea of a test is a shopping list. Lumber, ore, a Kor gem, and Jordan's own smithing hammer, which was somehow anywhere but with Jordan.\n\nI'll spare you the roads. There were a great many roads. The gem alone came to me corrupted and had to go all the way to Darkshore to be made clean.\n\nBut I stood by the anvil when it all came together, and I watched Jordan make Verigan's Fist. They say the Light is in all honest work. I'd believed that in a general sort of way. Standing at that forge, I believed it properly.\n\nRighteousness, it turns out, is mostly carrying things and not complaining. I managed one of the two."] = "Jordan Stilwell works a forge outside the gates of Ironforge, and Jordan's idea of a test is a shopping list. Lumber, ore, a Kor gem, and Jordan's own smithing hammer, which was somehow anywhere but with Jordan.\n\nI'll spare you the roads. There were a great many roads. The gem alone came to me corrupted and had to go all the way to Darkshore to be made clean.\n\nBut I stood by the anvil when it all came together, and I watched Jordan make Verigan's Fist. They say the Light is in all honest work. I'd believed that in a general sort of way. Standing at that forge, I believed it properly.\n\nRighteousness, it turns out, is mostly carrying things and not complaining. I managed one of the two."
L["Danitha Morr called me home to Bandarion Keep, and for once it wasn't to send me off somewhere else. It was to give me something.\n\nA paladin's warhorse isn't bought at a stable. It's consecrated for battle, and it comes when you call. Danitha taught me how. The first time mine appeared, I just stood and looked at it for a while. They say a paladin's steed is as fearless as the paladin it carries. In my experience, the horse has the better claim.\n\nI've walked a great many roads in the Light's service, and I'd walk every one of them again. I'd simply rather not, now that there's a horse."] = "Danitha Morr called me home to Bandarion Keep, and for once it wasn't to send me off somewhere else. It was to give me something.\n\nA paladin's warhorse isn't bought at a stable. It's consecrated for battle, and it comes when you call. Danitha taught me how. The first time mine appeared, I just stood and looked at it for a while. They say a paladin's steed is as fearless as the paladin it carries. In my experience, the horse has the better claim.\n\nI've walked a great many roads in the Light's service, and I'd walk every one of them again. I'd simply rather not, now that there's a horse."
L["Lord Grayson Shadowbreaker doesn't hand out horses. A paladin's charger is a spirit, and you earn it.\n\nYou also pay for it, which nobody mentions at the start. A hundred and fifty gold for a censer to cleanse Terrordale. A hundred and fifty more for barding. Fifty more to Merideth Carlson in Southshore for the horse feed, that fifty being to soothe her ruffled sensibilities. Nobody had told me sensibilities came so dear.\n\nThe part worth telling happened under Scholomance, in the Great Ossuary. I used the scryer, judged the spirits it raised, and then Death Knight Darkreaver came for me. He had the soul of a fallen charger, and he did not intend to give it up. He gave it up.\n\nThe charger came to me redeemed. Three hundred and fifty gold, all told. Worth every coin."] = "Lord Grayson Shadowbreaker doesn't hand out horses. A paladin's charger is a spirit, and you earn it.\n\nYou also pay for it, which nobody mentions at the start. A hundred and fifty gold for a censer to cleanse Terrordale. A hundred and fifty more for barding. Fifty more to Merideth Carlson in Southshore for the horse feed, that fifty being to soothe her ruffled sensibilities. Nobody had told me sensibilities came so dear.\n\nThe part worth telling happened under Scholomance, in the Great Ossuary. I used the scryer, judged the spirits it raised, and then Death Knight Darkreaver came for me. He had the soul of a fallen charger, and he did not intend to give it up. He gave it up.\n\nThe charger came to me redeemed. Three hundred and fifty gold, all told. Worth every coin."
L["A charger is a spirit, not a purchase. Nobody mentions the bill.\n\nA hundred and fifty gold for a censer to cleanse Terrordale. A hundred and fifty more to Ott for the barding, and worth it. Then fifty to Nathanos Blightcaller for the horse feed, the fifty being for his time. Nathanos values his time very highly.\n\nThe part worth telling happened under Scholomance, in the Great Ossuary. I used the scryer, judged the spirits it raised, and then Death Knight Darkreaver came for me. He was holding the soul of a fallen charger. The rite's last step is called Justice and Retribution, and I gave Darkreaver plenty of both.\n\nThe charger answered me after that. It has every time since."] = "A charger is a spirit, not a purchase. Nobody mentions the bill.\n\nA hundred and fifty gold for a censer to cleanse Terrordale. A hundred and fifty more to Ott for the barding, and worth it. Then fifty to Nathanos Blightcaller for the horse feed, the fifty being for his time. Nathanos values his time very highly.\n\nThe part worth telling happened under Scholomance, in the Great Ossuary. I used the scryer, judged the spirits it raised, and then Death Knight Darkreaver came for me. He was holding the soul of a fallen charger. The rite's last step is called Justice and Retribution, and I gave Darkreaver plenty of both.\n\nThe charger answered me after that. It has every time since."
L["Nobody in the Circle is ever in a hurry, and the spirits are worse. I was sent to sit with the great spirit of the bear, and it took its time telling me what a bear is. Strong, mostly. I had guessed that part, but it's polite to let a spirit finish.\n\nThe trial that followed had claws. There was a beast to face, and it was not interested in my reasons. I won. Winning, it turned out, was the easy half. The other half was what the spirit had been getting at all along: strength is for something. A bear doesn't brawl for the fun of it. It stands between its own and the world, and then it goes back to its berries.\n\nI came home able to wear that shape whenever I like. People step out of your way more. I find it very restful."] = "Nobody in the Circle is ever in a hurry, and the spirits are worse. I was sent to sit with the great spirit of the bear, and it took its time telling me what a bear is. Strong, mostly. I had guessed that part, but it's polite to let a spirit finish.\n\nThe trial that followed had claws. There was a beast to face, and it was not interested in my reasons. I won. Winning, it turned out, was the easy half. The other half was what the spirit had been getting at all along: strength is for something. A bear doesn't brawl for the fun of it. It stands between its own and the world, and then it goes back to its berries.\n\nI came home able to wear that shape whenever I like. People step out of your way more. I find it very restful."
L["The Circle never hands you a spell. It hands you a problem and sits back to see what you'll do with it.\n\nThis time it was the deer of Darkshore, sickening up and down the coast. I went up to Cliffspring Falls to find where it began, and Alanndarian Nightsong in Auberdine turned what I brought back into a salve.\n\nThen I spent a slow, pleasant afternoon walking Darkshore from the river nearly to Ashenvale, finding sick deer and putting salve on them. Ten in all. Not one of them said thank you. Each got up, gave me a look as though I'd been rude, and wandered off into the trees.\n\nHealthy, though. Gratitude is a people habit. Deer just get on with things, and the older I get, the more I think they have the right idea."] = "The Circle never hands you a spell. It hands you a problem and sits back to see what you'll do with it.\n\nThis time it was the deer of Darkshore, sickening up and down the coast. I went up to Cliffspring Falls to find where it began, and Alanndarian Nightsong in Auberdine turned what I brought back into a salve.\n\nThen I spent a slow, pleasant afternoon walking Darkshore from the river nearly to Ashenvale, finding sick deer and putting salve on them. Ten in all. Not one of them said thank you. Each got up, gave me a look as though I'd been rude, and wandered off into the trees.\n\nHealthy, though. Gratitude is a people habit. Deer just get on with things, and the older I get, the more I think they have the right idea."
L["Druids learn healing the way everyone learns anything: somebody's sick, and you're the one standing there.\n\nThe somebody was the gazelles of the northern Barrens. I climbed Dreadmist Peak to find where the sickness began, and Tonga Runetotem at the Crossroads set about making a salve. He needed kodo horns. The kodos had a view on that. We came to an arrangement.\n\nThen I spent a long, warm day north of the Crossroads, chasing sick gazelles and dabbing salve on them. Ten in all. Have you ever tried to doctor a gazelle? They're quick even when they're poorly. Not one stopped to thank me. They got up and ran.\n\nWhich is the point, really. A gazelle that runs is a gazelle that's well."] = "Druids learn healing the way everyone learns anything: somebody's sick, and you're the one standing there.\n\nThe somebody was the gazelles of the northern Barrens. I climbed Dreadmist Peak to find where the sickness began, and Tonga Runetotem at the Crossroads set about making a salve. He needed kodo horns. The kodos had a view on that. We came to an arrangement.\n\nThen I spent a long, warm day north of the Crossroads, chasing sick gazelles and dabbing salve on them. Ten in all. Have you ever tried to doctor a gazelle? They're quick even when they're poorly. Not one stopped to thank me. They got up and ran.\n\nWhich is the point, really. A gazelle that runs is a gazelle that's well."
L["Moonglade is the one place in the world where nobody rushes you, so naturally it's where they teach you to swim.\n\nThe first trial meant hunting the bottom of Lake Elune'ara for a shrine bauble and carrying it up to the Shrine of Remulos. Tajarri was waiting there to tell me I'd passed. I was too waterlogged to argue.\n\nThe second was the Pendant of the Sea Lion, which comes in two halves, and nobody would simply tell me where they were. You ask the folk of Moonglade, and they give you a hint and a kind smile and go back to whatever they were doing. Druids. I'm allowed to say that.\n\nI found both halves and brought the pendant to Dendrite Starblaze, and I've had the sea lion's shape ever since. Finest way to travel I know. No boats, no docks, and nobody asks you for a fare."] = "Moonglade is the one place in the world where nobody rushes you, so naturally it's where they teach you to swim.\n\nThe first trial meant hunting the bottom of Lake Elune'ara for a shrine bauble and carrying it up to the Shrine of Remulos. Tajarri was waiting there to tell me I'd passed. I was too waterlogged to argue.\n\nThe second was the Pendant of the Sea Lion, which comes in two halves, and nobody would simply tell me where they were. You ask the folk of Moonglade, and they give you a hint and a kind smile and go back to whatever they were doing. Druids. I'm allowed to say that.\n\nI found both halves and brought the pendant to Dendrite Starblaze, and I've had the sea lion's shape ever since. Finest way to travel I know. No boats, no docks, and nobody asks you for a fare."
L["Dendrite Starblaze called me back to Moonglade to meet a cat. The spirit of one, anyway, up in the southern hills, and cats, as everybody knows, don't teach just anybody.\n\nBefore it would say much, it wanted three relics: the Fang, the Claw and the Silent Shadow. All three were down in the Stormrage Barrow Dens, a long, dim place where it pays to be quiet. I went in, came out with all three and laid them in front of the spirit. It looked at them for a good long while. Cats do.\n\nThen it gave me its blessing, and the shape that goes with it: soft feet, sharp claws and the knack of not being noticed. Dendrite didn't look the least bit surprised. I suspect the whole thing had been arranged before I ever arrived."] = "Dendrite Starblaze called me back to Moonglade to meet a cat. The spirit of one, anyway, up in the southern hills, and cats, as everybody knows, don't teach just anybody.\n\nBefore it would say much, it wanted three relics: the Fang, the Claw and the Silent Shadow. All three were down in the Stormrage Barrow Dens, a long, dim place where it pays to be quiet. I went in, came out with all three and laid them in front of the spirit. It looked at them for a good long while. Cats do.\n\nThen it gave me its blessing, and the shape that goes with it: soft feet, sharp claws and the knack of not being noticed. Dendrite didn't look the least bit surprised. I suspect the whole thing had been arranged before I ever arrived."
L["You'd be amazed what pirates leave lying around. Aboard one of the Bloodsail ships off Stranglethorn's Wild Shore, I came across a little scroll with a riddle on it. Cortello's, it said. No idea who Cortello was. Still haven't.\n\nNow, I know better than to trust verse I find on a pirate ship. But nobody writes a riddle about nothing. There's always something at the bottom of it worth hiding, and I had time on my hands.\n\nI worked it out, more or less, and it sent me all the way to the Swamp of Sorrows, to the first wooden bridge west of Stonard. Under the bridge, in the muck, another scroll. Soggy, of course. It had been living under a bridge in a swamp. I'd be soggy too."] = "You'd be amazed what pirates leave lying around. Aboard one of the Bloodsail ships off Stranglethorn's Wild Shore, I came across a little scroll with a riddle on it. Cortello's, it said. No idea who Cortello was. Still haven't.\n\nNow, I know better than to trust verse I find on a pirate ship. But nobody writes a riddle about nothing. There's always something at the bottom of it worth hiding, and I had time on my hands.\n\nI worked it out, more or less, and it sent me all the way to the Swamp of Sorrows, to the first wooden bridge west of Stonard. Under the bridge, in the muck, another scroll. Soggy, of course. It had been living under a bridge in a swamp. I'd be soggy too."
L["The soggy scroll had another riddle on it, which by then I'd come to expect, and this one wanted me in Dustwallow Marsh. Over the sea. For a bit of paper. I did wonder on the boat what I was doing with myself.\n\nBloodfen Burrow is raptor country, and the Bloodfen raptors are not shy. They treat anybody poking around their home as dinner that's had the decency to walk in. I'll give Cortello this much: nobody was going to stumble on that hiding place by accident.\n\nDown among the raptors, sure enough, there was another scrap. Musty, this one, like it had been waiting a long time for somebody stubborn enough to turn up. I'm very stubborn about treasure."] = "The soggy scroll had another riddle on it, which by then I'd come to expect, and this one wanted me in Dustwallow Marsh. Over the sea. For a bit of paper. I did wonder on the boat what I was doing with myself.\n\nBloodfen Burrow is raptor country, and the Bloodfen raptors are not shy. They treat anybody poking around their home as dinner that's had the decency to walk in. I'll give Cortello this much: nobody was going to stumble on that hiding place by accident.\n\nDown among the raptors, sure enough, there was another scrap. Musty, this one, like it had been waiting a long time for somebody stubborn enough to turn up. I'm very stubborn about treasure."
L["The musty scroll was the last of them, and it pointed at the Hinterlands, at the great waterfall on the eastern coast. By then I'd boarded a pirate ship, crawled under a bridge and walked into a raptor den on the strength of a stranger's handwriting. You'd think I'd have learned something. I had not.\n\nUnder the falls, there it was: Cortello's treasure. An actual chest, the kind you hope for as a child and stop believing in once you're grown.\n\nI won't tell you what was in it. Not because it was disappointing. Because I've told this story enough times to know that once you say what's in the chest, everyone stops listening. Cortello paid well for the trouble. Leave it at that."] = "The musty scroll was the last of them, and it pointed at the Hinterlands, at the great waterfall on the eastern coast. By then I'd boarded a pirate ship, crawled under a bridge and walked into a raptor den on the strength of a stranger's handwriting. You'd think I'd have learned something. I had not.\n\nUnder the falls, there it was: Cortello's treasure. An actual chest, the kind you hope for as a child and stop believing in once you're grown.\n\nI won't tell you what was in it. Not because it was disappointing. Because I've told this story enough times to know that once you say what's in the chest, everyone stops listening. Cortello paid well for the trouble. Leave it at that."
L["The green shard was not won by simple killing. Eranikus had fallen into nightmare, and Remulos needed the corruption named before it could be answered. The defense of Moonglade became a rite before all who came to stand there: shades pouring from the Dream, Remulos holding the line, and Tyrande's grace turning a dragon's rage toward redemption."] = "The green shard was not won by simple killing. Eranikus had fallen into nightmare, and Remulos needed the corruption named before it could be answered. The defense of Moonglade became a rite before all who came to stand there: shades pouring from the Dream, Remulos holding the line, and Tyrande's grace turning a dragon's rage toward redemption."
L["Warriors learn to hit first. Then they learn to get hit. The second one keeps you alive.\n\nMy first trial was a job, and the job was a fight. It always is. I won, and I brought back what they'd asked for. Nothing clever. Hit the thing, carry the thing, come home.\n\nThen the lesson. Feet set. Shield up. Weight low. Let the blow land where you can take it. You hit softer standing like that. Doesn't matter. The other side tires first.\n\nI hated it at first. Standing still feels like losing. Then I took a few hits in that stance that would have dropped me flat a week before, and I was still up. I came round fast after that.\n\nPeople think a warrior is all swing. The good ones are mostly stand."] = "Warriors learn to hit first. Then they learn to get hit. The second one keeps you alive.\n\nMy first trial was a job, and the job was a fight. It always is. I won, and I brought back what they'd asked for. Nothing clever. Hit the thing, carry the thing, come home.\n\nThen the lesson. Feet set. Shield up. Weight low. Let the blow land where you can take it. You hit softer standing like that. Doesn't matter. The other side tires first.\n\nI hated it at first. Standing still feels like losing. Then I took a few hits in that stance that would have dropped me flat a week before, and I was still up. I came round fast after that.\n\nPeople think a warrior is all swing. The good ones are mostly stand."
L["Here's how I got this.\n\nThe Islander runs a fighting pit on Fray Island, out in the Barrens. You step on the grate and they come at you, one after another, and then Big Will comes. Big Will is well named. I put Big Will on the ground.\n\nThat got me sent to Bath'rah the Windwatcher, up in the Alterac hills. Bath'rah wanted charms and tusks and roots, a cauldron's worth, all to call up an air elemental named Cyclonian.\n\nCyclonian came. Cyclonian is a storm with a temper. I hit it until it stopped being a storm, and I took its heart.\n\nBath'rah set the heart into the weapon I chose. Now it spins. So does anybody standing too close."] = "Here's how I got this.\n\nThe Islander runs a fighting pit on Fray Island, out in the Barrens. You step on the grate and they come at you, one after another, and then Big Will comes. Big Will is well named. I put Big Will on the ground.\n\nThat got me sent to Bath'rah the Windwatcher, up in the Alterac hills. Bath'rah wanted charms and tusks and roots, a cauldron's worth, all to call up an air elemental named Cyclonian.\n\nCyclonian came. Cyclonian is a storm with a temper. I hit it until it stopped being a storm, and I took its heart.\n\nBath'rah set the heart into the weapon I chose. Now it spins. So does anybody standing too close."
L["Nimboya had the kind of problem nobody at Grom'gol wanted to say out loud. The Darkspear chief's youngest son, Yenniku, was gone, and the Bloodscalps were the only trail anyone had.\n\nThe Bloodscalps don't take questions, so I started by collecting. Shrunken heads, twenty of them, so Nimboya could go through every one and make sure none of them was Yenniku. That's a job you remember. None of them was.\n\nOnly the Bloodscalp leaders knew more, and they weren't sharing. Nimboya had an answer for that as well. I brought back both their heads and dropped them in the cauldron at camp.\n\nTurns out a Bloodscalp gets a lot chattier once you've parted the talking end from the rest."] = "Nimboya had the kind of problem nobody at Grom'gol wanted to say out loud. The Darkspear chief's youngest son, Yenniku, was gone, and the Bloodscalps were the only trail anyone had.\n\nThe Bloodscalps don't take questions, so I started by collecting. Shrunken heads, twenty of them, so Nimboya could go through every one and make sure none of them was Yenniku. That's a job you remember. None of them was.\n\nOnly the Bloodscalp leaders knew more, and they weren't sharing. Nimboya had an answer for that as well. I brought back both their heads and dropped them in the cauldron at camp.\n\nTurns out a Bloodscalp gets a lot chattier once you've parted the talking end from the rest."
L["Here's something nobody tells you about talking heads. They haggle.\n\nNezzliok's head spoke up from the cauldron first and wanted trophies out of the Skullsplitter ruins, one from each. Gan'zulah's wanted Skullsplitters, dead, by the dozen, and somebody's broken armor on top. Being dead hadn't made either of them any less Bloodscalp. If anything, it had given them time to think about what they wanted.\n\nI paid both bills. Only then did they tell me what they knew. The chief's son hadn't died with the Bloodscalps at all. Zanzil the Outcast had him.\n\nI've bargained with goblins in my time. The goblins were easier."] = "Here's something nobody tells you about talking heads. They haggle.\n\nNezzliok's head spoke up from the cauldron first and wanted trophies out of the Skullsplitter ruins, one from each. Gan'zulah's wanted Skullsplitters, dead, by the dozen, and somebody's broken armor on top. Being dead hadn't made either of them any less Bloodscalp. If anything, it had given them time to think about what they wanted.\n\nI paid both bills. Only then did they tell me what they knew. The chief's son hadn't died with the Bloodscalps at all. Zanzil the Outcast had him.\n\nI've bargained with goblins in my time. The goblins were easier."
L["Nezzliok had one more thing to say, and it wasn't cheerful. Zanzil didn't just have the chief's son. Zanzil had him body and soul, and you can't cut a soul loose with a blade. Believe me, that was my first idea.\n\nKin'weelay, who'd stood beside that cauldron the whole time as if talking heads were nothing new, knew an old Stranglethorn legend about something called the Mind's Eye. The Eye was in the Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound, with an ogre sitting on it who didn't want to share. I made my case. Kin'weelay took the Eye and a few singing crystals I'd dug out of Crystalvein Mine and worked the lot into a Soul Gem.\n\nPretty thing. Not the sort you'd wear."] = "Nezzliok had one more thing to say, and it wasn't cheerful. Zanzil didn't just have the chief's son. Zanzil had him body and soul, and you can't cut a soul loose with a blade. Believe me, that was my first idea.\n\nKin'weelay, who'd stood beside that cauldron the whole time as if talking heads were nothing new, knew an old Stranglethorn legend about something called the Mind's Eye. The Eye was in the Mosh'Ogg Ogre Mound, with an ogre sitting on it who didn't want to share. I made my case. Kin'weelay took the Eye and a few singing crystals I'd dug out of Crystalvein Mine and worked the lot into a Soul Gem.\n\nPretty thing. Not the sort you'd wear."
L["I had the Soul Gem and a very short plan. Find Yenniku. Use the gem. Bring it back full.\n\nHe was down at the Ruins of Aboraz, by the Crystal Shore, with Zanzil's people all around him. The chief's son, standing in the jungle with somebody else's will where his own should be. Not easy to look at. I got close enough, I used the gem, and whatever Zanzil had wrapped around his soul let go. The soul went into the stone.\n\nI carried that gem back to Grom'gol the way you'd carry a full cup through a crowded tavern. Nimboya took it, and for once there was no next errand. Just a promise: the Darkspear would remember who brought their chief's son home.\n\nI've been paid in gold plenty of times. That was better."] = "I had the Soul Gem and a very short plan. Find Yenniku. Use the gem. Bring it back full.\n\nHe was down at the Ruins of Aboraz, by the Crystal Shore, with Zanzil's people all around him. The chief's son, standing in the jungle with somebody else's will where his own should be. Not easy to look at. I got close enough, I used the gem, and whatever Zanzil had wrapped around his soul let go. The soul went into the stone.\n\nI carried that gem back to Grom'gol the way you'd carry a full cup through a crowded tavern. Nimboya took it, and for once there was no next errand. Just a promise: the Darkspear would remember who brought their chief's son home.\n\nI've been paid in gold plenty of times. That was better."
L["There's a poster outside Westbrook Garrison. WANTED, it says, and then Hogger, in quotes, as if even the marshal wasn't sure that was his real name. A Riverpaw brute, out at Forest's Edge. Bring back his claw.\n\nOne gnoll, I thought. How bad can one gnoll be?\n\nHere's what nobody tells you about Hogger. He's big, he's quick, he hits like a runaway cart, and he doesn't live alone. The Riverpaw are thick in those woods, and every one of them takes his side. Say his name in Goldshire and watch the room go quiet.\n\nI brought back the claw, and it's a big one. You could rake a garden with it. Marshal Dughan looked at it for a good while, and then paid me.\n\nThe road to Westfall is a little safer now. A little."] = "There's a poster outside Westbrook Garrison. WANTED, it says, and then Hogger, in quotes, as if even the marshal wasn't sure that was his real name. A Riverpaw brute, out at Forest's Edge. Bring back his claw.\n\nOne gnoll, I thought. How bad can one gnoll be?\n\nHere's what nobody tells you about Hogger. He's big, he's quick, he hits like a runaway cart, and he doesn't live alone. The Riverpaw are thick in those woods, and every one of them takes his side. Say his name in Goldshire and watch the room go quiet.\n\nI brought back the claw, and it's a big one. You could rake a garden with it. Marshal Dughan looked at it for a good while, and then paid me.\n\nThe road to Westfall is a little safer now. A little."
L["The red shard was the bluntest path and perhaps the cruelest. Vaelastrasz, already broken by Nefarian, could only point toward the master of Blackwing Lair and set a clock running. The shard was taken from Nefarian under pressure, a raid victory folded into the long duty of the scepter."] = "The red shard was the bluntest path and perhaps the cruelest. Vaelastrasz, already broken by Nefarian, could only point toward the master of Blackwing Lair and set a clock running. The shard was taken from Nefarian under pressure, a raid victory folded into the long duty of the scepter."
L["The red tear outside Ahn'Qiraj was memory made visible. Anachronos let the past speak: the night elves, dragons, and qiraji locked in a war old enough to feel mythic. The scepter had broken across dragonflights and tragedies. To open the gates, the champion would have to follow those wounds into the Emerald Dream, Blackwing Lair, and Azshara's blue-dragon riddles."] = "The red tear outside Ahn'Qiraj was memory made visible. Anachronos let the past speak: the night elves, dragons, and qiraji locked in a war old enough to feel mythic. The scepter had broken across dragonflights and tragedies. To open the gates, the champion would have to follow those wounds into the Emerald Dream, Blackwing Lair, and Azshara's blue-dragon riddles."
L["Under the Thandol Span, where the bridge is broken, there's a stretch of riverbed nobody bothers to look at. I looked. There was what was left of a dwarf down there, and a waterlogged envelope.\n\nIt was a letter from Sully Balloo to Sara Balloo in Ironforge. Not orders. Not a map to anything. A man writing home to his wife, saying goodbye in case he didn't get another chance. He didn't.\n\nSo I carried it to Ironforge and found Sara. You don't want to be the one handing over a letter like that, but somebody has to. Sara read it, wrote a note of her own and asked me to take it to King Magni. She wanted her king to know what Sully had given. So up to the High Seat I went."] = "Under the Thandol Span, where the bridge is broken, there's a stretch of riverbed nobody bothers to look at. I looked. There was what was left of a dwarf down there, and a waterlogged envelope.\n\nIt was a letter from Sully Balloo to Sara Balloo in Ironforge. Not orders. Not a map to anything. A man writing home to his wife, saying goodbye in case he didn't get another chance. He didn't.\n\nSo I carried it to Ironforge and found Sara. You don't want to be the one handing over a letter like that, but somebody has to. Sara read it, wrote a note of her own and asked me to take it to King Magni. She wanted her king to know what Sully had given. So up to the High Seat I went."
L["Kings get a lot of letters. Magni read Sara's and decided Sully Balloo would have a memorial, and that the Grand Mason would build it.\n\nThe Grand Mason took one look at the commission and decided only the best stone would do: Alterac Granite, five pieces of it. Long way to go for rocks. Very good rocks, I'm told. I carried every one of them back to Ironforge and felt each of them in my shoulders the next morning.\n\nThen I reported back to the king, and Sully got his memorial. Kings hand out a lot of things, titles and medals and land. A stone for one soldier nobody else was out looking for isn't the grandest of them. I liked it best."] = "Kings get a lot of letters. Magni read Sara's and decided Sully Balloo would have a memorial, and that the Grand Mason would build it.\n\nThe Grand Mason took one look at the commission and decided only the best stone would do: Alterac Granite, five pieces of it. Long way to go for rocks. Very good rocks, I'm told. I carried every one of them back to Ironforge and felt each of them in my shoulders the next morning.\n\nThen I reported back to the king, and Sully got his memorial. Kings hand out a lot of things, titles and medals and land. A stone for one soldier nobody else was out looking for isn't the grandest of them. I liked it best."
L["Feathermoon was missing a courier. Raschal, coming from Thalanaar. Latronicus Moonspear had already written him down as missing in action, and once Feathermoon writes you down, you tend to stay written.\n\nGinro Hearthkindle hadn't given up. Ginro had spent a week combing Feralas and come back with nothing but gnoll teeth. So I took a turn along the Forgotten Coast and found the boat. A row boat with the Feathermoon crest on it, torn almost in two.\n\nNo Raschal. No body, either, which was something. Just a knife, lying in the mud by the wreck.\n\nI brought it back to Ginro. A knife can't tell you much, I thought. I was wrong about that, but that's the next part."] = "Feathermoon was missing a courier. Raschal, coming from Thalanaar. Latronicus Moonspear had already written him down as missing in action, and once Feathermoon writes you down, you tend to stay written.\n\nGinro Hearthkindle hadn't given up. Ginro had spent a week combing Feralas and come back with nothing but gnoll teeth. So I took a turn along the Forgotten Coast and found the boat. A row boat with the Feathermoon crest on it, torn almost in two.\n\nNo Raschal. No body, either, which was something. Just a knife, lying in the mud by the wreck.\n\nI brought it back to Ginro. A knife can't tell you much, I thought. I was wrong about that, but that's the next part."
L["Ginro's next idea was a new one on me. Take the knife to Quintis Jonespyre, who can hold a thing and read where it's been.\n\nSo Quintis held the knife and told me what it remembered. Raschal, hauling two big leather backpacks inland. Raschal, fretting about Woodpaw gnolls the whole way. And Raschal, not noticing the water elementals forming up behind him.\n\nThat last part was not encouraging.\n\nHandy trick, psychometry. I'd hate to have it at a dinner party, though. Imagine picking up somebody's fork. Still, it beat a week of turning over rocks, and now we knew where to look: inland, toward the gnolls, after a courier who'd had a worse day than anyone had guessed."] = "Ginro's next idea was a new one on me. Take the knife to Quintis Jonespyre, who can hold a thing and read where it's been.\n\nSo Quintis held the knife and told me what it remembered. Raschal, hauling two big leather backpacks inland. Raschal, fretting about Woodpaw gnolls the whole way. And Raschal, not noticing the water elementals forming up behind him.\n\nThat last part was not encouraging.\n\nHandy trick, psychometry. I'd hate to have it at a dinner party, though. Imagine picking up somebody's fork. Still, it beat a week of turning over rocks, and now we knew where to look: inland, toward the gnolls, after a courier who'd had a worse day than anyone had guessed."
L["The Woodpaw camps are about what you'd expect. Gnolls, bones, a smell you take home with you. I went through them looking for anything of Raschal's and found both his packs, pinned to a tree like a trophy, Feathermoon insignia and all.\n\nThe other thing in the packs was a note, and the note changed the whole picture. Raschal had seen strange insect creatures south of the gnoll camps, and gone to have a look.\n\nNot lost, then. Not dragged off by gnolls, either. Our courier had gone looking for trouble on purpose. I liked him already."] = "The Woodpaw camps are about what you'd expect. Gnolls, bones, a smell you take home with you. I went through them looking for anything of Raschal's and found both his packs, pinned to a tree like a trophy, Feathermoon insignia and all.\n\nThe other thing in the packs was a note, and the note changed the whole picture. Raschal had seen strange insect creatures south of the gnoll camps, and gone to have a look.\n\nNot lost, then. Not dragged off by gnolls, either. Our courier had gone looking for trouble on purpose. I liked him already."
L["Inside was a parcel he'd never delivered, meant for Falfindel Waywarder in Thalanaar. I took it there myself. A courier's job ought to get finished, even if the courier can't finish it."] = "Inside was a parcel he'd never delivered, meant for Falfindel Waywarder in Thalanaar. I took it there myself. A courier's job ought to get finished, even if the courier can't finish it."
L["South of the gnolls there's a hole in the ground called the Writhing Deep, and it's full of Zukk'ash. Insects, big ones, all legs and mandibles. Somewhere in there was Raschal, or what was left of him.\n\nWhat was left of him turned out to be all of him, sealed up in a pod. I got him out, and he had a report to make.\n\nBack at Feathermoon, Shandris Feathermoon read that report and sent me straight to Darnassus with it, where somebody finally put a name to what was under Feralas.\n\nSilithid.\n\nI went out looking for one lost courier and came back with a warning for all of Kalimdor. Not bad for a knife in the mud."] = "South of the gnolls there's a hole in the ground called the Writhing Deep, and it's full of Zukk'ash. Insects, big ones, all legs and mandibles. Somewhere in there was Raschal, or what was left of him.\n\nWhat was left of him turned out to be all of him, sealed up in a pod. I got him out, and he had a report to make.\n\nBack at Feathermoon, Shandris Feathermoon read that report and sent me straight to Darnassus with it, where somebody finally put a name to what was under Feralas.\n\nSilithid.\n\nI went out looking for one lost courier and came back with a warning for all of Kalimdor. Not bad for a knife in the mud."
L["Vaelastrasz gives little time for ceremony. The red shard must be claimed before the chance is lost."] = "Vaelastrasz gives little time for ceremony. The red shard must be claimed before the chance is lost."
L["When the shards were whole, the matter no longer belonged to one champion. The Might of Kalimdor waited on the war effort, the armies at the wall, and all who had prepared for the gates to open. The gong turned a repaired scepter into history: Ahn'Qiraj opened, and the war beyond the Scarab Wall began."] = "When the shards were whole, the matter no longer belonged to one champion. The Might of Kalimdor waited on the war effort, the armies at the wall, and all who had prepared for the gates to open. The gong turned a repaired scepter into history: Ahn'Qiraj opened, and the war beyond the Scarab Wall began."
L["When your hunter trainer turns from weapons to trust, learn how to tame and train a beast. Later, if an ancient leaf ever finds its way into your hands, answer the stranger calling from Felwood."] = "When your hunter trainer turns from weapons to trust, learn how to tame and train a beast. Later, if an ancient leaf ever finds its way into your hands, answer the stranger calling from Felwood."
L["When your old dungeon armor has seen enough battle, Deliana or Mokvar will offer work that sounds simple enough: bring the right pieces, prove you are ready, and keep following instructions.\n\nThe errands soon reach far beyond a single set of bracers. Expect old dungeons, stranger patrons, expensive favors, and a brazier whose purpose is not explained all at once."] = "When your old dungeon armor has seen enough battle, Deliana or Mokvar will offer work that sounds simple enough: bring the right pieces, prove you are ready, and keep following instructions.\n\nThe errands soon reach far beyond a single set of bracers. Expect old dungeons, stranger patrons, expensive favors, and a brazier whose purpose is not explained all at once."
L["If you ever bring the Eye of Divinity out of Molten Core, carry it to the Eastern Plaguelands. Eris Havenfire waits there for a priest who has it, with a trial that asks for more than bright prayers."] = "If you ever bring the Eye of Divinity out of Molten Core, carry it to the Eastern Plaguelands. Eris Havenfire waits there for a priest who has it, with a trial that asks for more than bright prayers."
L["With the three shards restored, take up the Scepter of the Shifting Sands and return to the Scarab Gong."] = "With the three shards restored, take up the Scepter of the Shifting Sands and return to the Scarab Gong."
L["Your mage trainer sends you to Tabetha in Dustwallow Marsh, where practical magic begins with travel, errands, and materials that will not gather themselves.\n\nFollow the lessons that turn study into tools: a wand, a strange polymorph, and food enough for hungry companions."] = "Your mage trainer sends you to Tabetha in Dustwallow Marsh, where practical magic begins with travel, errands, and materials that will not gather themselves.\n\nFollow the lessons that turn study into tools: a wand, a strange polymorph, and food enough for hungry companions."
