L["The Valley of Bones"] = "The Valley of Bones"
L["Two centaur clans, the Magram and the Gelkis, have feuded for generations. Captain Pentigast at Nijel's Point wants the Alliance to have a friend in one of them. Which one is up to you. Either way, the road leads to the Valley of Bones."] = "Two centaur clans, the Magram and the Gelkis, have feuded for generations. Captain Pentigast at Nijel's Point wants the Alliance to have a friend in one of them. Which one is up to you. Either way, the road leads to the Valley of Bones."
L["Two centaur clans, the Magram and the Gelkis, have feuded for generations. Gurda Wildmane at Ghost Walker Post wants the Horde to have a friend in one of them. Which one is up to you. Either way, the road leads to the Valley of Bones."] = "Two centaur clans, the Magram and the Gelkis, have feuded for generations. Gurda Wildmane at Ghost Walker Post wants the Horde to have a friend in one of them. Which one is up to you. Either way, the road leads to the Valley of Bones."
L["Neither clan is finished with the Valley of Bones. Ride with the Magram, and Orgrul has work for you at the valley's northern entrance. Ride with the Gelkis, and Nerlokh waits at the southern one. The feud is far from settled."] = "Neither clan is finished with the Valley of Bones. Ride with the Magram, and Orgrul has work for you at the valley's northern entrance. Ride with the Gelkis, and Nerlokh waits at the southern one. The feud is far from settled."
L["A cracked crystal pylon stands among the elven ruins of Shen'dralas, past the centaur battle lines, and it's still humming. Ruins don't usually hum. Look for others like it, and for whoever still looks after them."] = "A cracked crystal pylon stands among the elven ruins of Shen'dralas, past the centaur battle lines, and it's still humming. Ruins don't usually hum. Look for others like it, and for whoever still looks after them."
L["Mithera, at the temple in Shen'dralas, once kept a garden nearby and left treants to guard the flowers. Now Mithera wonders what became of them. Go and look in on the Forlorn Gardens. The name isn't encouraging."] = "Mithera, at the temple in Shen'dralas, once kept a garden nearby and left treants to guard the flowers. Now Mithera wonders what became of them. Go and look in on the Forlorn Gardens. The name isn't encouraging."
L["The Magram and the Gelkis have been feuding longer than anyone can remember, and each wanted me on their side. I'm not saying which one got me. Either way I ended up in the Valley of Bones, where skeletons were clawing up out of the ground. Not centaur skeletons, either.\n\nThe Magram looked at that and saw an army. Orgrul wanted bones to build it with, gently cursed, and wanted exactly four hundred and twenty-six of them. Somebody there can count. The Gelkis looked at the necromancers' robes, saw their own exiles, and wanted the curse broken.\n\nEither way, I ended up holding somebody else's staff and pointing it at the dead. What it did depended on who'd made it."] = "The Magram and the Gelkis have been feuding longer than anyone can remember, and each wanted me on their side. I'm not saying which one got me. Either way I ended up in the Valley of Bones, where skeletons were clawing up out of the ground. Not centaur skeletons, either.\n\nThe Magram looked at that and saw an army. Orgrul wanted bones to build it with, gently cursed, and wanted exactly four hundred and twenty-six of them. Somebody there can count. The Gelkis looked at the necromancers' robes, saw their own exiles, and wanted the curse broken.\n\nEither way, I ended up holding somebody else's staff and pointing it at the dead. What it did depended on who'd made it."
L["Ride with the Gelkis, and the khan of the Magram is the problem. With so many Magram tied up in the valley their village was nearly empty, and Khan Jehn died in his own home at the hands of outsiders working for the Gelkis.\n\nRide with the Magram, and the khan's death is the news waiting for you when you come back from the front. Orgrul didn't rage about it. Orgrul just handed the necromancy staff back to the Magram, and in a tent by the village lake, Khan Jehn got up again.\n\nWhich ride was mine, I'm keeping to myself. Either way, the khan had a worse week than I did."] = "Ride with the Gelkis, and the khan of the Magram is the problem. With so many Magram tied up in the valley their village was nearly empty, and Khan Jehn died in his own home at the hands of outsiders working for the Gelkis.\n\nRide with the Magram, and the khan's death is the news waiting for you when you come back from the front. Orgrul didn't rage about it. Orgrul just handed the necromancy staff back to the Magram, and in a tent by the village lake, Khan Jehn got up again.\n\nWhich ride was mine, I'm keeping to myself. Either way, the khan had a worse week than I did."
L["In the woods past the centaur lines I found a crystal pylon, cracked and humming, and Mordent Evenshade, who wanted it fixed. Its missing pieces had been carried off as trophies to both centaur villages. I took them back. From both.\n\nThen I wandered the woods with a borrowed relic, scooping up loose mana and pouring it into the pylons, and right about the time they hummed properly, the sky changed. Mordent only got round to explaining afterward.\n\nThe temple's historian was still rattled that I'd carried that relic through haunted woods, but not too rattled to send me after a dying protector's crystal heart and point me at an unstable rift. Was there still enough mana here to make new guardians?\n\nI threw it. We found out."] = "In the woods past the centaur lines I found a crystal pylon, cracked and humming, and Mordent Evenshade, who wanted it fixed. Its missing pieces had been carried off as trophies to both centaur villages. I took them back. From both.\n\nThen I wandered the woods with a borrowed relic, scooping up loose mana and pouring it into the pylons, and right about the time they hummed properly, the sky changed. Mordent only got round to explaining afterward.\n\nThe temple's historian was still rattled that I'd carried that relic through haunted woods, but not too rattled to send me after a dying protector's crystal heart and point me at an unstable rift. Was there still enough mana here to make new guardians?\n\nI threw it. We found out."
L["Mithera's garden had gone wild and mean, and the necklace that woke its treants was missing from its shrine. Her old friend Woodwyrd was still there, dead and still feeling the flames. I put Woodwyrd out with a vial of water from the depths of Maraudon, which is not a sentence I ever expected to say.\n\nThe thieves were quilboar. Kog'thug the Brave owned up to it, which was brave, I suppose, but the necklace was with Kog'thug's mate now, and the two of them weren't speaking. Peace took a bouquet from the very garden they'd robbed and the blood of Agamaggan from Razorfen Downs. Not Razorfen Kraul. Kog'thug made very sure I knew the difference.\n\nThe necklace went home with its magic spent. Still from her daughter, though."] = "Mithera's garden had gone wild and mean, and the necklace that woke its treants was missing from its shrine. Her old friend Woodwyrd was still there, dead and still feeling the flames. I put Woodwyrd out with a vial of water from the depths of Maraudon, which is not a sentence I ever expected to say.\n\nThe thieves were quilboar. Kog'thug the Brave owned up to it, which was brave, I suppose, but the necklace was with Kog'thug's mate now, and the two of them weren't speaking. Peace took a bouquet from the very garden they'd robbed and the blood of Agamaggan from Razorfen Downs. Not Razorfen Kraul. Kog'thug made very sure I knew the difference.\n\nThe necklace went home with its magic spent. Still from her daughter, though."
