L["Someone left more than ash in a burned-out wreck: a note, written for whoever thought to look, and a plank nailed up where it would be found.\n\nFollow the trail across both continents, through a hidden camp in the mountains and over a great dam, to whatever its writer went to such lengths to hide."] = "Someone left more than ash in a burned-out wreck: a note, written for whoever thought to look, and a plank nailed up where it would be found.\n\nFollow the trail across both continents, through a hidden camp in the mountains and over a great dam, to whatever its writer went to such lengths to hide."
L["Stonetalon Mountains / Arathi Highlands"] = "Stonetalon Mountains / Arathi Highlands"
L["... and that note you found"] = "... and that note you found"
L["Burned-Out Remains, Westfall"] = "Burned-Out Remains, Westfall"
L["A Note in the Ashes"] = "A Note in the Ashes"
L["In Westfall there's a burned-out wreck nobody looks at twice, and in the ash there's a note. Charred, mostly gone, but someone plainly meant it to be found. Read what's left and see where it points."] = "In Westfall there's a burned-out wreck nobody looks at twice, and in the ash there's a note. Charred, mostly gone, but someone plainly meant it to be found. Read what's left and see where it points."
L["Out in the Barrens there's a burned-out wreck nobody looks at twice, and in the ash there's a note. Charred, mostly gone, but someone plainly meant it to be found. Read what's left and see where it points."] = "Out in the Barrens there's a burned-out wreck nobody looks at twice, and in the ash there's a note. Charred, mostly gone, but someone plainly meant it to be found. Read what's left and see where it points."
L["Beside the burned-out remains in the Barrens, someone has nailed up a plank with a note on it. More directions, and this time they point up into the Stonetalon Mountains. Whoever left this trail went to a lot of trouble."] = "Beside the burned-out remains in the Barrens, someone has nailed up a plank with a note on it. More directions, and this time they point up into the Stonetalon Mountains. Whoever left this trail went to a lot of trouble."
L["Beside the burned-out remains in Westfall, someone has nailed up a plank with a note on it. More directions, and this time they point up into the Stonetalon Mountains. Whoever left this trail went to a lot of trouble."] = "Beside the burned-out remains in Westfall, someone has nailed up a plank with a note on it. More directions, and this time they point up into the Stonetalon Mountains. Whoever left this trail went to a lot of trouble."
L["On the ledge below the abandoned camp in the Stonetalon Mountains sits a mound of fresh dirt. Somebody was digging here not long ago. You've followed this trail across an ocean. A pile of dirt isn't going to stop you."] = "On the ledge below the abandoned camp in the Stonetalon Mountains sits a mound of fresh dirt. Somebody was digging here not long ago. You've followed this trail across an ocean. A pile of dirt isn't going to stop you."
L["Some of the best things I've ever found, I found poking through ash. There's a burned-out wreck in Westfall, nothing left in it worth stealing, and in the middle of it a note. Charred half to nothing. But somebody had written it for whoever bothered to look, and I always bother to look.\n\nWhat was left of it pointed across the sea. To the Barrens, of all places. So I went, a very long way for a scrap of paper, and found a second burned-out ruin waiting for me. The twin of the first, an ocean apart, with the same sign left on it. And nailed up beside it, a plank with more writing.\n\nWhoever this was had gone to a lot of trouble. That's usually a good sign. Usually."] = "Some of the best things I've ever found, I found poking through ash. There's a burned-out wreck in Westfall, nothing left in it worth stealing, and in the middle of it a note. Charred half to nothing. But somebody had written it for whoever bothered to look, and I always bother to look.\n\nWhat was left of it pointed across the sea. To the Barrens, of all places. So I went, a very long way for a scrap of paper, and found a second burned-out ruin waiting for me. The twin of the first, an ocean apart, with the same sign left on it. And nailed up beside it, a plank with more writing.\n\nWhoever this was had gone to a lot of trouble. That's usually a good sign. Usually."
L["There's a burned-out wreck out in the Barrens, the kind you ride past a hundred times. I didn't ride past it. In the ash was a note, charred so badly I could barely make it out, but somebody had plainly meant it to be found.\n\nWhat was left of it sent me across the sea to Westfall. Long trip for a scrap of paper. And there it was: a second burned-out ruin, twin of the first, a whole ocean away, with the same sign left on it. Nailed up beside it was a plank with the next set of instructions.\n\nI stood there a while thinking somebody out there had far too much time on their hands. Then I read the plank."] = "There's a burned-out wreck out in the Barrens, the kind you ride past a hundred times. I didn't ride past it. In the ash was a note, charred so badly I could barely make it out, but somebody had plainly meant it to be found.\n\nWhat was left of it sent me across the sea to Westfall. Long trip for a scrap of paper. And there it was: a second burned-out ruin, twin of the first, a whole ocean away, with the same sign left on it. Nailed up beside it was a plank with the next set of instructions.\n\nI stood there a while thinking somebody out there had far too much time on their hands. Then I read the plank."
L["The plank sent me up into the Stonetalon Mountains, to a camp tucked away where nobody would ever stumble on it. Whoever lived there had left in a hurry. Crates still full of tools. Two good sturdy bags. And the sort of pocket litter you only leave behind when you're in a rush to be somewhere else.\n\nThe litter pointed me to the edge of the cliff, and then, as far as I could tell, over it. So I jumped.\n\nThey call that a leap of faith. I call it a very long moment. I came down on a ledge below in one piece, right beside a mound of fresh dirt. Somebody had been digging there, and not long ago.\n\nI'd come this far. I wasn't going to be stopped by a pile of dirt."] = "The plank sent me up into the Stonetalon Mountains, to a camp tucked away where nobody would ever stumble on it. Whoever lived there had left in a hurry. Crates still full of tools. Two good sturdy bags. And the sort of pocket litter you only leave behind when you're in a rush to be somewhere else.\n\nThe litter pointed me to the edge of the cliff, and then, as far as I could tell, over it. So I jumped.\n\nThey call that a leap of faith. I call it a very long moment. I came down on a ledge below in one piece, right beside a mound of fresh dirt. Somebody had been digging there, and not long ago.\n\nI'd come this far. I wasn't going to be stopped by a pile of dirt."
L["The dirt gave up a messenger bag with a parchment in it, and the parchment sent me clear across the sea to Stonewrought Dam, where a carved eagle on the stone heads pointed its fist at Thoradin's Wall. Lovely view, if you like heights.\n\nUp on the old wall was one more bag and one last note. Whoever had left it was gone. I'd just missed them, by the look of it. And under the note was what the whole trail had been guarding, across two continents, a cliff and a dam.\n\nA sleeping bag. A cozy one.\n\nI'd love to tell you I was disappointed. I wasn't. It's a very good sleeping bag."] = "The dirt gave up a messenger bag with a parchment in it, and the parchment sent me clear across the sea to Stonewrought Dam, where a carved eagle on the stone heads pointed its fist at Thoradin's Wall. Lovely view, if you like heights.\n\nUp on the old wall was one more bag and one last note. Whoever had left it was gone. I'd just missed them, by the look of it. And under the note was what the whole trail had been guarding, across two continents, a cliff and a dam.\n\nA sleeping bag. A cozy one.\n\nI'd love to tell you I was disappointed. I wasn't. It's a very good sleeping bag."
