L["Zaruk"] = "Zaruk"
L["In the northern mountains of the Arathi Highlands, a cluster of crystals called the Shards of Myzrael pulses with a voice. It belongs to a princess of the earth, trapped beneath the Highlands by giants, and she would very much like out."] = "In the northern mountains of the Arathi Highlands, a cluster of crystals called the Shards of Myzrael pulses with a voice. It belongs to a princess of the earth, trapped beneath the Highlands by giants, and she would very much like out."
L["So there's a crystal up in the mountains of northern Arathi, and it talks. Put a hand on it and a voice turns up in your head: Myzrael, a princess of the earth, chained deep under the Highlands by the giants. She asked very nicely.\n\nThe kobolds of Drywhisker Gorge were allies of the giants, and they had what she needed: a dozen Motes of Myzrael, to wake a second cluster of shards in their cave. They gave them up eventually.\n\nThen the Stones of Binding. Three stone circles across the Highlands, a key in each, and elementals on guard at every one. I brought all three keys to the Stone of Inner Binding, and the princess called me her rescuer.\n\nNobody had ever called me that before. I could get used to it."] = "So there's a crystal up in the mountains of northern Arathi, and it talks. Put a hand on it and a voice turns up in your head: Myzrael, a princess of the earth, chained deep under the Highlands by the giants. She asked very nicely.\n\nThe kobolds of Drywhisker Gorge were allies of the giants, and they had what she needed: a dozen Motes of Myzrael, to wake a second cluster of shards in their cave. They gave them up eventually.\n\nThen the Stones of Binding. Three stone circles across the Highlands, a key in each, and elementals on guard at every one. I brought all three keys to the Stone of Inner Binding, and the princess called me her rescuer.\n\nNobody had ever called me that before. I could get used to it."
L["I took a pendant off a fallen foe once, and it would not stop pulsing. Hold it and a vision comes: the northern mountains of Arathi, a brilliant crystal jutting out of the earth, and a sense of someone pleading for help. I followed the vision. The crystal was there, and so was the pleading."] = "I took a pendant off a fallen foe once, and it would not stop pulsing. Hold it and a vision comes: the northern mountains of Arathi, a brilliant crystal jutting out of the earth, and a sense of someone pleading for help. I followed the vision. The crystal was there, and so was the pleading."
L["Crystal in the Mountains"] = "Crystal in the Mountains"
L["Pendant of Myzrael"] = "Pendant of Myzrael"
L["a rare drop from foes around level 30 to 40"] = "a rare drop from foes around level 30 to 40"
L["Stones of Binding"] = "Stones of Binding"
L["Breaking the Keystone"] = "Breaking the Keystone"
L["Myzrael has one more favor to ask, through the Stone of Inner Binding. A last lock, the Keystone, still holds her, and its key is carried by Fozruk, a stone watcher who walks the Arathi Highlands. You may want company."] = "Myzrael has one more favor to ask, through the Stone of Inner Binding. A last lock, the Keystone, still holds her, and its key is carried by Fozruk, a stone watcher who walks the Arathi Highlands. You may want company."
L["The last lock was the Keystone, and its key, the Rod of Order, belonged to Fozruk. Fozruk is a stone watcher, which is a polite word for a giant, and spends the day walking the Highlands. Finding the giant took a while. Taking the rod took a while longer.\n\nI set the rod in the Keystone and felt the ground shudder. Then the princess, very loud: free, she was free! Not ready to face her old jailers, mind you. She needed time to gather her strength, and when she was ready, someone would have to summon her to the surface. You can guess who.\n\nShe sent me to a friend of hers, Gerrig Bonegrip, in a shop in the Forlorn Cavern of Ironforge. Gerrig had been expecting me, and welcomed me as one who serves a higher cause. Nobody had ever said that about me before either."] = "The last lock was the Keystone, and its key, the Rod of Order, belonged to Fozruk. Fozruk is a stone watcher, which is a polite word for a giant, and spends the day walking the Highlands. Finding the giant took a while. Taking the rod took a while longer.\n\nI set the rod in the Keystone and felt the ground shudder. Then the princess, very loud: free, she was free! Not ready to face her old jailers, mind you. She needed time to gather her strength, and when she was ready, someone would have to summon her to the surface. You can guess who.\n\nShe sent me to a friend of hers, Gerrig Bonegrip, in a shop in the Forlorn Cavern of Ironforge. Gerrig had been expecting me, and welcomed me as one who serves a higher cause. Nobody had ever said that about me before either."
L["The last lock was the Keystone, and its key, the Rod of Order, belonged to Fozruk. Fozruk is a stone watcher, which is a polite word for a giant, and spends the day walking the Highlands. Finding the giant took a while. Taking the rod took a while longer.\n\nI set the rod in the Keystone and felt the ground shudder. Then the princess, very loud: free, she was free! Not ready to face her old jailers, mind you. She needed time to gather her strength, and when she was ready, someone would have to summon her to the surface. You can guess who.\n\nShe sent me to a friend of hers, Zaruk, in Hammerfall. Zaruk had been expecting me, and said that if we were to bring the Lady back to the surface, we had much to discuss. Nobody had ever needed me for anything that grand before."] = "The last lock was the Keystone, and its key, the Rod of Order, belonged to Fozruk. Fozruk is a stone watcher, which is a polite word for a giant, and spends the day walking the Highlands. Finding the giant took a while. Taking the rod took a while longer.\n\nI set the rod in the Keystone and felt the ground shudder. Then the princess, very loud: free, she was free! Not ready to face her old jailers, mind you. She needed time to gather her strength, and when she was ready, someone would have to summon her to the surface. You can guess who.\n\nShe sent me to a friend of hers, Zaruk, in Hammerfall. Zaruk had been expecting me, and said that if we were to bring the Lady back to the surface, we had much to discuss. Nobody had ever needed me for anything that grand before."
L["Myzrael's Allies"] = "Myzrael's Allies"
L["Gerrig Bonegrip, in the Forlorn Cavern of Ironforge, knows how Myzrael can be summoned. It takes a scroll, and the scroll went missing along with someone called Theldurin the Lost, last heard of in the Badlands. The name is not encouraging."] = "Gerrig Bonegrip, in the Forlorn Cavern of Ironforge, knows how Myzrael can be summoned. It takes a scroll, and the scroll went missing along with someone called Theldurin the Lost, last heard of in the Badlands. The name is not encouraging."
L["Zaruk, in Hammerfall, knows how Myzrael can be summoned. It takes a scroll, and the scroll went missing along with someone called Theldurin the Lost, last heard of in the Badlands. The name is not encouraging."] = "Zaruk, in Hammerfall, knows how Myzrael can be summoned. It takes a scroll, and the scroll went missing along with someone called Theldurin the Lost, last heard of in the Badlands. The name is not encouraging."
L["Gerrig had more to say after that. The higher cause had a name, the Twilight's Hammer, and the Lady was their Lady. To bring her back they needed the Scroll of Myzrael, and one of their own, Theldurin, had lost his mind, scorned their teachings and fled to the Badlands with it.\n\nTheldurin was mad, all right. He said hello seven times, apologized, and took the apology back. But when I mentioned the scroll, he made perfect sense. Summon her? You don't want that, he said. If she comes up, we're all in trouble. Maybe she's already free, gathering her strength.\n\nI had just finished setting her free.\n\nThe scroll had been torn up by enraged rock elementals, who were still fighting over the scraps. I collected the scraps. Theldurin called them our only chance against the evil of Myzrael, and went looking for his scrap-ratchet."] = "Gerrig had more to say after that. The higher cause had a name, the Twilight's Hammer, and the Lady was their Lady. To bring her back they needed the Scroll of Myzrael, and one of their own, Theldurin, had lost his mind, scorned their teachings and fled to the Badlands with it.\n\nTheldurin was mad, all right. He said hello seven times, apologized, and took the apology back. But when I mentioned the scroll, he made perfect sense. Summon her? You don't want that, he said. If she comes up, we're all in trouble. Maybe she's already free, gathering her strength.\n\nI had just finished setting her free.\n\nThe scroll had been torn up by enraged rock elementals, who were still fighting over the scraps. I collected the scraps. Theldurin called them our only chance against the evil of Myzrael, and went looking for his scrap-ratchet."
L["Zaruk had more to say after that. The higher cause had a name, the Twilight's Hammer, and the Lady was their Lady. To bring her back they needed the Scroll of Myzrael, and one of their own, Theldurin, had lost his mind, scorned their teachings and fled to the Badlands with it.\n\nTheldurin was mad, all right. He said hello seven times, apologized, and took the apology back. But when I mentioned the scroll, he made perfect sense. Summon her? You don't want that, he said. If she comes up, we're all in trouble. Maybe she's already free, gathering her strength.\n\nI had just finished setting her free.\n\nThe scroll had been torn up by enraged rock elementals, who were still fighting over the scraps. I collected the scraps. Theldurin called them our only chance against the evil of Myzrael, and went looking for his scrap-ratchet."] = "Zaruk had more to say after that. The higher cause had a name, the Twilight's Hammer, and the Lady was their Lady. To bring her back they needed the Scroll of Myzrael, and one of their own, Theldurin, had lost his mind, scorned their teachings and fled to the Badlands with it.\n\nTheldurin was mad, all right. He said hello seven times, apologized, and took the apology back. But when I mentioned the scroll, he made perfect sense. Summon her? You don't want that, he said. If she comes up, we're all in trouble. Maybe she's already free, gathering her strength.\n\nI had just finished setting her free.\n\nThe scroll had been torn up by enraged rock elementals, who were still fighting over the scraps. I collected the scraps. Theldurin called them our only chance against the evil of Myzrael, and went looking for his scrap-ratchet."
L["The Lost Fragments"] = "The Lost Fragments"
L["Summoning the Princess"] = "Summoning the Princess"
L["Theldurin the Lost, hiding in the Badlands, has pieced the Scroll of Myzrael back together with his scrap-ratchet. Read at the Shards of Myzrael, where you first heard her voice, it will call the princess up. Theldurin has some firm ideas about what happens next."] = "Theldurin the Lost, hiding in the Badlands, has pieced the Scroll of Myzrael back together with his scrap-ratchet. Read at the Shards of Myzrael, where you first heard her voice, it will call the princess up. Theldurin has some firm ideas about what happens next."
L["I read the scroll at the Shards of Myzrael, right where the princess first asked me for help, and up she came. Free, rested, and in no mood to thank anyone. She'd promised me once that her gratitude would shake the very foundations of the earth. She meant it literally.\n\nWhen she finally went down, I took her Eldritch Shackles and bound them to the Shards. From somewhere far below came a wail, and then a roar.\n\nThe Shards don't feel warm anymore. They hum with something that wishes the surface ill. Fair enough. If somebody let me out and then put me back, I'd hold a grudge too.\n\nRescuing a princess, it turns out, is only half the job. The other half is putting her back."] = "I read the scroll at the Shards of Myzrael, right where the princess first asked me for help, and up she came. Free, rested, and in no mood to thank anyone. She'd promised me once that her gratitude would shake the very foundations of the earth. She meant it literally.\n\nWhen she finally went down, I took her Eldritch Shackles and bound them to the Shards. From somewhere far below came a wail, and then a roar.\n\nThe Shards don't feel warm anymore. They hum with something that wishes the surface ill. Fair enough. If somebody let me out and then put me back, I'd hold a grudge too.\n\nRescuing a princess, it turns out, is only half the job. The other half is putting her back."
L["Thunderfury"] = "Thunderfury"
L["Deep in Molten Core, Garr and Baron Geddon each keep half of a relic called the Bindings of the Windseeker. They give them up rarely, and only after a great many evenings of asking.\n\nCarry a half to Silithus and see where it leads. Legends are made at the end of roads like this one, provided you bring patience, a great deal of arcanite, and thirty-nine friends."] = "Deep in Molten Core, Garr and Baron Geddon each keep half of a relic called the Bindings of the Windseeker. They give them up rarely, and only after a great many evenings of asking.\n\nCarry a half to Silithus and see where it leads. Legends are made at the end of roads like this one, provided you bring patience, a great deal of arcanite, and thirty-nine friends."
L["Molten Core / Silithus"] = "Molten Core / Silithus"
L["Examine the Vessel"] = "Examine the Vessel"
L["the Ravaged Twilight Camp, Silithus"] = "the Ravaged Twilight Camp, Silithus"
L["Prince Thunderaan"] = "Prince Thunderaan"
L["The Bindings of the Windseeker"] = "The Bindings of the Windseeker"
L["Deep in Molten Core, Garr and Baron Geddon each carry half of the Bindings of the Windseeker, and neither gives it up often. Win either half, then take it to Highlord Demitrian at the Ravaged Twilight Camp in northwestern Silithus."] = "Deep in Molten Core, Garr and Baron Geddon each carry half of the Bindings of the Windseeker, and neither gives it up often. Win either half, then take it to Highlord Demitrian at the Ravaged Twilight Camp in northwestern Silithus."
L["Garr and Baron Geddon live down in Molten Core, and each of them carries half of the Bindings of the Windseeker. They carry them the way a miser carries his coin: rarely seen, never offered. We killed the pair of them week after week. I won't say how many weeks. Some of the people who were there still won't talk about it.\n\nThen one night a half dropped, and it was mine.\n\nIt led me to Silithus, to a ruined Twilight camp and a man called Highlord Demitrian, who took one look at it and fell to his knees. My Master's eternal prison, he said. He must be released. He gave me a vessel to examine, I examined it, and he asked the Master to bless my efforts.\n\nI noticed the word Master. I decided to worry about it later."] = "Garr and Baron Geddon live down in Molten Core, and each of them carries half of the Bindings of the Windseeker. They carry them the way a miser carries his coin: rarely seen, never offered. We killed the pair of them week after week. I won't say how many weeks. Some of the people who were there still won't talk about it.\n\nThen one night a half dropped, and it was mine.\n\nIt led me to Silithus, to a ruined Twilight camp and a man called Highlord Demitrian, who took one look at it and fell to his knees. My Master's eternal prison, he said. He must be released. He gave me a vessel to examine, I examined it, and he asked the Master to bless my efforts.\n\nI noticed the word Master. I decided to worry about it later."
L["Thunderaan the Windseeker"] = "Thunderaan the Windseeker"
L["Highlord Demitrian, in his ruined camp in Silithus, knows how to free the Windseeker from his prison. It takes both halves of the Bindings, ten bars of elementium, and the Essence of the Firelord, which only Ragnaros can give up."] = "Highlord Demitrian, in his ruined camp in Silithus, knows how to free the Windseeker from his prison. It takes both halves of the Bindings, ten bars of elementium, and the Essence of the Firelord, which only Ragnaros can give up."
L["Demitrian's list was short. Both halves of the Bindings. Ten bars of elementium to fortify his vessel. And the Essence of the Firelord, which you only get by destroying Ragnaros' body at the bottom of Molten Core.\n\nThe second half of the Bindings took its own sweet time, same as the first. Ragnaros took some persuading. The elementium took everything else. Every bar wants ten bars of arcanite and a fiery core besides, so a hundred bars of arcanite went into a sword I hadn't even seen yet. I stopped counting the gold. The banker didn't.\n\nWhen I finally laid it all out, Demitrian didn't thank me. He called on his Master to rise and break his bonds, and told me to behold him who is older than existence.\n\nSo I beheld. He was free, and he was not in a grateful mood."] = "Demitrian's list was short. Both halves of the Bindings. Ten bars of elementium to fortify his vessel. And the Essence of the Firelord, which you only get by destroying Ragnaros' body at the bottom of Molten Core.\n\nThe second half of the Bindings took its own sweet time, same as the first. Ragnaros took some persuading. The elementium took everything else. Every bar wants ten bars of arcanite and a fiery core besides, so a hundred bars of arcanite went into a sword I hadn't even seen yet. I stopped counting the gold. The banker didn't.\n\nWhen I finally laid it all out, Demitrian didn't thank me. He called on his Master to rise and break his bonds, and told me to behold him who is older than existence.\n\nSo I beheld. He was free, and he was not in a grateful mood."
L["Rise, Thunderfury!"] = "Rise, Thunderfury!"
L["The Dormant Wind Kissed Blade is in your hands, and it sleeps. Highlord Demitrian, at the Ravaged Twilight Camp in Silithus, has the craft to wake it. Go and ask him. Firmly."] = "The Dormant Wind Kissed Blade is in your hands, and it sleeps. Highlord Demitrian, at the Ravaged Twilight Camp in Silithus, has the craft to wake it. Go and ask him. Firmly."
L["For someone who'd spent ages in a prison that had been riding around in my bags, Prince Thunderaan came out swinging. It took forty of us to put him down. When he went, he left a sword behind, dormant, with the wind still moving in it.\n\nDemitrian was a different man by then. He cowered. He begged me to spare him and hand over the blade, and he'd wake it with the very materials I'd brought for his Master's return. Then I could leave him to reshape his pathetic life. His words. I didn't argue with any of them.\n\nSo he made it. Thunderfury, Blessed Blade of the Windseeker. It takes longer to say than to swing, and far longer to get than either.\n\nNow and then someone in a tavern asks, did someone say Thunderfury? Someone did. It was me."] = "For someone who'd spent ages in a prison that had been riding around in my bags, Prince Thunderaan came out swinging. It took forty of us to put him down. When he went, he left a sword behind, dormant, with the wind still moving in it.\n\nDemitrian was a different man by then. He cowered. He begged me to spare him and hand over the blade, and he'd wake it with the very materials I'd brought for his Master's return. Then I could leave him to reshape his pathetic life. His words. I didn't argue with any of them.\n\nSo he made it. Thunderfury, Blessed Blade of the Windseeker. It takes longer to say than to swing, and far longer to get than either.\n\nNow and then someone in a tavern asks, did someone say Thunderfury? Someone did. It was me."
L["Molten Core"] = "Molten Core"
L["The Green Hills"] = "The Green Hills"
-- WoW Forever: The Drowned City
L["The Drowned City"] = "The Drowned City"
L["At the Rebel Camp in northern Stranglethorn, a few deserters are holding out against their old commander, Colonel Kurzen, and Brother Nimetz wants to know what turned a good officer so cruel. To the south, at Grom'gol Base Camp, Kin'weelay of the Darkspear wants a piece of an old legend from the Vile Reef, a troll city the sea swallowed long ago.\n\nBoth trails lead out to the water off Stranglethorn's coast, where something old has come back up. Every treasure hunter in Booty Bay has heard about it."] = "At the Rebel Camp in northern Stranglethorn, a few deserters are holding out against their old commander, Colonel Kurzen, and Brother Nimetz wants to know what turned a good officer so cruel. To the south, at Grom'gol Base Camp, Kin'weelay of the Darkspear wants a piece of an old legend from the Vile Reef, a troll city the sea swallowed long ago.\n\nBoth trails lead out to the water off Stranglethorn's coast, where something old has come back up. Every treasure hunter in Booty Bay has heard about it."
L["At the Rebel Camp in northern Stranglethorn, a few deserters are holding out against their old commander, Colonel Kurzen, outnumbered ten to one. Lieutenant Doren needs every blade he can get, and Brother Nimetz wants to know what turned a good officer so cruel.\n\nThe answer leads a long way from the jungle and then straight back to it, out to the water off Stranglethorn's coast, where something old has come back up. Every treasure hunter in Booty Bay has heard about it."] = "At the Rebel Camp in northern Stranglethorn, a few deserters are holding out against their old commander, Colonel Kurzen, outnumbered ten to one. Lieutenant Doren needs every blade he can get, and Brother Nimetz wants to know what turned a good officer so cruel.\n\nThe answer leads a long way from the jungle and then straight back to it, out to the water off Stranglethorn's coast, where something old has come back up. Every treasure hunter in Booty Bay has heard about it."
L["At Grom'gol Base Camp in Stranglethorn, Kin'weelay of the Darkspear wants a piece of an old legend from the Vile Reef, a troll city the sea swallowed long ago. Commander Aggro'gosh has trouble enough on land without more of it coming out of the water.\n\nThe legend leads out to the reef and past it, to something old that has come back up off Stranglethorn's coast. Every treasure hunter in Booty Bay has heard about it."] = "At Grom'gol Base Camp in Stranglethorn, Kin'weelay of the Darkspear wants a piece of an old legend from the Vile Reef, a troll city the sea swallowed long ago. Commander Aggro'gosh has trouble enough on land without more of it coming out of the water.\n\nThe legend leads out to the reef and past it, to something old that has come back up off Stranglethorn's coast. Every treasure hunter in Booty Bay has heard about it."
